hadiths[486]{hadithnumber,text}:
"22801","39 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Hasan ibn Salih, from Simak ibn Harb, from Qabus ibn al-Mukhariq who said: Umm al-Fadl said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw as if part of your limbs were in my house. He said: Good; Fatimah will give birth and you will nurse him. She gave birth to Hasan or Husayn and she nursed him. I brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day and placed him in his lap, and he urinated, so I struck his shoulder. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have hurt my son, may Allah have mercy on you."
"22802","40 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Minjab ibn al-Harith narrated to us. And Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, both saying: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Qabus ibn al-Mukhariq, from Lubabah bint al-Harith who said: al-Hasan ibn Ali urinated in the lap of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I said: Give me your garment and wear another garment. He said: The urine of a female is washed, and the urine of a male is sprinkled over."
"22803","41 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn al-Husayn Abu Malik al-Ashja'i informed us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Qabus ibn al-Mukhariq, from his father, from Umm al-Fadl who said: I said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I saw in a dream as if part of you were in my house. He said: Good; Fatimah will give birth to a boy and you will nurse him in your house. She gave birth to Hasan, and he was in my house. I brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he urinated on him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, cast off this garment so I may wash it. He said: The urine of females is washed, and the urine of a male is not washed."
"22804","Shaddad Abu Ammar from Umm al-Fadl 42 - Abu Zayd Ahmad ibn Yazid al-Hawti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Mus'ab al-Qarqasani narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, from Abu Ammar, from Umm al-Fadl: that she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw a repugnant dream in my sleep. He said: And what is it? She said: May Allah keep you well, it is severe. He said: What is it? She said: I saw as if a piece of your body was cut off then placed in my lap. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You saw good; Fatimah will give birth - if Allah wills - to a boy who will be in your lap. Fatimah gave birth to Hasan - and he was in her lap - so I entered with him upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and placed him, and he urinated on him. I went to take him, and he said: Leave my son, for my son is not impure. Then he called for water and poured it over him."
"22805","Hind bint al-Harith al-Khath'amiyyah, Umm Abd Allah ibn Shaddad, from Umm al-Fadl 43 - Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Sa'igh al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn al-Had: Hind bint al-Harith al-Khath'amiyyah, wife of Abd Allah ibn Shaddad, narrated to me, from Umm al-Fadl, Umm Abd Allah ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he stood one night at Makkah during the night and said: O Allah, have I conveyed? Three times. Umar ibn al-Khattab stood and said: O Allah, yes; you were keen, you advised, and you strove. In the morning he said: Faith will surely prevail until it drives disbelief back to its homelands, and the seas will surely be crossed with Islam. A time will surely come upon people in which they learn the Qur'an, and they learn it and recite it, then they say: We have recited and learned, so who is better than us? Is there any good in those? They said: No, O Messenger of Allah, and who are those? He said: Those are the fuel of the Fire."
"22806","44 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub and Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: that Ibn al-Had narrated to them from Hind bint al-Harith, from Umm al-Fadl, wife of al-Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon al-Abbas while he was ill, and al-Abbas wished for death. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O uncle, do not wish for death; if you are a doer of good, then if you are delayed you will increase in goodness added to your good, and if you are a doer of evil, then if you are delayed and you seek to make amends for your evil, that is better for you. So do not wish for death."
"22807","Layla al-Ghifariyyah 45 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Hashim ibn al-Barid, from Muslim Abu Hashim, from Musa ibn al-Qasim al-Tha'labi, from Layla al-Ghifariyyah who said: I was a woman who used to go out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) treating the wounded."
"22808","Layla bint Qanif al-Thaqafiyyah 46 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, Nuh ibn Hakim - who was a reciter of the Qur'an - narrated to me, from a man of Banu Urwah ibn Mas'ud called Dawud, whose mother was Umm Habibah bint Abi Sufyan, from Layla bint Qanif al-Thaqafiyyah who said: I was among those who washed Umm Kulthum, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the first thing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave us was the waist-wrapper, then the shirt, then the head-cover, then the sheet, then she was wrapped in the last garment."
"22809","Layla bint Abi Hathmah ibn Hudhayfah, Umm Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, among the emigrant women 47 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah informed me, from his mother Layla who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab was among the severest of people against us regarding our Islam. When we prepared to leave for the land of Abyssinia, Umar ibn al-Khattab came to me while I was on my camel intending to depart, and said: Where to, O Umm Abd Allah? I said: You have harmed us regarding our religion, so we go in Allah's land where we will not be harmed in the worship of Allah. He said: May Allah accompany you. Then he left, and my husband Amir ibn Rabi'ah came to me, and I informed him of what I had seen of Umar's softness. He said: Do you hope he will become Muslim? I said: Yes. He said: By Allah, he will not become Muslim until the donkey of al-Khattab becomes Muslim."
"22810","Lubaysah bint Ka'b, also called bint Harb, Umm Umarah al-Ansariyyah of Banu al-Najjar 48 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Among the women who witnessed al-Aqabah were Lubaysah and her sister, the two daughters of Ka'b ibn Amr ibn Awf, and she is Umm Umarah whose son was taken by Musaylimah."
"22811","49 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Habib al-Ansari, from Layla, a freedwoman of theirs, from her grandmother Umm Umarah bint Ka'b: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her, and she called for food for him, and he invited her to eat with him. She said: I am fasting. He said: When a fasting person has food eaten in his house, the angels supplicate for him until they finish their meal and are done."
"22812","50 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani and Ali ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to us. And Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Yahya Zahmawayh narrated to us, all saying: Sharik narrated to us, from Habib ibn Zayd, from Layla, from her mistress who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me, so I brought him fruit or food, and some of the people withdrew and did not eat. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is wrong with them? I said: They are fasting. He said: There is no fasting person at whose place fasting-breaking people eat except that the angels bless him until evening."
"22813","51 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Umm Umarah al-Ansariyyah: that she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I see everything is for men; I do not see women mentioned with anything. Then was revealed: Verily the Muslim men and Muslim women, and the believing men and believing women."
"22814","52 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Musarrif ibn Amr al-Yami narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ikrimah who said: A woman called Umm Umarah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, the Qur'an is revealed only about men. So Allah revealed this verse: Verily the Muslim men and Muslim women, and the believing men and believing women."
"22815","53 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, my father narrated to us. And Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, both saying: Jarir narrated to us, from Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Umm Umarah who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I see everything is only for men. So Allah - mighty and majestic is He - revealed: Verily the Muslim men and Muslim women, and the believing men and believing women, the verse."
"22816","The chapter of Mim: Maymunah bint Sa'd, servant of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) 54 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abi Sawdah, from Maymunah - who is not Maymunah the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - that she said: O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling about Bayt al-Maqdis. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The land of gathering and resurrection; go to it and pray in it, for prayer in it is like a thousand prayers. She said: What about one who cannot bear to go to it? He said: If he cannot do that, let him send oil to be lit in it; whoever sends a gift to it is as one who prayed in it."
"22817","55 - Ahmad ibn Mutayr al-Ramli the judge narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari al-Asqalani narrated to us, Rawwad ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us. And Ya'qub ibn Ishaq ibn al-Zubayr al-Halabi narrated to us, Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Ziyad ibn Abi Sawdah, from his brother Uthman ibn Abi Sawdah, from Maymunah, freedwoman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she said: O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling about Bayt al-Maqdis. He said: The land of resurrection and gathering; go to it and pray in it, for a prayer in it is like a thousand prayers. She said: What if we cannot bear to travel to it or come to it? He said: Then send oil to it to be lit in it, for whoever sends a gift to it is as one who prayed in it."
"22818","56 - Ahmad ibn Mutayr al-Ramli the judge narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari al-Asqalani narrated to us, Rawwad ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, from Sadaqah ibn Sadaqah, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Ziyad ibn Abi Sawdah, from his brother, from Maymunah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same as it."
"22819","57 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us. And Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malati narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, both saying: Isra'il narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Jubayr, from Abu Yazid al-Dinni, from Maymunah bint Sa'd: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about a man who kissed his wife while both were fasting. He said: They have both broken their fast."
"22820","58 - Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malati narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us. And Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, Isra'il informed us, from Zayd ibn Jubayr, from Abu Yazid al-Dinni, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, freedwoman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the child of adultery. He said: There is no good in him; two sandals with which I strive in jihad are more beloved to me than freeing a child of adultery."
"22821","59 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tara'ifi narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, that she said: Give us a ruling, O Messenger of Allah, about a man who forgot prayer until the sun rose or set; what is its expiation? He said: When he remembers it, let him pray it, and let him pray it well, and let him perform ablution and perform his ablution well; that is its expiation."
"22822","60 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tara'ifi narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, that she said: O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling about fasting. He said: Three days of every month for whoever is able to fast them; each day expiates ten evil deeds, and it cleanses of sin as water cleanses a garment."
"22823","61 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, that she said: Give us a ruling, O Messenger of Allah, about theft. He said: Whoever consumes it while knowing it is stolen has shared in the sin of its thief."
"22824","62 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, that she said: Give us a ruling about charity. He said: It is a barrier from the Fire for whoever does it well, seeking thereby the Face of Allah, mighty and majestic is He."
"22825","63 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, that she said: O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling about dogs. He said: Food of the pre-Islamic era, and Allah has sufficed beyond it."
"22826","64 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, that she said: Give us a ruling, O Messenger of Allah, about washing from major ritual impurity. He said: Wet the roots of the hair and clean the skin; the likeness of those who do not wash well is as a tree struck by water, neither its leaves grow nor its root is quenched. So fear Allah and wash well, for it is among the trust you carry and the secrets entrusted to you. She said: I said: How much water suffices the head, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Three handfuls."
"22827","65 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, may one of us eat while in major ritual impurity? He said: He should not eat until he performs ablution. She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, may one in major impurity sleep? He said: I do not like that he sleep while in major impurity until he performs ablution and performs his ablution well; I fear he may die and Jibril - peace be upon him - will not attend him."
"22828","66 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, that she said: Give us a ruling, O Messenger of Allah, about the Friday prayer. He said: In it there is an hour in which a servant does not supplicate his Lord except He answers him. I said: Which hour is it, O Messenger of Allah? He said: That is when the imam stands."
"22829","67 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, that she said: Give us a ruling, O Messenger of Allah, about a man who is distracted in his prayer and does not know how much he prayed? He said: He should leave, then stand in his prayer until he knows how much he prayed; that is only whispering that comes to him and makes him forgetful in his prayer."
"22830","68 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, that she said: Give us a ruling, O Messenger of Allah, from what is the punishment of the grave? He said: From traces of urine; whoever is struck by urine, let him wash it, and if he finds no water, let him wipe it with clean earth."
"22831","69 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid, from Aminah bint Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, that she said: Give us a ruling, O Messenger of Allah, about one who did not fight but gave his wealth for fighting to be done with it; does he have reward, or does the one who goes forth? He said: He has the reward of his wealth, and the one who goes forth has the reward of what he intended thereby."
"22832","70 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us. And Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, al-Muharibi narrated to us, both from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Ayyub ibn Khalid, from Maymunah bint Sa'd - who was a servant of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of a woman who struts in adornment before those not of her family is as darkness on the Day of Resurrection, with no light for her."
"22833","71 - Mus'ab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Ayyub ibn Khalid, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There is no woman who goes out in conspicuous perfume while men look at her except she remains in Allah's wrath until she returns to her house."
"22834","Maymunah bint Abi Asib, freedwoman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) 72 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini narrated to us, al-Muntaji' ibn Mus'ab al-Mazini narrated to us, Rabi'ah bint Yazid narrated to me, Mayyah narrated to me, from Maymunah bint Asib, freedwoman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that a woman of Harish came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a camel and called out: O A'ishah, help me with a supplication from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to settle me or comfort me, and that he said to her: Place your right hand on your heart and say: In the name of Allah; O Allah, treat me with Your remedy, cure me with Your cure, enrich me with Your bounty from all besides You, and remove Your harm from me. Rabi'ah said: I supplicated with it and found it good. al-Muntaji' said: And I think Rabi'ah said in this hadith: The woman was other than me."
"22835","Maymunah bint Kardam al-Thaqafiyyah 73 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'la ibn Ka'b al-Ta'ifi narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Miqsam, from Maymunah bint Kardam: that she was riding behind her father, and she heard her father ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I have vowed to slaughter at Buwanah. He said: Is there an idol or false deity worshipped there? He said: No. He said: Fulfill your vow where you vowed."
"22836","74 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam and al-Hadrami narrated to us, both saying: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ta'ifi, from Maymunah bint Kardam al-Yasariyyah: that her father met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while she was riding behind him, and he asked him: I have vowed to slaughter at Buwanah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Is there an idol there? He said to him: No. He said: Then fulfill your vow where you vowed."
"22837","75 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Jahm al-Shammari narrated to us, al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Abu Qutaybah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid ibn Dabbah al-Ta'ifi, my aunt Sarah bint Miqsam narrated to me, from Maymunah bint Kardam who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and the finger next to the thumb was longer than the thumb, meaning of the foot."
"22838","Maymunah bint Subayh, mother of Abu Hurayrah 76 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Abu Kathir al-Suhaymi narrated to me, saying: Abu Hurayrah narrated to us, saying: By Allah, no believer whom Allah created hears of me or sees me except he loves me. I said: And how do you know that, O Abu Hurayrah? He said: My mother was a polytheist woman, and I used to call her to Islam, but she refused me. One day I called her and she said to me about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what I disliked. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) weeping, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I used to call my mother to Islam, but she refused me; I called her and she said to me about you what I disliked, so supplicate to Allah to guide the mother of Abu Hurayrah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, guide the mother of Abu Hurayrah. He said: I went out running to give her glad tidings of the supplication of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When I came to the door, it was ajar, and I heard the splashing of water, and she heard the sound of my feet and said: O Abu Hurayrah, stay where you are. She opened the door, put on her shirt, and hastened to her head-cover, then said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. I returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) weeping from joy as I had wept from grief, and said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has answered you regarding my mother, so supplicate to Allah to make me and my mother beloved to His believing servants and make them beloved to us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, make Your servant and Your maidservant beloved to Your believing servants and make them beloved to them."
"22839","Mariyah, grandmother of al-Muthanna ibn Salih, servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) 77 - Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Saffar narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Muthanna ibn Salih, from his grandmother Mariyah - who was a servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - who said: I never saw a hand softer than that of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"22840","78 - Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi narrated to us, al-Mu'alla ibn Asad al-Ammi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humran narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Habib, from Umm Sulayman, from her mother, from her grandmother Mariyah who said: I lowered myself for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he climbed a wall on the night he fled from the polytheists."
"22841","Mulaykah bint Amr al-Sa'diyyah 79 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, a woman of my family narrated to me, from Mulaykah bint Amr al-Zaydiyyah of the descendants of Zayd Allah ibn Sa'd who said: I complained of a pain in my throat, so I came to her and she prepared cow's ghee for me. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Its milk is a cure, its ghee is a remedy, and its meat is a disease."
"22842","Maryam al-Maghaliyyah, wife of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas 80 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ubadah ibn al-Walid ibn Ubadah ibn al-Samit who said: I entered upon al-Rubayyi' bint Mu'awwidh ibn Afra and asked her about her affair. She said: I was with a cousin of mine and I disliked him, so I sought khul' from him. I came to Uthman, and he said: There is no waiting period upon you from him, unless he was recently with you, then you should ensure you are free of pregnancy by one menstruation. al-Rubayyi' said: Uthman only took that from the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Maryam al-Maghaliyyah, wife of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas, when she sought khul' from him."
"22843","The chapter of Nun: Nu'ma bint Ja'far ibn Abi Talib 81 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Faraj narrated to me, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ata, from Asma bint Umays: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Nu'ma bint Ja'far: Why do I see the bodies of the sons of Ja'far thin? Are they in need? She said: No, but the evil eye quickly strikes them; shall I recite protective supplications for them? He said: Yes. She presented to him words with no harm in them, and he said: Recite for them."
"22844","Nuwaylah bint Muslim 82 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Raja ibn Muhammad al-Saqati narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Idris narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Ja'far narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his grandmother Umm Nuwaylah bint Muslim who said: We prayed Zuhr or Asr in the mosque of Banu Harithah, facing the mosque of Iliya, and we prayed two rak'ahs. Then one who informed us came to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had faced the Sacred House, so the men moved to the place of the women and the women to the place of the men, and we prayed the remaining two prostrations facing the Sacred House. A man of Banu Harithah narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Those are men who believed in the unseen."
"22845","Nasikah Umm Amr ibn Julas 83 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qatawani narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Isma'il, from Habibah bint Sam'an, from Nasikah Umm Amr ibn Julas who said: I was with A'ishah when she had slaughtered a sheep of hers, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered with a small stick in his hand. He cast it, then went to the mosque and prayed two rak'ahs in it, then went to his bed and lay on it, then said: Is there any lunch? We brought him a dish with barley bread in it, with a morsel and a piece of tripe in it, and the foreleg in it. He said: She took a piece of tripe, and she was biting it, when she said: We slaughtered a sheep today and kept nothing but this. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says: No, rather all of it was kept except this."
"22846","Nusaybah Umm Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah, who settled in Basrah; what Umm Atiyyah narrated: Anas ibn Malik from Umm Atiyyah 84 - Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Awfi narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik: that he had taken that from Umm Atiyyah who said: We washed the daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he commanded us to wash her with lote leaves three times, and if not, five, and if not, more than that; we thought more than that was seven."
"22847","Isma'il ibn Abd al-Rahman from his grandmother Umm Atiyyah 85 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Nusayr narrated to us. And Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, both saying: Isma'il ibn Uthman al-Adawi narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Atiyyah narrated to us, from his grandmother Umm Atiyyah who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Madinah, he gathered the women in a house, then sent Umar ibn al-Khattab to us. He stood at the door, greeted, then said: I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to you, to take your allegiance that you will not steal, nor commit adultery, nor kill your children, nor bring slander you fabricate between your hands and feet, nor disobey him in what is right. She said: We put our hands out from outside the door and he put out his hand, and we gave him allegiance. She said: He commanded us to bring out in the two Eids the secluded maidens and menstruating women, and forbade us to go out in a funeral procession or attend Friday prayer. I said to her: What is not disobeying him? She said: Wailing."
"22848","Muhammad ibn Sirin from Umm Atiyyah 86 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), passed away, and he said: Wash her three or five times, or more than that if you see fit, and wash her with water and lote leaves, and put in the last something of camphor. When you finish, inform me. When we finished we informed him, and he cast to us his waist-wrapper. He said: Wrap her in it. She said: We made her hair three braids and sent them behind her. She said: The waist-wrapper is a small garment."
"22849","87 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani informed me that he heard Ibn Sirin say: There was a woman of the Ansar called Umm Atiyyah, among those who gave allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she came to Basrah hastening to a son of hers, but did not reach him. She narrated to us, and he mentioned similar to the hadith of Ma'mar."
"22850","88 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, from Malik. And Abu Yazid al-Qaratisisi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us. And Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, both saying: Malik ibn Anas informed us, from Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us when his daughter passed away, and said: Wash her three or five times, or more than that if you see fit, with water and lote leaves, and put in the last camphor or something of camphor. When you finish, inform me. She said: When we finished we informed him, and he gave us his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it, meaning his garment."
"22851","89 - al-Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and we gathered with her. We entered upon her, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us and said: Wash her three or five times, or more than that if you see fit, with water and lote leaves, and put in the last camphor or something of camphor. When you finish, inform me. When we finished we informed him, and he cast to us his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it."
"22852","90 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us. And Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Harb al-Abbadani narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, both saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Umm Atiyyah who said: One of the daughters of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and said: Wash her three or five times, or more if you see fit, with water and lote leaves, and put in the last camphor or something of camphor. When you finish, inform me. When we finished we informed him, and he cast to us his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it."
"22853","91 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us while we were washing his daughter, and said: Wash her three or five times, or more than that if you see fit, with water and lote leaves, and put in the last camphor or something of camphor. When you finish, inform me. When we finished we informed him, and he cast to us his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it."
"22854","92 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah: that she said: The daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wash her with water and lote leaves three or five times, or more than that if you see fit, and put in the last of them something of camphor. When you finish, inform me. We informed him, and he cast his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it."
"22855","93 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ullayah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us while we were washing his daughter, and he mentioned similar to it."
"22856","94 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Mansur ibn Zadham informed us, from Ibn Sirin; and Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin; and Khalid ibn al-Hadhdha, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: One of the daughters of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and he commanded us to wash her with water and lote leaves, and said: Wash her three or five times, or more than that if you see fit, and put in the last of them camphor or something of camphor. When you finish, inform me. When we informed him, he cast to us his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it."
"22857","95 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Abu al-Sha'tha Ali ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Hisham and Ash'ath, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: One of the daughters of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wash her with water and lote leaves, and wash her an odd number: three or five times, and if you see fit to add, then add, and let that be odd. Put in the last camphor or something of camphor. Then he cast to us his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it."
"22858","96 - Yusuf the judge narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Zayd ibn Ibrahim al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Umm Atiyyah who said: One of the daughters of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and he commanded us to wash her three or five times, or more than that if you see fit, and to put in the last washing something of camphor and lote leaves."
"22859","97 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, al-Nu'man ibn Abd al-Salam narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman, brother of Abu Hurrah, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and he said: Wash her three or five times, or more than that if you see fit, with water and lote leaves, and put in the last something of camphor. When you finish, inform me. When we finished we informed him, and he cast to us his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it."
"22860","98 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati and Muhammad ibn Mu'adh al-Halabi narrated to us, both saying: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub and Habib and Hisham, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and he said: Wash her with water and lote leaves three or five times, or more than that if you see fit, and put in the last of them something of camphor. When you finish, inform me. She said: We informed him, and he cast to us his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it. Ayyub said: She said in a story: Wash her three or five or seven times, and make for her three braids."
"22861","99 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Jawaribi al-Wasiti narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Rashid al-Adami narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Hamzah al-Shu'aythi narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and he entered upon us while we were washing her, and said: Wash her three or five times. When you finish, inform me. When we finished we informed him, and he cast to us his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it."
"22862","100 - Ishaq ibn Jamil al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mani' informed us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Ibn Awn informed us, from Muhammad, from Umm Atiyyah, in marfu' form, similar to it."
"22863","Chapter 101 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, Imran al-Qattan informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, Umm Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah narrated to us, saying: I went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on expeditions; we used to attend to the wounded and treat the injured. I said: O Messenger of Allah, one of us goes out with people on the day of Fitr and the day of sacrifice. She said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Bring out the secluded maidens, those in seclusion, and menstruating women; they witness goodness and the supplication of the Muslims. I said: O Messenger of Allah, one of us may not have a garment? He said: Let her sister clothe her with her garment."
"22864","102 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub and Yunus and Hisham and Habib, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah: that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to bring out the secluded maidens and those in seclusion. It was said: And menstruating women? He said: Yes; they witness goodness and the supplication of the Muslims. We said: O Messenger of Allah, one of us may not have a garment. He said: Let her sister clothe her with part of her garment."
"22865","103 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it."
"22866","104 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us. And Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna and Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, both saying: Ibrahim ibn Abi Suwayd narrated to us, both saying: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub and Yunus and Habib and Hisham and Yahya ibn Atiq, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to bring out the secluded maidens and those in seclusion on the day of Eid. It was said: And menstruating women? He said: They witness goodness and the supplication of the Muslims. A woman said: O Messenger of Allah, if one of us has no garment, what should she do? He said: Let her companion clothe her with part of her garment."
"22867","105 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hushaym narrated to us, Mansur ibn Zadham informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bring out the virgins, secluded maidens, those in seclusion, and menstruating women on the two Eids. As for menstruating women, they keep away from the prayer place and witness goodness and the supplication of the Muslims. One of them said: If one of them has no cloak? He said: Let her sister lend her from her cloaks."
"22868","106 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna ibn Mu'adh al-Anbari narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We were commanded to go out on the two Eids, and we bring out menstruating women, secluded maidens, and those in seclusion. As for menstruating women, they witness the congregation of the Muslims and their supplication and keep away from their prayer."
"22869","107 - al-Nu'man ibn Ahmad al-Wasiti the judge narrated to us, Haydun ibn Abd Allah Abu Haydarah narrated to us, Silah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ash'ath ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We used to be commanded to go out on the two Eids, those in seclusion and menstruating women. As for menstruating women, they witness the prayer place of the Muslims."
"22870","108 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Jawaribi al-Wasiti narrated to us, Abu Mahdhurah Muhammad ibn Ubayd Allah narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn Atiyyah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Umm Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to bring out the secluded maidens, those in seclusion, and menstruating women on the day of Eid. As for menstruating women, they witness the prayer place of the Muslims."
"22871","109 - Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tustari narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to bring out on the two Eids those in seclusion and menstruating women; they witness with the Muslims their supplication and prayer, and menstruating women keep away from prayer."
"22872","Chapter 110 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna ibn Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh al-Anbari narrated to us, my father narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah: that she said, mentioning the allegiance of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): No woman among us fulfilled it except Umm Sulaym, Umm Kulthum, and the wife of Mu'adh ibn Abi Sabrah."
"22873","111 - Ibrahim ibn Na'ilah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, al-Nu'man ibn Abd al-Salam narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman, Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Umm Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took from the women in what he took that they would not wail; none of them fulfilled it except so-and-so and so-and-so and the mother of so-and-so."
"22874","Chapter 112 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, more than one narrated to us, among them Mansur ibn Zadham from Muhammad ibn Sirin, and Hisham from Muhammad, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We were forbidden from following funeral processions, but it was not made binding upon us."
"22875","113 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We were forbidden from following funeral processions, but it was not made binding upon us."
"22876","114 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ullayah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin who said: Umm Atiyyah said: We used to be forbidden from following funeral processions, but it was not made binding upon us."
"22877","115 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna ibn Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh al-Anbari narrated to us, my father narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We were forbidden from following funeral processions, but it was not made binding upon us."
"22878","Chapter 116 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Nusayr ibn Ali al-Jahdami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bilal narrated to us, Imran al-Qattan narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn more than three days, except for a husband."
"22879","117 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We were commanded not to wear dyed clothes in mourning, except asb garments; we were commanded not to mourn over one who perishes - or she said over a dead person - more than three, except for a husband; and we were commanded not to touch perfume except the slightest at purification: kust and nails."
"22880","118 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, Salamah ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin who said: A son of Umm Atiyyah died, and when it was the third day she called for yellow perfume and wiped herself with it, and said: We were forbidden to mourn over a dead person more than three nights, except for a husband."
"22881","Chapter 119 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We did not consider yellowish and murky discharge anything."
"22882","120 - Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Husayn al-Sabuni al-Tustari narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Mudrik narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah al-Qurashi narrated to us, Sulayman al-Qafalani narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah does not accept the prayer of one whose nose does not touch the ground."
"22883","Hafsah bint Sirin from Umm Atiyyah 121 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, Hafsah bint Sirin narrated to us, from Umm Atiyyah who said: I went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on seven expeditions; I stayed behind them in their dwellings, prepared food for them, attended to the wounded, and treated the sick."
"22884","122 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on seven expeditions; I stayed behind them in their dwellings, prepared food for them, treated the wounded, and attended to the sick."
"22885","Chapter 123 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin who said: Umm Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah came to us in Basrah, and said: May my father and mother be sacrificed for him - meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he commanded us to bring out on the two Eids the secluded maidens, those in seclusion, and menstruating women. As for menstruating women, they keep away from the prayer place and witness goodness and the supplication of the Muslims."
"22886","124 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, Hafsah bint Sirin narrated to me, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - may my father and mother be sacrificed for him - commanded us to bring them out on the day of Fitr and the day of sacrifice: the secluded maidens, those in seclusion, and menstruating women. She said: As for menstruating women, they keep away from the prayer place and witness goodness and the supplication of the Muslims. She said: We said: What about one of them who has no cloak? He said: Let her sister clothe her with her cloak. Hisham said: I heard Muhammad mention it from Umm Atiyyah, but I remember the hadith of Hafsah better."
"22887","125 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it."
"22888","126 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it."
"22889","127 - Muhammad ibn Musa al-Istakhri narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Abbad al-Kirmani narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to bring out on the day of Fitr the secluded maidens, those in seclusion, and menstruating women. As for menstruating women, they keep away from prayer and witness goodness and the supplication of the Muslims. A woman said: O Messenger of Allah, if one of us has no cloak? He said: Let her sister clothe her with her cloak."
"22890","128 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We used to be commanded to go out on the two Eids, the menstruating and the virgin. She said: Menstruating women go out and stay behind people, magnifying Allah with people."
"22891","129 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Hafsah bint Sirin who said: I asked Umm Atiyyah: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning about a woman who has no cloak, may she attend Eid? She said: Yes, may my father be sacrificed; whenever she narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) she said: May my father be sacrificed; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Bring out the secluded maidens and those in seclusion; let them witness Eid and the supplication of the Muslims, and let menstruating women keep away from the prayer place of the Muslims."
"22892","130 - Ishaq ibn Jamil al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mani' narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ullayah narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, similar to it."
"22893","131 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to go out on the day of the two Eids, until young girls and secluded maidens go out. Hafsah said: A woman said to Umm Atiyyah: And menstruating women? She said: Menstruating women go out and keep away from the prayer place and remember Allah."
"22894","Chapter 132 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: It was taken from us in the allegiance at the time of allegiance that we would not wail; none of them fulfilled it except five: Umm Sulaym, Umm al-Ala, the daughter of Abu Sabrah, Umm Mu'adh bint Abi Sabrah, and another woman."
"22895","133 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna and Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, both saying: Musaddad narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We gave allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he recited to us that we associate nothing with Allah. He forbade us from wailing. A woman among us withheld her hand and said: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so helped me and I want to repay her. He said nothing to her. She went, then returned. She said: No woman among us fulfilled it except Umm Sulaym, Umm al-Ala, the daughter of Abu Sabrah, and the wife of Mu'adh."
"22896","134 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: Among what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took from us at the allegiance was that we would not wail."
"22897","135 - Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Hafs al-Nufayli narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We gave allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and among what he commanded us of right was that we would not wail. A woman said: O Messenger of Allah, the family of so-and-so helped me, and I will not give you allegiance until I help them. She said: She helped them, then gave him allegiance. She said: No woman among us fulfilled it except me and Umm Sulaym."
"22898","136 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us. And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, both saying: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah: regarding His saying: And they will not disobey you in what is right, she said: Among it is wailing. I said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when we gave him allegiance that we would not disobey him in what is right: O Messenger of Allah, except the family of so-and-so."
"22899","Chapter 137 - Yusuf ibn Ya'qub the judge narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us. And Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, both saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We were forbidden to mourn over a dead person more than three days; we do not apply perfume, nor apply kohl, nor wear a dyed garment except an asb garment. She said: They were permitted, when washing at purification, kust and nails."
"22900","138 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us. And Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, both saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: A woman whose husband has died does not mourn over a dead person except over a husband; she does not apply kohl, nor dye, nor wear a dyed garment except an asb garment, nor apply perfume. Hisham said, from Hafsah, from Umm Atiyyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it."
"22901","139 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us. And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, both saying: Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Mourning is not observed over a dead person more than three days except by a woman, for she mourns over her husband four months and ten days; she does not wear a dyed garment except an asb garment, nor apply kohl, nor apply perfume, except at her slightest purification, perfuming with kust and nails."
"22902","140 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Ubayd narrated to us. And Ishaq ibn Jamil al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mani' informed us, both saying: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn more than three days, except over a husband. He mentioned similar to it."
"22903","141 - Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tustari narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A woman does not mourn more than three days, except over her husband, for she mourns four months and ten days; she does not wear a dyed garment except a washed garment, nor apply kohl nor dye, nor touch perfume, except when she is purified from her menstruation thereafter, with kust or nails."
"22904","Chapter 142 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Salim al-Razi narrated to us, Sahl ibn Uthman narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We were forbidden from following funeral processions, but it was not made binding upon us."
"22905","143 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We used to be forbidden from following funeral processions, but it was not made binding upon us."
"22906","144 - Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah who said: We were forbidden from following funeral processions, but it was not made binding upon us."
"22907","145 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, the same as it."
"22908","146 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Hisham, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, the same as it."
"22909","147 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Fazari, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Umm al-Hudhayl, from Umm Atiyyah who said: He forbade us from following funeral processions, but did not make it binding upon us."
"22910","Chapter 148 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Amr ibn Awf al-Wasiti narrated to us. And Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, both saying: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon A'ishah and said: Do you have anything? She said: No, except something Nusaybah - who is Umm Atiyyah - sent us of the sheep you sent her from charity. He said: It has reached its place."
"22911","149 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Khalid al-Hadhdha' narrated to us, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah: that the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said to A'ishah: Do you have anything to feed me? She said: No, except from the sheep you sent to Nusaybah from the charity. He said: Bring it, for it has reached its lawful place."
"22912","150 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - sent me a sheep from the charity, so I sent some of it to A'ishah. When the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - came to A'ishah, he said: Do you have anything? She said: No, except that Nusaybah sent us from the sheep you sent to her. He said: It has reached its lawful place."
"22913","Chapter. 151 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Umm al-Hudhayl, from Umm Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah, who had pledged allegiance to the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - she said: We used not to count murky or yellowish discharge after the bath as anything."
"22914","152 - Yusuf the judge narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: We used not to count murky or yellowish discharge after purity as anything."
"22915","153 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: We used not to count murky or yellowish discharge as anything."
"22916","Chapter. 154 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Hisham ibn Hassan (ha). And Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: One of the daughters of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - died, so the Prophet of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - came to us and said: Wash her with lote leaves, and wash her an odd number of times, three or five, or more than that if you see fit, and put camphor in the last wash. When you finish, inform me. She said: When we finished, we informed him, so he threw his waist-wrapper to us and said: Wrap her in it as an inner garment. She said: We plaited her head into three braids: her forelock and her two side locks, then we threw them behind her."
"22917","155 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Fazari, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah: One of the daughters of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - died, so the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Wash her with water and lote leaves, and wash her an odd number of times, three or five, or more if you see fit, and put camphor in the last wash, or something of camphor. When you finish, inform me. So we informed him, and he threw his waist-wrapper to us and said: Wrap her in it as an inner garment. She said: We plaited her head into three braids: its front and its two side locks, and we threw them behind her."
"22918","156 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, the like of it."
"22919","157 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, from the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - the like of it."
"22920","158 - Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Mustamli narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: We washed the daughter of the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and parted her head into three braids."
"22921","159 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani informed me, he said: I heard Hafsah bint Sirin saying: Umm Atiyyah narrated to us: that they made the head of the daughter of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - into three braids. I said: Did you undo it, wash it, and make it into three braids? She said: Yes."
"22922","160 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us (ha). And Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna and Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, they said: Musaddad narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, that the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said regarding the washing of his daughter: Begin with her right side and the places of ablution."
"22923","161 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Umm al-Hudhayl, who is Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: When we washed the daughter of the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - he said to us while we were washing her: Begin with her right side and the places of ablution."
"22924","162 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Khallad al-Qattan al-Nasri narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Jabalah narrated to us, Umm Isa bint Hashim narrated to us, she said: I heard Hafsah bint Sirin, Umm Atiyyah said to us: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - commanded us not to withhold small household utensils. I said: What is ma'un? She said: What people pass around among themselves."
"22925","163 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Khallad narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Jabalah narrated to us, Hafsah bint Amr, the freedwoman of Anas ibn Sirin, narrated to us, she said: I heard Hafsah bint Sirin saying: I heard Umm Atiyyah saying: We used to go out with the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - treating the wounded and burying the slain."
"22926","164 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Khallad narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Jabalah narrated to us, Habbah bint Habib al-Adawiyyah narrated to us, she said: Hafsah bint Sirin narrated to us, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - used to command us to guard our private parts and our tongues, and he said: They bring you in but do not bring you out."
"22927","165 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us (ha). And Ishaq ibn Jamil al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mani' narrated to us, they said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: When the daughter of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - died, the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said to us: Wash her an odd number of times, three or five, and put camphor in the last wash, or something of camphor. When you have washed her, inform me. So we informed him. She said: He threw his waist-wrapper to us and said: Wrap her in it as an inner garment."
"22928","166 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Salim al-Hannat, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, that a daughter of the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - died, so the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered and said: Wash her three times, an odd number, or five, or as you see fit, and make it odd. So we washed her, then he entered and threw his waist-wrapper to us and said: Wrap her in it beneath her shroud, and put camphor in the last of it."
"22929","167 - Banubah ibn Khalid al-Ubulli narrated to us, Umar ibn Yahya al-Ayli narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Abd al-Karim al-Dall narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from his sister, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - forbade us wearing gold and silver-plating cups; the women spoke to him about wearing gold but he refused us, and he permitted us silver-plating of cups."
"22930","Umm Sharahil, from Umm Atiyyah. 168 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abu al-Jarrah, from Jabir ibn Subh, from Umm Sharahil, from Umm Atiyyah, that the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - sent Ali on a detachment, and I saw him raising his hands and saying: O Allah, do not cause me to die until You show me Ali."
"22931","Umm Habibah, freedwoman of Umm Atiyyah, from Umm Atiyyah. 169 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Umm Habibah, freedwoman of Umm Atiyyah, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: I was among the women who conducted one of the daughters of the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - to her husband, so the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said to us: When she bathes, pour water over her head three pourings. Yahya said: I said to Sharik: For major impurity? He said: Yes."
"22932","Chapter of Ha'. Hind bint Utbah ibn Rabi'ah ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf, mother of Mu'awiyah; from her reports. 170 - Muhammad ibn al-Sari ibn Mihran al-Naqid narrated to us, Abu al-Sukayn Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Ta'i narrated to us, my father's uncle Zahr ibn Hisn narrated to me, from his grandfather Humayd ibn Munhib al-Ta'i, he said: Hind bint Utbah was married to al-Fakih ibn al-Mughirah al-Makhzumi, and al-Fakih was among the youths of Quraysh, and he had a guest-house which people entered without permission. One day that house was empty, and al-Fakih and Hind lay down in it at midday. Then al-Fakih went out for one of his needs, and a man who used to visit him came, entered the house, and when he saw the woman he fled. Al-Fakih saw him leaving the house, so he came to Hind, kicked her with his foot and said to her: Who was this who was with you? She said: There was no one with me, and I did not wake until you woke me. He said: Go to your family. People talked about her. Her father said to her: My daughter, people have talked much about you, so tell me your story; if the man is truthful against you, I shall secretly send one to kill him so talk about you stops, and if he is lying I shall take him for judgment to one of the soothsayers of Yemen. She swore to him by what they used to swear by that he was lying about her. He said to al-Fakih: You have accused my daughter of a grave matter, so judge with me before one of the soothsayers of Yemen. Utbah went out with a group of Banu Abd Manaf, al-Fakih went out with a group of Banu Makhzum, and Hind went out with them along with women with her. When they neared the land and said: We shall come to the soothsayer, Hind's condition changed and her face altered. Her father said to her: I see what has come over you of changed condition, and that is only for something bad in your view; was this not before people witnessed our march? She said: No, by Allah, father, that is not for something bad, but I know you are coming to a human who errs and is right, and I do not feel safe that he may brand me with a mark that will be a disgrace upon me among the Arabs. He said: I shall test him before he looks into your matter. He whistled to his horse until it urinated, then took a grain of wheat, inserted it into its urethra and tied over it with a strap. In the morning they honored them and slaughtered for them. When they sat, Utbah said to him: We have come to you about a matter, and I have hidden for you something to test you with, so see what it is. He said: A striped cloth in a casing. He said: I want clearer than this. He said: A grain of wheat in the urethra of a foal. He said: You have spoken truly. Look into the matter of these women. He began approaching each of them, striking her shoulder and saying: Rise, until he approached Hind, struck her shoulder and said: Rise, neither wild nor an adulteress, and you shall give birth to a boy called Mu'awiyah. Al-Fakih rose to her and took her hand, but she pulled her hand from his and said: Away from me; by Allah, I shall strive that it be from other than you. Then Abu Sufyan married her, and she bore Mu'awiyah."
"22933","171 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah, she said: Hind came to the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, there was no household on the face of the earth dearer to me that Allah should disgrace than your household, and today there is no household on the face of the earth dearer to me that Allah should honor than your household. The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: And also, by Him in Whose Hand is my soul. Ma'mar said: meaning, it shall increase. Then she said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a withholding man; is there sin upon me if I take for his dependents from his wealth without his permission? The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: There is no harm upon you to spend upon them in a reasonable manner."
"22934","171 / 2 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, that he narrated to him, from A'ishah, that Hind, mother of Mu'awiyah, came to the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and said: Abu Sufyan is a miserly man, and he does not give me and my child except what I take from him without his knowledge; is there anything upon me in that? He said: Take what suffices you and your children in a reasonable manner."
"22935","172 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, she said: Hind, mother of Mu'awiyah, said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a miserly man; is there sin upon me if I take from his wealth secretly? He said: Take, you and your children, what suffices you in a reasonable manner."
"22936","173 - Muhammad ibn Mu'adh al-Halabi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, she said: Hind, mother of Mu'awiyah, came to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and said: Abu Sufyan is a miserly man and does not give me what suffices me and my child except what I take from him without his knowledge; is there any harm upon me in that? The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said to her: Take what suffices you and your children in a reasonable manner."
"22937","174 - Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-San'ani narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, that Hind, mother of Mu'awiyah, came to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a miserly man, and he does not give my child except what I take from him without his knowledge; is there anything upon me in that? The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said to her: Take what suffices you and your children in a reasonable manner."
"22938","175 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Waki' ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, she said: Hind came to the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and said: Abu Sufyan is a miserly man and does not give me and my child what suffices me except what I take from his wealth without his knowledge. He said: Take for yourself and your child what suffices you in a reasonable manner."
"22939","176 - Ahmad ibn al-Fadl al-Askari narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Hafs al-Nufayli narrated to us, Yunus ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, that Hind, mother of Mu'awiyah, came to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a miserly man, and he does not give me and my child except what I take from him without his knowledge; is there anything upon me in that? The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Take what suffices you and your children in a reasonable manner."
"22940","177 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Hammad al-Dabbi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yaman narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Hind, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a withholding man; may we feed our slaves from his wealth? He said: Yes."
"22941","Hujaymah, Umm al-Darda', and it is said her name is Khayrah, and we have already written it in the chapter of Kha'. 178 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Khalaf ibn Hawshab, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Umm al-Darda', he said: It was said to her: Did you hear anything from the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? She said: Yes, I heard him saying: The first thing placed in the Balance is good character."
"22942","179 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Rushdin ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Zabban ibn Fa'id, from Sahl ibn Mu'adh ibn Anas al-Juhani, from his father, he said: I heard Umm al-Darda' saying: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, no woman removes her clothes in other than her husband's house or her mothers' houses except that she tears every veil between her and the Most Merciful, mighty and majestic."
"22943","Chapter of Ya'. Yusayrah, among the emigrant women. 180 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Isma'il narrated to us (ha). And Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, Yahya al-Himmani and Abu Kurayb narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Hani ibn Uthman narrated to us, from his mother Humaydah, from her grandmother Yusayrah, who was one of the emigrant women, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said to us: O Muslim women, upon you is glorification, declaring Allah's oneness, and sanctification; count them on the fingertips, for they shall be given speech and questioned, and do not be heedless lest you be made to forget mercy. And Abu Kurayb gave the lineage of Hani ibn Uthman, saying: al-Juhani."
"22944","181 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna and Yusuf the judge narrated to us, they said: Musaddad narrated to us (ha). And Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Maktum narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Hani ibn Uthman, from his mother Humaydah, from her grandmother Yusayrah, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - commanding women to observe glorification, declaring Allah's oneness and sanctification, and to count on the fingertips, saying: They shall be questioned and given speech. And Ibn Dawud said: It reached me that al-Thawri asked Hani about this hadith."
"22945","Chapter of women known by kunyas, whose names have not reached us, among those who have companionship; among them Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt, wife of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf. 182 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, they said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Muslim, nephew of al-Zuhri, narrated to us, from his uncle Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt, that the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - was asked about: Say, He is Allah, One, and he said: A third of the Qur'an, or it equals it."
"22946","183 - Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Husayn informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from his mother Umm Kulthum, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good."
"22947","184 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, who was among the first emigrant women, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good."
"22948","185 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: He is not a liar who makes peace between people."
"22949","186 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir al-Buhturi al-Ta'i al-Himsi narrated to us, Bishr ibn Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from his father, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Umm Kulthum, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good."
"22950","187 - Abu Usamah Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Usamah al-Halabi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Abi Mani' al-Rusafi narrated to us, my grandfather Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, conveying good or saying good."
"22951","188 - Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, that she heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: The liar is not the one who makes peace between people, conveying good or saying it."
"22952","189 - Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, that Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman informed him that his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, who was among the female companions of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and among the first emigrant women who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, conveying good or saying good."
"22953","190 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me (ha). And Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, they said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: The liar is not that a man should say in mending what is between people."
"22954","191 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Malik ibn Sa'd al-Qaysi narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt, who was Uthman's sister through his mother, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good."
"22955","192 - Muhammad ibn Hatim al-Marwazi narrated to us, Hibban ibn Musa and Suwayd ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak informed us, from Yunus (ha). And Isma'il ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me that his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt, who was among the first emigrant women who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - informed him that she heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: The liar is not the one who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good."
"22956","193 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah informed us that Ibn al-Had narrated to him, from Abd al-Wahhab ibn Rufay', from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, she said: I did not see the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - permitting lying about anything except three; the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - did not count as lying: a man making peace between people, adding in speech intending reconciliation; a man speaking in war; and a man speaking to his wife and a woman speaking to her husband."
"22957","194 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Musa ibn Shaybah narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn Sirmah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abi Bakr, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum, she said: I did not hear the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - permit lying except in three; the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - used to say: I do not count as lying: a man making peace between people; a man speaking in war; and a man speaking to his wife, and a woman speaking to her husband."
"22958","195 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, they said: Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad al-Narsi narrated to us, Wuhayb narrated to us, Ayyub and Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, that the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good."
"22959","196 - Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb ibn al-Hajjaj al-Zubaydi narrated to us, Abu Humah Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Abu Qurrah narrated to us, he said: Zam'ah ibn Salih mentioned, from Ya'qub ibn Ata', from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good."
"22960","197 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good."
"22961","198 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his father, from Burd ibn Sinan and al-Awza'i, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: He is not a liar who says good or conveys good."
"22962","199 - Abu Usamah Abd Allah ibn Muhammad Abu Usamah al-Halabi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Abi Mani' al-Rusafi narrated to us, my grandfather Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me that his mother, Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, informed him that she heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, conveying good or saying good."
"22963","200 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, with its isnad, the like of it."
"22964","201 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khaythamah narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Tawbah narrated to us, Yunus ibn Muhammad al-Mu'addib narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Atiq, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum, from the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - he said: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, conveying good or saying good."
"22965","202 - Ishaq ibn Dawud al-Sawwaf al-Tustari narrated to us, Abu al-Ash'ath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Sulayman al-Numayri narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd narrated to us, from his father, from Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, that she heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: He is not a liar who reconciles a man between two persons."
"22966","203 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna and Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us, they said: Abu Bakr ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, my uncle Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt, who was the wife of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and had prayed facing both qiblas with the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good."
"22967","204 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, that the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: The best charity is upon the kindred who is hostile."
"22968","Umm Kulthum bint Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Asad al-Makhzumiyyah, foster-daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. 205 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us (ha). And Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us (ha). And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, they said: Muslim ibn Khalid al-Zanji narrated to us, Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to us, from his mother Umm Kulthum, she said: When the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - married Umm Salamah, he said: I had gifted to the Negus a gift, and I think it will only return to us; as I see, the Negus has died. I gifted him a suit and measures of musk, so when it returns to us, O Umm Salamah, it is yours. She said: It was as the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said; the Negus died and the gift returned to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - so he sent to each of his wives an ounce of musk, and sent the suit and the remaining musk to Umm Salamah."
"22969","206 - Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Hasan ibn Bakr ibn al-Sharud al-San'ani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Muslim ibn Khalid al-Zanji, the like of it."
"22970","Umm Farwah bint Abi Quhafah, sister of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq. 207 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, from al-Qasim ibn Ghannam, from some of his mothers or grandmothers, from Umm Farwah, who had pledged allegiance to the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - was asked: Which deeds are best? He said: Prayer at the beginning of its time."
"22971","208 - Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, from al-Qasim ibn Ghannam, from his grandmother Umm Abihi al-Dunya, from Umm Farwah, his father's grandmother, who was among those who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - that she heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: The most beloved of deeds to Allah is hastening prayer to the beginning of its time."
"22972","209 - Bakr ibn Ahmad ibn Sadwayh al-Basri narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, Qaza'ah ibn Suwayd al-Bahili narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Umar narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Ghannam, from his mothers, from Umm Farwah, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: The most beloved of deeds to Allah - mighty and majestic - is prayer at the beginning of its time."
"22973","210 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Judhu'i the judge narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Maymun al-Ataki narrated to us, Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from al-Qasim ibn Ghannam, from his grandmother Umm Farwah, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - was asked, while I listened: Which deeds are best? He said: Prayer at the beginning of its time."
"22974","211 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn Uthman, from al-Qasim ibn Ghannam, from a woman among those who pledged allegiance: that the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - was asked: Which deeds are best? He said: Faith in Allah, then prayer at the beginning of its time."
"22975","Umm Ruman, wife of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq. 212 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Husayn, from Shaqiq, from Masruq, he said: I asked Umm Ruman, mother of A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, about the affair of A'ishah when what was said about her was said, and Allah revealed her innocence. She said: Umm Mistah said: May Allah do to Mistah, and do. A'ishah said: Why? She said: Because he was among those who spread the talk. A'ishah said: And what talk of his? So she told her. She said: Did the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and Abu Bakr hear it? She said: Yes. So she fell unconscious, and she did not wake except with a shaking fever upon her. She said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - came and said: What is wrong with her? She said: We said: A fever seized her. He said: Perhaps it is because of talk being spoken. She said: She sat up and said: By Allah, if I swear you will not believe me, and if I excuse myself you will not excuse me; my likeness and yours is the likeness of Ya'qub and his sons, and Allah is the One Whose help is sought against what you describe. She said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - left, then Allah - mighty and majestic - revealed what He revealed; he came to her and told her, and she said: By the praise of Allah, not by the praise of anyone."
"22976","Umm Hakim bint al-Zubayr ibn Abd al-Muttalib ibn Hashim. 213 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar, from Umm Hakim, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon me and ate a shoulder; Bilal came and announced the prayer to him, so he prayed and did not perform ablution."
"22977","214 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us (ha). And Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us (ha). And Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us (ha). And Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, all of them from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Salih Abu al-Khalil, from Abd Allah ibn Harith, from Umm Hakim bint al-Zubayr, that the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon her sister Duba'ah bint al-Zubayr, tore meat from a shoulder, then stood and prayed without performing ablution."
"22978","215 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Harith, from Umm Hakim bint al-Zubayr, that she handed the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - a shoulder of meat, and he ate from it, then prayed."
"22979","216 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to me, Ja'far ibn Sulayman al-Duba'i narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ishaq al-Hashimi, Safiyyah narrated to me, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon me, so I offered him a shoulder and he ate from it, then he stood and prayed."
"22980","217 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Mukram al-Ammi narrated to me, Mahbub ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Harith, Umm Hakim bint al-Zubayr narrated to me, that she used to prepare food for the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and send it to him, and sometimes he came to her and ate with her; she claimed that he came to her one day, so she brought him a shoulder, and she scraped it for him, and she claimed that he ate and prayed without performing ablution."
"22981","Umm Ayman, mother of Usamah ibn Zayd, freedwoman of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - she belonged to the sister of Khadijah, who gifted her to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - so he married her to Zayd ibn Harithah; and it is said her name is Barakah. 218 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Ishkab narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Umm Ayman, who was among those who pledged allegiance to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"22982","219 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Umm Ayman is the mother of Usamah."
"22983","220 - Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Miqlas narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Umm Ayman, mother of Usamah ibn Zayd, was from Abyssinia; she was a servant girl of Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, and she used to nurse the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - when he was small. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - freed her, then married her to Zayd ibn Harithah, and she died five months after the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Thus said al-Zuhri; and it is narrated in this hadith that she lived after the death of Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"22984","221 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: Umm Ayman said on the day Umar was killed: Today Islam has weakened."
"22985","222 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab: that Umm Ayman, when she arrived from the land of Abyssinia, gestured with the nest."
"22986","223 - Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Miqlas narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, Bakr ibn Sawadah informed me, from Hifsh ibn Abd Allah, who narrated to him, from Umm Ayman, that she sifted flour and made it into a loaf for the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and he said: What is this? She said: Food we make here; I wanted to make for you a loaf from it. He said: Return it into it, then knead it."
"22987","What Umm Ayman narrated. 224 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Salih ibn Rustum Abu Amir al-Khazzaz narrated to us, from Abu Yazid al-Madani, he said: Umm Ayman said: The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Hand me the prayer mat from the mosque. She said: I am menstruating. He said: Your menstruation is not in your hand."
"22988","225 - Darran ibn Sufyan al-Qattan narrated to us, Abu Kamil al-Jahdari narrated to us, Mutahhar ibn Sawwar Abu Bishr narrated to us, from Abu Amir al-Khazzaz, from Abu Yazid al-Madini, from Umm Ayman, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said to me: Hand me the prayer mat. I said: I am menstruating. He said: Is it in your hand?"
"22989","226 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Musa ibn Qays al-Hadrami narrated to us, Iyad ibn Iyad narrated to us, he said: Umm Ayman wept over the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - so they said to her: Do you weep over the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - while he is of the people of Paradise? She said: I weep only over the news of heaven that used to come to us."
"22990","227 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: When the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - passed away, Umm Ayman, mother of Usamah ibn Zayd, wept. It was said to her: What makes you weep? She said: Revelation from heaven has been cut off from us."
"22991","228 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ata', from Ibn Umm Ayman, from Umm Ayman, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: The thief is cut only for a shield, and it was valued in the time of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - at a dinar or ten dirhams."
"22992","229 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Amr ibn Uthman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, Khalid ibn Muhammad of the family of al-Zubayr narrated to us, from his father, he said: Ali ibn Husayn ibn Ali narrated to me, Umm Ayman narrated to me, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: The black man is but his belly and his private part."
"22993","230 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Shabbabah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, Abu Malik al-Nakha'i narrated to me, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Nubayh al-Anazi, from Umm Ayman, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - rose at night to an earthenware vessel at the side of the house and urinated in it. I rose at night thirsty and drank what was in it without realizing. In the morning the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: O Umm Ayman, go and pour out what is in that earthen vessel. I said: By Allah, I have drunk what was in it. She said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - laughed until his molars appeared, then said: You shall never suffer stomach pain."
"22994","231 - Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi narrated to me, Safwan ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Namir, he said: al-Zuhri said: Harmalah, freedman of Usamah ibn Zayd, narrated to me: that while he was sitting with Abd Allah ibn Umar, al-Hajjaj ibn Ayman ibn Umm Ayman entered and prayed a prayer in which he did not complete its bowing or its prostration. Ibn Umar called him when he saluted and said: Nephew, do you think you have prayed? You have not prayed; repeat your prayer. When al-Hajjaj left, Abd Allah ibn Umar said to me: Who is this? I said: This is al-Hajjaj ibn Ayman ibn Umm Ayman. He said: Had the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - seen him, he would have loved him; and he mentioned his love for the offspring of Umm Ayman. She was the nurse of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"22995","232 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us, Amr ibn Salih narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Umm Ayman, that the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - married his daughter Fatimah to Ali ibn Abi Talib, and commanded him not to approach his wife until he comes to him; then he came to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and he mentioned the hadith."
"22996","Umm Ayyash, freedwoman of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and his servant. 233 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abd al-Wahid ibn Safwan, from his father, from Umm Ayyash, who was a servant of the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - he sent her with his daughter to Uthman ibn Affan."
"22997","234 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Kurdus ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Abd al-Karim ibn Rawh narrated to us, my father Rawh ibn Anbasah narrated to me, from his father Anbasah ibn Sa'id, from his grandmother, his father's mother Umm Ayyash, who was a slave girl of Ruqayyah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - she said: I used to perform ablution for the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - while I was standing and he was sitting."
"22998","235 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Kurdus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abd al-Karim ibn Rawh narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his father, from his grandmother Umm Ayyash, she said: Ruqayyah bore Uthman a boy, so the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - named him Abd Allah, and gave Uthman the kunya Abu Abd Allah."
"22999","236 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Kurdus narrated to us, Abd al-Karim ibn Rawh narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his father, from his grandmother Umm Ayyash, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: I married Uthman to Umm Kulthum only by revelation from heaven."
"23000","237 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, Kurdus narrated to us, Abd al-Karim ibn Rawh narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his father, from his grandmother Umm Ayyash, she said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - dye his hair until he died."
"23001","Umm Habib bint al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib. 238 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Mukram al-Dabbi narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Husayn ibn Abd Allah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - looked at Umm Habibah bint al-Abbas crawling before him and said - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: If she lives while I am alive, I shall marry her. He died before she grew up, so al-Aswad ibn Abd al-Asad married her, and she bore him Rizq ibn al-Aswad and Lubabah bint al-Aswad; she named her after her mother Umm al-Fadl."
"23002","Umm Habib, freedwoman of Umm Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah. 239 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Umm Habib, freedwoman of Umm Atiyyah, she said: I was among the women who conducted the daughter of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - so he said: Pour, when you pour, upon her head three times in the bath for major impurity."
"23003","Umm Khalid bint Khalid ibn Sa'id ibn al-As. 240 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us (ha). And Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, they said: Ishaq ibn Sa'id ibn Amr ibn Sa'id ibn al-As narrated to us, from his father, from Amr, from Umm Khalid bint Khalid, that the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - was brought garments among which was a small black cloak with marks, and he said: Whom do you think we should clothe with this? The people fell silent, so he said: Bring me Umm Khalid. I was brought, carried, so he took the cloak with his hand and clothed me with it, saying: Wear out and replace, wear out and replace. She said: In it was a green or yellow mark, and he said: O Umm Khalid, this is sanah; sanah in Abyssinian means: good."
"23004","241 - Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Mustamli narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Sa'id al-Umawi, from Umm Khalid bint Khalid, she said: The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - clothed me with a marked cloak, stroking it and saying: Sanihi, sanihi, which in Abyssinian means: good."
"23005","242 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Umm Khalid bint Khalid ibn Sa'id ibn al-As, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - seeking refuge from the punishment of the grave."
"23006","243 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, he said: I heard Umm Khalid bint Khalid saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: Seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave, for the punishment of the grave is true. Musa ibn Uqbah said: I never heard anyone saying: I heard the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - except Umm Khalid."
"23007","244 - Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Hulwani narrated to us, Mus'ab ibn Abd Allah al-Zubayri narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Umm Khalid bint Khalid, that she heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - seeking refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave."
"23008","245 - Mas'adah ibn Sa'd al-Attar and Abd Allah ibn al-Saqr al-Sukkari narrated to us, they said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, Bakkar ibn Harast narrated to us, Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Umm Khalid bint Khalid narrated to me, she said: I came to the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and looked at the seal of prophethood between his shoulders."
"23009","246 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Salim al-Razi narrated to us, Sahl ibn Uthman narrated to us, Junadah ibn Salim narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Umm Khalid bint Khalid ibn Sa'id ibn al-As al-Akbar, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - seeking refuge from the punishment of the grave."
"23010","Umm Khalid bint al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth. 247 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Hafs narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Umm Khalid bint al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth, that she entered upon the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and he said: Who is this? They said: Umm Khalid bint al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth. He said: Praise be to Allah who brings forth the living from the dead, meaning: the believer from the disbeliever."
"23011","248 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, he said: The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon Umm Khalid and said: Who is this? They said: One of your maternal aunts. He said: My maternal aunts in this land are strangers; who is this? They said: Umm Khalid bint al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth. He said: Glory be to Him who brings forth the living from the dead."
"23012","Umm Sharik al-Qurashiyyah al-Amiriyyah. 249 - Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-San'ani narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abd al-Karim narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Aqil ibn Ma'qil narrated to us, from his father, from Wahb ibn Munabbih, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from Umm Sharik, that she heard the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: People shall flee from the Dajjal into the mountains. Umm Sharik said: O Messenger of Allah, where shall the Arabs be that day? He said: They are few."
"23013","250 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr ibn Shaybah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Umm Sharik, that the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - commanded her to kill geckos."
"23014","251 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from Abu Idris, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Umm Sharik, that the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - commanded killing geckos."
"23015","252 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Judhu'i the judge narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Mukram al-Ammi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musa narrated to us, Hammad ibn Bashir narrated to us, Abu Abd Allah al-Shami narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Umm Sharik, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - used to recite the Opening of the Book over funerals."
"23016","Umm Uthman bint Sufyan, mother of the child of Shaybah ibn Uthman al-Hajabiyyah. 253 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Hisham al-Dastuwa'i narrated to us, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from the mother of the child of Shaybah, that she saw the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - hastening between al-Safa and al-Marwah, saying: The valley is crossed only running."
"23017","254 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Isa al-Marwazi narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, from Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother, from Umm Uthman bint Sufyan, mother of the child of Shaybah, who had pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - that the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - called Shaybah, who opened the House; when he entered, he bowed and turned aside."
"23018","Umm Sa'd bint Amr al-Jumahiyyah, and it is said Umm Sa'id. 255 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Harun ibn Ishaq al-Hamdani narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Umm Sa'd bint Amr al-Jumahiyyah, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: Whoever sponsors an orphan of his own or of others among people, I and he shall be in Paradise like these two."
"23019","256 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, Safwan ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Umm Sa'id bint Amr ibn Murrah al-Jumahi, from the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - the like of it. And Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated it from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Umm Sa'id bint Murrah al-Fihriyyah, from her father."
"23020","Umm al-Mundhir al-Ansariyyah, it is said her name is Salma bint Qays. 257 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr saying: Umm al-Mundhir who narrated from the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - her name is Salma bint Qays. Ibn Numayr said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated that to us, from Ibn Ishaq: And she prayed facing both qiblas with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"23021","258 - Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Awfi narrated to us (ha). And Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Mu'addib narrated to us, Surayj ibn al-Nu'man al-Jawhari narrated to us, they said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah, from Ya'qub ibn Abi Ya'qub, from Umm al-Mundhir, she said: The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon me while Ali was with him, convalescing. She said: We had hanging clusters of dates. She said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - stood eating, and Ali stood eating, so the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Slowly, for you are convalescing. She said: Ali sat down, and the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - ate from them. Then I prepared for them chard and barley, so the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Take from this."
"23022","Umm Ata', freedwoman of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam. 259 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Abd Allah ibn Ata' ibn Ibrahim, freedman of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, narrated to me, from his mother and his grandmother Umm Ata', they said: By Allah, it is as if we look at al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam when he came to us upon a white mule of his and said: O Umm Ata', the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - has forbidden Muslims to eat of their sacrifices beyond three days, so do not eat it. I said: May my father be your ransom, what do we do with what was gifted to us? He said: As for what was gifted to you, do with it as you wish."
"23023","Umm Mubashshir al-Ansariyyah. 260 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon Umm Mubashshir among palm trees and said: Who planted these palms, a Muslim or a disbeliever? She said: Rather a Muslim. He said: No Muslim plants a plant or sows a crop from which a beast, bird or human eats except that it is charity for him."
"23024","261 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Mu'alla ibn Asad al-Ammi narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umm Mubashshir, she said: The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon me in a garden and said: Who planted this, a Muslim or a disbeliever? I said: A Muslim. He said: No Muslim plants a plant from which a human, beast or bird eats except that it is charity for him."
"23025","262 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, Shayban narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umm Mubashshir, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon me while I was among palms and said: Did a Muslim plant this or a disbeliever? I said: No, rather a Muslim. He said: No Muslim plants a plant or sows a crop from which a bird, beast, animal or human eats except that it is charity for him."
"23026","263 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umm Mubashshir, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon me while I was among my palms and said: Who planted these palms, a Muslim or a disbeliever? I said: No, rather a Muslim. He said: No Muslim plants a plant from which a bird, human, beast or animal eats except that it is charity for him."
"23027","264 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, my father narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: Umm Mubashshir, wife of Zayd ibn Harithah, narrated to me, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon me while I was among my palms and said: Is this yours? I said: Yes. He said: Who planted it, a Muslim or a disbeliever? I said: A Muslim. He said: No Muslim plants a plant or sows a crop from which a bird, human, beast or anything eats except that it is charity for him."
"23028","Chapter. 265 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umm Mubashshir (ha). And Zakariyya al-Saji narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umm Mubashshir, she said: The boy of Hatib came to the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and said: By Allah, Hatib shall never enter Paradise. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: You have lied; he witnessed Badr and al-Hudaybiyah."
"23029","266 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umm Mubashshir, she said: I heard the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - in the house of Hafsah saying: No man who witnessed Badr and al-Hudaybiyah shall enter the Fire. Hafsah said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah says: There is none of you but shall come to it; it is with your Lord an ordained decree. He said: Then what? She said: Then We shall save those who feared Allah and leave the wrongdoers therein on their knees."
"23030","Chapter. 267 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umm Mubashshir: that the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - proposed to the daughter of al-Bara' ibn Ma'rur, and she said: I stipulated to my husband that I shall not marry after him. He said: That is not proper."
"23031","Chapter. 268 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umm Mubashshir, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered upon me while I was in a garden of Banu al-Najjar in which were graves of those who had died in ignorance; he left, and I heard him saying: Seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave. I said: O Messenger of Allah, is there punishment in the grave? He said: Yes; they are punished in their graves with a punishment heard by beasts."
"23032","269 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: Umm Mubashshir narrated to me that she heard the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying near Hafsah: None of the companions of the tree who pledged beneath it shall enter the Fire, if Allah wills. She said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He rebuked her, so Hafsah said: There is none of you but shall come to it. The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Then We shall save those who feared Allah and leave the wrongdoers therein on their knees."
"23033","270 - Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Umm Mubashshir, that the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said to her: O Umm Mubashshir, whoever has three forerunners among her children, Allah admits her to Paradise by His mercy upon them. Umm Mubashshir was cooking a dish and said: Or two forerunners? He said: Or two forerunners."
"23034","271 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Iqil al-Harrani narrated to us, Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Umm Mubashshir, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Shall I inform you of the best of people as a man? They said: Yes. He pointed with his hand toward the east and said: A man holding the bridle of his horse in the path of Allah, waiting to raid or be raided upon. Shall I inform you of the best of people after him as a man? They said: Yes. He pointed with his hand toward al-Hijaz and said: A man among his sheep, establishing prayer and giving zakat, knowing what is Allah's due in his wealth, who has withdrawn from people."
"23035","Umm Bishr bint al-Bara' ibn Ma'rur. 272 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Harith ibn Fudayl, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, he said: When death came to Ka'b, Umm Bishr bint al-Bara' ibn Ma'rur entered upon him and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, if you meet my son, convey my greeting to him. He said: May Allah forgive you, O Umm Bishr; we are lower than that. She said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, did you not hear the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saying: The souls of the believers are in the bellies of green birds, feeding upon the trees of Paradise? He said: Yes. She said: So it is that."
"23036","Umm Sulaym bint Milhan al-Ansariyyah, mother of Anas ibn Malik; from her reports. 273 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ja'far ibn Sulayman informed us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas, he said: Abu Talhah proposed to Umm Sulaym before he became Muslim, and she said: I am indeed desirous of you, and one like you is not refused, but you are a disbelieving man and I am a Muslim woman; if you become Muslim, that is my dowry, I ask you for nothing else. So he became Muslim, and Abu Talhah married her."
"23037","274 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musa al-Makhzumi al-Fitri narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Talhah married Umm Sulaym, and the dowry between them was Islam; Umm Sulaym became Muslim before Abu Talhah, so he proposed to her and she said: I have become Muslim; if you become Muslim, I shall marry you. So he became Muslim, and the dowry between them was Islam."
"23038","Account of the loaf of Umm Sulaym and her butter-skin. 275 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me (ha). And Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'awiyah al-Utbi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from al-Qurazi, meaning Muhammad ibn Ka'b, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Talhah came to Umm Sulaym, mother of Anas, Abu Talhah being his stepfather, and said: Do you have anything, O Umm Sulaym? For I passed by the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - while he was teaching the companions of the bench the chapter of Women, having tied a stone upon his belly from hunger. She said: I had some barley which I ground, then she sent me to the markets, the markets being gardens of theirs, and I brought them some firewood, and she made from it a loaf. Then he said: Do you have condiment? She said: I had a skin with ghee in it; I do not know if anything remains in it. She brought it to him and he squeezed it, saying: The squeezing of two is more effective than the squeezing of one. They squeezed it together and brought out about a date's worth, and she greased the loaf. Then he called me and said: O Anas, do you know the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? I said: Yes. He said: I left him with his companions at the bench teaching them; call him and do not call anyone else with him; see that you do not disgrace me. I came to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and when he saw me he said: Perhaps your father sent you to us? I said: Yes. Then he said to the people: Go. They went, eighty men that day. He held my hand; when I neared the house I pulled his hand from my hand. Abu Talhah began seeking me in the house, pelting me with stones and saying: You have disgraced me before the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Then he went out to him and told him the news. He said: It shall not harm you. He commanded them and they sat, then he entered, and we brought him the loaf. He said: Is there condiment? Umm Sulaym said: O Messenger of Allah, we had a skin which I and Abu Talhah squeezed. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Bring it, for the squeezing of three is more effective than the squeezing of two. It was brought, and the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - squeezed it with them, and they brought out about a date's worth, and they greased the loaf with it. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - wiped it with his hand, then invoked blessing upon it, then said: Call ten for me. I called ten, and they ate from it until filled to satiety. They kept entering ten by ten until all were full. Then the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - sat, and we sat with him and ate until there remained extra."
"23039","276 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas informed us, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, that he heard Anas ibn Malik saying: Abu Talhah said to Umm Sulaym: I have heard the speech of the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - recognizing hunger in it; do you have anything? She said: Yes. She brought out loaves of barley, then brought out a veil of theirs and wrapped the bread partly in part, then sent me to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I went and found the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - in the mosque with people. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said to me: Did Abu Talhah send you? I said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said to those with him: Rise. He went, and I went ahead of them until I came to Abu Talhah and told him. Abu Talhah said: O Umm Sulaym, the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - has come with people, and we do not have food to fill them. She said: Allah and His Messenger know best. Abu Talhah went until we reached the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - then the Messenger of Allah and Abu Talhah came until they entered. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Bring, O Umm Sulaym, what you have. She brought that bread; the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - commanded it be broken up, and Umm Sulaym squeezed a butter-skin and seasoned it with it. Then the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said over it what Allah willed him to say, then said: Permit ten. He permitted them and they ate until full, then left. Then he said: Permit ten. He permitted them and they ate until full, then left. Then he said: Permit ten. He permitted them and they ate until full, then left. Then he said: Permit ten. He permitted them and they ate until full, then left. Then he said: Permit ten. All the people ate until full, the people being seventy or eighty."
"23040","277 - Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-San'ani narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us (ha). And Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Musa al-Fitri narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Talhah said: O Umm Sulaym, prepare something for the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. She ground some barley and prepared it, then Abu Talhah called me and said: Go to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and say: My father invites you, and tell him privately. Anas said: I came while the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - was sitting in the mosque; when he saw me he said: O Anas. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Did your father invite me? I said: Yes. He said: Rise. Then he said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - did not pass a gathering without saying: Rise. I went out quickly until I came to Abu Talhah and said: This is the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - who has come, bringing people. Abu Talhah said: Did I not command you to be alone with him? I said: When he asked me, did your father invite me? I said: Yes; I did not lie to him. Abu Talhah met the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - at the door of the house and said: O Messenger of Allah, it was only something we wished to single you out with. He said: Enter. He and the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered. He said: Umm Sulaym was with a butter-skin, squeezing it, and it had dried or nearly dried. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: O Umm Sulaym, hand it to me so I may squeeze it better than you. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - took it with his hand and did thus with the ghee, then the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - placed his hand upon the top of the tharid, then said: O Abu Talhah, admit ten. Ten entered upon him and ate until full, and he kept admitting ten by ten, and they ate until filled, until between seventy and eighty entered upon him. Then the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and the household ate, and they gave extra to their neighbors."
"23041","278 - Isma'il ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that Amr ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah narrated to him that he heard Anas ibn Malik saying: I came to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - one day and found him sitting with his companions, speaking to them, having bound his belly upon a stone. I said to one of his companions: Why has the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - bound his belly? He said: From hunger. I went to Abu Talhah, husband of Umm Sulaym bint Milhan, and said: Father, I have seen the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - binding his belly with a band; I asked one of his companions and he said: From hunger. Abu Talhah entered upon my mother and said: Is there anything? She said: I have pieces of bread and dates; if the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - comes alone, we shall fill him, and if one comes with him it shall be too little for them. Abu Talhah said: Go, O Anas, stand near the Messenger of Allah; when he rises, call him after his companions and those with him disperse, until when he stands at his doorstep, say: My father invites you. I did that; when I said: My father invites you, he said to his companions: Come. Then he took my hand, gripped it, and came with his companions until when they neared our house he released my hand. I entered, grieved at the multitude who came with him, and said: Father, I said to the Messenger of Allah what you told me, yet he called his companions and has come to you with them. Abu Talhah went out to them and said: O Messenger of Allah, I only sent Anas to invite you alone, and I do not have what fills whom I see. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Enter, for Allah - mighty and majestic - shall fill them with what you have. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - entered with me and said: Gather what you have, then bring it near. Those with him sat at the bench, and I brought near what we had of bread and dates, and we placed it upon our mat. He invoked blessing in it, then said: Admit eight upon me. I admitted eight upon him, placing his palm over the food, saying: Eat and mention Allah. They ate from between his fingers until full. Then he commanded me and I admitted eight, and the first stood; I did so, and they entered and ate until full. Then he commanded me and I admitted eight; he kept thus until eighty men entered, all eating until full. Then he called me and called my mother and Abu Talhah and said: Eat. We ate until full. Then he raised his hand and said: O Umm Sulaym, how is this compared to your food when you presented it? She said: May my father and mother be your ransom; had I not seen them eating, I would have said: Nothing of our food has decreased."
"23042","279 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us (ha). And Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Yahya ibn Umarah, from his father, from Anas ibn Malik - Umm Sulaym bint Milhan being under Abu Talhah - she prepared khazir, then Abu Talhah said to me: Go, my son, and call the Messenger of Allah. I went and called him, coming to him while he was among people, and said: My father invites you. He said to the people: Go. When I saw him saying to the people, I went ahead of them until I came to Abu Talhah and said: Father, this is the Messenger of Allah with people. Abu Talhah stood at the door until the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - came, and said: O Messenger of Allah, it was only a small thing. He said: Bring it, for Allah shall place blessing in it. It was brought, and the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - placed his hands in it and invoked over it until eighty men ate from it."
"23043","280 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami and Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, they said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani and Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, that Abu Talhah, when he saw the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - hungry, came to Umm Sulaym and said: I have seen the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - hungry; do you have anything? She said: We have about two mudds of barley flour. He said: Knead it and cook it; perhaps we may call the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - to eat from it. She kneaded it and baked it, and it came as a loaf. He said to me: Call the Messenger of Allah. I came to the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - with people with him, I think he said: some eighty-odd. I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Talhah invites you. He said to his companions: Answer Abu Talhah. I came hastening until I told him he had come, he and his companions. He said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah knows better what is in my house than I. Abu Talhah received him and said: By Allah, we have nothing but a loaf; I saw you hungry, so I commanded Umm Sulaym and she made for you a loaf. He called for the loaf and called for a bowl, placed it in it and said: Is there ghee? Abu Talhah said: There was something in the butter-skin. He said: It was brought, and the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and Abu Talhah squeezed it until something came out. The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - wiped his forefinger with it, then wiped the loaf, then said: In the name of Allah. The loaf swelled, and he kept doing that while the loaf swelled until I saw the loaf in the bowl liquefying. He said: Call ten of my companions for me. I called ten for him; the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - placed the loaf and said: Eat in the name of Allah. They ate from around the loaf until full. He kept calling ten by ten, they eating that loaf, until some eighty-odd men ate from it, from around the loaf, until full. He said: The middle of the loaf where the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - placed his hand remained as it was."
"23044","281 - Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Khashshab al-Raqqi narrated to us, Amr ibn Qusayr narrated to us (ha). And Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Hafs al-Nufayli narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Amr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Talhah commanded Umm Sulaym, saying: Prepare for the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - food for himself alone to eat from. Then Abu Talhah sent me to the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - so I came to him and said: Abu Talhah sent me to you. He said to the people: Rise. Abu Talhah met us and said: O Prophet of Allah, I prepared food for you alone. He said: Never mind; go with us. The people went with him. He brought the food, made only for the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - alone. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - placed his hand and mentioned Allah over it, then said: Permit ten. He permitted them and they entered, saying: Eat in the name of Allah. They ate until full, then stood. The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - placed his hand as he did the first time and mentioned Allah, then said: Permit ten. He said: Until he did that with eighty men. Then the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and the household ate, leaving remainder."
"23045","282 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Jandal ibn Waliq al-Taghlibi narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Talhah commanded Umm Sulaym, saying: Prepare for the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - food for himself alone to eat from. Then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Amr ibn Qusayr and Sa'id ibn Hafs, exactly."
"23046","283 - Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Ayyub al-Mukharrimi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Imran ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to me: that Abu Talhah, his uncle and husband of his mother, brought two mudds of barley and commanded them be prepared. Then he said to me: Go and call the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - for us. I called him, and he said to the people: Rise. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - came, and I came ahead of him until I entered upon Abu Talhah. He said: What did you do? I said: I have called the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and he said to the people: Rise. He said: Did you not know what we have? I said: Yes, but I could not say anything to the Messenger of Allah. When the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - reached the door, he called ten by ten, speaking what Allah willed. He said to the people: Eat. They ate until full, then left, and he called another ten, until eighty men ate from it, and there remained what filled the household."
"23047","284 - Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, that Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman informed him that he heard Anas ibn Malik saying: Abu Talhah came one day to find the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - standing, teaching the companions of the bench, with a young camel's hump of stone upon his belly, holding his belly up from hunger. He returned to Umm Sulaym and said: I have seen in the Messenger of Allah what grieved me; do you have anything? She said: Yes, some barley. He said to me: Prepare it. She prepared it, then said to me: Go and call the Prophet of Allah, and let no one know of you. Anas said: When the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - saw me he said: Did Abu Talhah send you? I said: Yes. He said: Rise. Seventy rose with him. I came to Abu Talhah and told him. He said: O Prophet of Allah, it is only something we prepared for you; by Allah, we do not have what fills them. He said: Bring what you have. I brought it to him; Umm Sulaym had a butter-skin of ghee, and she squeezed it to season the food with it. Then he placed his hand, then said: Come ten by ten. They ate, none sparing another, until filled, and there remained extra, until Umm Sulaym gifted to her neighbors."
"23048","285 - Sahl ibn Musa Shiran al-Ramahurmuzi narrated to us, Isa ibn Shadhan narrated to us, al-Salt ibn Muhammad al-Kharaki Abu Hammam narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Ja'd Abu Uthman, from Anas ibn Malik, and Hisham, from Muhammad, from Anas ibn Malik - and Sinan Abu Rabi'ah mentioned it, from Anas ibn Malik - that his mother Umm Sulaym took two mudds of barley, crushed it and made from it a porridge, and squeezed a butter-skin she had. Then she sent me to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - so I came to him while he was among his companions and called him. He said: And who is with me? I came to my mother and said: He says: And who is with me? The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - came with his companions. Abu Talhah went out to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, it is only something Umm Sulaym prepared for you. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - smiled, entered, and it was brought. He said: Admit ten upon me. They entered and ate until full. Then he said: Admit ten, until he counted forty. Then the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - ate, then stood, and I kept looking whether anything had decreased from it."
"23049","286 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sulayman, Luwayn, narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Hisham, from Muhammad, from Anas, he said: Umm Sulaym prepared a porridge from barley she had, crushing it. He said: She sent me to him to call him. I came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, Umm Sulaym has prepared for you a porridge of barley. He said: I and who is with me? He said: I returned to her and told her. Abu Talhah came and said: O Messenger of Allah, it is only a mudd and less than a mudd which Umm Sulaym has prepared for you. He said: I and who is with me? The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and those with him rose, entered the house, and said to me: O Anas, admit ten upon me. Ten entered and ate until full. Then he said: Admit ten. Ten entered and ate until full. Then he said: Admit ten upon me. I said to Anas: How many were they? He said: Thirty or forty. They ate until full, then the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - ate, and we ate until full. He said: As if nothing had decreased from it. And in the hadith of Abu Talhah: some barley which Umm Sulaym prepared, squeezing upon it a butter-skin in which was ghee."
"23050","Account of Umm Sulaym regarding the death of her son by Abu Talhah. 287 - Muhammad ibn Harun narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musa al-Makhzumi al-Fitri narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Umm Sulaym bore a boy; he fell ill, his illness intensified, then he died, while Abu Talhah was with the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Abu Talhah returned from him after praying Maghrib, Umm Sulaym having wrapped him and placed him in a corner of her house. Abu Talhah inclined toward him, so she said: I adjure you by my right not to approach him, for he has not been better since he fell ill than tonight. She brought near to him his breakfast meal and he broke fast, then she took perfume and applied it, then drew near to Abu Talhah and he had her. She said: O Abu Talhah, if you saw neighbors lending neighbors of theirs a loan until they thought they had left it to them, then when they sought it from them they resented it, he said: Evil is what they did. She said: Allah had lent you so-and-so, then took him from you, and He is more entitled to him. In the morning he went to the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and told him the news. He said: O Allah, bless them in their night. She conceived Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, giving birth to him at the end of the night. She said: Do not rouse him until morning. In the morning she washed him and wrapped him, then called Anas and said: My son, take your brother to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Anas said: I came carrying him while the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - was standing in a waist-wrapper with a branding tool with him. He said: O Anas, what is this with you? I said: My brother, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Hand him to me. I handed him to him; he called for dates, chewed them, then rubbed the palate with them, and the child smacked his lips. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - smiled and said: The love of the Ansar is dates."
"23051","288 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Talhah had a son by Umm Sulaym who died. She said to her family: Do not tell Abu Talhah of my son's death until I am the one who tells him. He said: He came, and she brought near to him his supper and drink; he ate and drank, then she adorned herself for him better than she used to adorn herself before. When he was full and quenched and had his need of her, she knew he was full and quenched and had fulfilled his need of her, and said: O Abu Talhah, what if a household lent their loan to another household, then sought their loan; do they have the right to withhold their loan? He said: No. She said: Then seek reward for your son. He said: She said: He angered, then said: You left me until I soiled myself with what I soiled myself, then you tell me of my son's death?! He went to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and said: O Messenger of Allah, did you not see what Umm Sulaym did, such and such? The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: May Allah bless you both in the remainder of your night. He said: She conceived that night; she carried. He said: She and Abu Talhah were with the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - and when they neared Medina, labor seized her, and Abu Talhah was detained over her, while the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - went on. He said: Abu Talhah says: O Lord, You know it pleased me to go out with the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - when he went out, and enter with him when he entered; she is detained with what You see. He said: Abu Talhah says: Umm Sulaym says: O Abu Talhah, I do not feel what I felt; go with the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: He went until he arrived with the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - then labor struck her and she bore a boy. My mother said to me: O Anas, let him taste nothing until you take him in the morning to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: I spent that night watching over him, bent over him while he wept until morning. In the morning I took him to the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - finding a branding iron in his hand. When he saw the child with me, the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: Perhaps Umm Sulaym has given birth? I said: Yes. He said: He placed the branding iron down and sat. I brought him the child and placed him in his lap. He called for an Ajwah date of Medina dates, chewed it in his mouth until it dissolved, then spat it into the child's mouth, and he smacked his lips. The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - said: See the love of the Ansar for dates. Then he wiped his face and named him Abd Allah."
"23052","Account of Umm Sulaym regarding the sweat of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. 289 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - came and took midday rest with us and sweated. Umm Sulaym came with a bottle and began collecting the sweat into it. The Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - woke and said: O Umm Sulaym, what are you doing? She said: This is your sweat; we put it in our perfume, and it is the best of perfume."
"23053","290 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Hashim ibn al-Harith al-Harrani narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Sulaym, she said: The Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - used to take midday rest with me; I would spread for him a leather mat, and he would rest upon it and sweat. I used to knead sukk perfume with his sweat. Muhammad ibn Sirin said: I asked Umm Sulaym for some of that sukk, and she gave me some of it. When Muhammad died, he was embalmed with that sukk; Muhammad liked that the dead be embalmed with sukk."
"23054","Account of Umm Sulaym in battle with the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. 291 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us (ha). And al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Thabit al-Bunani informed us, from Anas ibn Malik, that Umm Sulaym was with Abu Talhah al-Ansari on the day of Hunayn, with a dagger with her. Abu Talhah said to her: What is this with you, O Umm Sulaym? She said: A dagger I have taken; if one of the polytheists nears me, I shall rip open his belly with it. Abu Talhah said: O Messenger of Allah, do you not hear what Umm Sulaym says? She says such and such. I said: O Messenger of Allah, turn to those after us of the freed ones; they have fled with you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: O Umm Sulaym, Allah has sufficed and done good."
"23055","Account of Umm Sulaym when the Messenger of Allah - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - visited her and prayed in her house. 292 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Umawi narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, that Umm Sulaym asked the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - to pray in her house so they may take it as a prayer place. He came to her, to a mat of hers or theirs, and she sprinkled it with water; he prayed upon it, and they prayed with him."
"23056","What Umm Sulaym transmitted. 293 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal: narrated to us Shayban ibn Farrukh: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Burjumi: narrated to us Abu Zilal, from Anas ibn Malik, from his mother, she said: I had a sheep, and I collected its ghee into a skin bag, and it was sent with Zaynab, and I said: O Zaynab, deliver this skin bag to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for him to use it as condiment. He said: So Zaynab came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is ghee sent to you by Umm Sulaym. He said: Empty her skin bag for her. So the skin bag was emptied and handed back to her. Then she came while Umm Sulaym was not in the house, so she hung the skin bag on a peg. Then Umm Sulaym came and saw the skin bag full and dripping ghee, so she said: O Zaynab, did I not order you to deliver this skin bag to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for him to use it as condiment? She said: I have done so; and if you do not believe me, come with me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Umm Sulaym went, with Zaynab along with her, to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she said: I sent to you with her a skin bag containing ghee. He said: She has indeed brought it. I said: By the One who sent you with guidance and the true religion, it is full of ghee, dripping it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you wonder, O Umm Sulaym, that Allah has fed you."
"23057","294 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Uqbah al-Shaybani: narrated to us al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Hulwani: narrated to us Hammad ibn Mas'adah, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Anas, from Umm Sulaym: that a driver used to drive her along with some women accompanying the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), called Anjashah, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Gently, O Anjashah, such is your driving with the glass vessels."
"23058","295 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi: narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us al-Hajjaj ibn Farrukh al-Tamimi al-Wasiti: narrated to us Abu 'Ammar, from Anas ibn Malik, from Umm Sulaym, she said: I was in one of the rooms of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was with her, when a man came to him and complained to him of need. He said: Be patient, for by Allah, there has been nothing in the household of Muhammad for seven days, and no fire has been lit under a cooking pot of theirs for three days. By Allah, if I were to ask Allah to turn all the mountains of Tihamah into gold, He would do so."
"23059","296 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to me Harami ibn Hafs al-Qasmali and Muhammad ibn Abi Nu'aym al-Wasiti: narrated to us Wuhayb ibn Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, from Umm Sulaym: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray on a khumrah (small straw mat)."
"23060","297 - Narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Hamdawayh al-Saffar: narrated to us 'Affan: narrated to us Wuhayb, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, from Umm Sulaym: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to her and take his midday rest at her house, so she would spread for him a leather mat and he would rest on it. He used to sweat profusely, so she would collect his sweat and put it into perfume and flasks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is this? She said: It is your sweat; we put it into perfume. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray on a khumrah."
"23061","298 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi, from Ayyub, from Anas ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, from Umm Sulaym: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray in her house on a khumrah."
"23062","299 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid Yusuf ibn Yazid al-Qaratis: narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn Talib: narrated to us 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id, from Abu al-Tayyah, from Anas ibn Malik, from Umm Sulaym: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray on a khumrah."
"23063","300 - Narrated to us Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Salih: narrated to me al-Layth, from Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah al-Ansari, from Anas ibn Malik, from Umm Sulaym: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited them one day and supplicated for me so abundantly that I would not mind if he added more. I said to him: I have among my family one who holds a special place with me, and I would love for you to supplicate for her with the special regard she holds with me. He said: And who is he? I said: Anas, your servant; may my father and mother be sacrificed for you. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated so abundantly that I would not mind if he did not add more. Among what he supplicated was that he said: And grant him wealth and children. Anas said: I know no one who attained of comfortable easy living like what I attained, and with these two hands of mine I have buried a hundred of my children, not counting miscarriages nor grandchildren."
"23064","301 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti: narrated to us Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi, from Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas, from his mother, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"23065","302 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us al-Salt ibn Mas'ud al-Jahdari: narrated to us Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Thabit, from Anas, from Umm Sulaym, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to take us campaigning with him, womenfolk of the Ansar, and we would give drink to the sick and treat the wounded."
"23066","303 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us Yahya ibn Ma'in: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Anas, from Umm Sulaym: that she said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Your servant Anas, supplicate to Allah for him. He said: O Allah, increase his wealth and his children, and bless for him in what You have given him."
"23067","304 - Narrated to us al-Hasan ibn al-'Abbas al-Razi: narrated to us Sahl ibn 'Uthman: narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman, from Junayd ibn Abi Wahr? al-Taymi, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Sulaym, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ... And narrated to us Abu Zur'ah 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr al-Dimashqi: narrated to us Adam: narrated to us Shayban ... And narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari: narrated to us 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab: narrated to us Shayban, from Layth, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm Sulaym, mother of Anas, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a woman dies and they intend to wash her, let them begin with her belly; let them wipe her belly with a gentle wiping if she is not pregnant, and if she is pregnant then do not move her. If you intend to wash her, begin with her lower private parts: place over her private area a covering cloth, then take a piece of cotton and wash it and wash well, then insert your hand under the cloth and wipe it with cotton three times, wiping well, before you perform ablution for her. Then perform ablution for her with water containing lote leaves, and let a woman pour the water while standing, attending to nothing else, until she cleans with the lote while you wash. Let her washing be undertaken by the closest of the women to her, otherwise a pious woman; and if she is small or weak, let a pious Muslim woman undertake it. When she finishes washing her lower part with a thorough washing with water and lote, let her perform for her the ablution of prayer; this is the explanation of her ablution. Then wash her after that three times with water and lote, beginning with her head before everything, cleaning its washing of the lote with water, and do not comb her head with a comb. If anything issues from her after the three washings, make it five; if something issues on the fifth, make it seven, and all of that in odd numbers with water and lote. If it is the fifth or the third, put in it some camphor and some lote. Then put that in a new jar. Then seat her and pour over her, beginning with her head until you reach her feet. When you finish with her, cast over her a clean cloth, then insert your hand from behind the cloth and remove it from her. Then stuff her lower part with cotton as much as you can, and stuff her cotton with her perfume. Then take a long washed strip of cloth and tie it over her buttocks as one ties over a waist-wrapper, then knot it between her thighs and draw her thighs together, then cast the end of the strip from her buttocks to near her knees; this is the matter of her lower part. Then perfume her and shroud her, and braid her hair into three braids: a forelock and two plaits, and do not liken her to men. Let her shroud be in five garments, one of them a waist-wrapper with which you wrap her thighs. Do not remove any of her hair with depilatory nor anything else, and whatever falls of her hair, wash it then insert it into the hair of her head, and perfume the hair of her head, perfuming it well. Do not wash her with heated water. Veil her, and what you shroud her with, with seven sprinklings if you wish, and make everything of it odd-numbered. If it occurs to you to veil her on her bier, make it odd-numbered; this is the matter of her shroud and her head. If she is menstruating or in postnatal bleeding or the like, take a single rag, dip it in water, and trace thereby everything of her, and do not move her, for I fear that something may escape from her that cannot be put back."
"23068","305 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Uthman ... And narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari: narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani, both said: narrated to us 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad, from 'Uthman ibn Hakim, from 'Amr ibn 'Umar al-Ansari, he said: I heard Umm Sulaym say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There are no two Muslims for whom three children die before reaching puberty except that Allah admits them into Paradise by the bounty of His mercy toward them. I said: And two? He said: And two."
"23069","306 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Numayr, from 'Uthman ibn Hakim: narrated to me 'Amr al-Ansari, from Umm Sulaym bint Milhan, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There are no two Muslims for whom three of their children die before reaching puberty except that Allah admits them into Paradise by the bounty of His mercy toward them."
"23070","307 - Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Ma'mari: narrated to us 'Amr ibn Abi 'Asim: narrated to us my father: narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, from 'Abd al-Karim: that al-Bara' ibn Zayd, son of the daughter of Anas, informed him, from Umm Sulaym: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her while she had a hanging water-skin, so he stood to it and drank from its mouth. Then she stood to the mouth of the water-skin and cut it off."
"23071","308 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal: narrated to us al-Salt ibn Mas'ud al-Jahdari: narrated to us Sulaym ibn Akhdar, from Ibn 'Awn, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf, from Umm Sulaym, she said: I brought near to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a roasted shoulder, and he ate of it, then stood for prayer without performing ablution."
"23072","309 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti: narrated to us my father: narrated to us Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abdullah, from Hakim ibn Hakim ibn 'Abbad ibn Hunayf, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from Umm Sulaym, mother of Anas ibn Malik: that she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in the house of Umm Salamah with a group sitting with him. She sat until they left, then said: O Messenger of Allah, I shall never leave any matter that gives me understanding in my religion and draws me near to Allah without asking about it. Tell me, O Messenger of Allah: a woman who sees in a dream what a man sees, should she wash? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If she sees fluid, let her wash. Umm Salamah said: May your hands be rubbed with dust, O Umm Sulaym, you have exposed women. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather, may your own hands be rubbed with dust; were it not for that, how would the child resemble its mother?"
"23073","310 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Khallal al-Makki: narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad Abu 'Alqamah al-Farawi, from Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm: Umm Sulaym informed me, she said: I entered upon 'A'ishah and said: Where is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: In the house, revelation is coming to him. Then I remained as long as Allah willed that I remain, then I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: O 'A'ishah, this is Jibril (peace be upon him) conveying greetings of peace to you."
"23074","311 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Warah: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Sabiq: narrated to us 'Amr ibn Abi Qays, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Umm Sulaym, she said: A son of mine died while my husband was absent. I stood and covered him in a corner of the house. My husband arrived, so I stood and perfumed myself for him, and he had intercourse with me. Then I brought him food and he began to eat. I said: Shall I not tell you something surprising about our neighbors? He said: What is with them? I said: They were lent a loan, and when it was asked back of them, they grieved. He said: Evil is what they did. I said: This is your son. He said: Surely you shall not overcome me in patience tonight. In the morning he went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. He said: O Allah, bless for them in their night. And I have indeed seen thereafter seven of her offspring in the mosque, all of whom had recited the Qur'an."
"23075","312 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi: narrated to us my father: narrated to us al-Walid ibn Muslim: narrated to us Malik ibn Anas, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr: that Abu Salamah informed him, from Umm Sulaym bint Milhan: that she sought a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after she had menstruated, or given birth, after performing the farewell circumambulation on the Day of Sacrifice. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her permission, and she departed."
"23076","313 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi: narrated to us Hisham ibn 'Ammar: narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Nastas: narrated to me Mirba', from Umm Anas, that she said: O Messenger of Allah, advise me. He said: Abandon acts of disobedience, for it is the best emigration; guard the obligatory duties, for it is the best jihad; and remember Allah much, for you will not bring to Allah anything more beloved to Him than much remembrance of Him."
"23077","314 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi: narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi: narrated to us Ayyub, from 'Ikrimah: that some people of Madinah asked Ibn 'Abbas about a woman who menstruated after performing the farewell circumambulation. He said: She may depart. They said: We do not care whether you gave us a ruling or not, while Zayd ibn Thabit says she may not depart. Ibn 'Abbas said: I only say what I know, and I do not care whether you take it or not; but when you arrive at Madinah, ask Umm Sulaym and others. Then they informed them that 'A'ishah said to Safiyyah: Are you detained in disappointment? You are holding us back. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is that? 'A'ishah said: Safiyyah has menstruated. It was said: She has already performed the farewell circumambulation. He said: Then no harm. He said: So they returned to Ibn 'Abbas and said: We found the report as you had narrated to us."
"23078","Umm Sulaym bint Abi Hakim al-Ansariyyah. 315 - Narrated to us Abu 'Umar al-Darir: narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Kufi: narrated to us Ahmad ibn Yunus: narrated to us Abu Shihab, from Ibn Abi Layla, from 'Abd al-Karim, from 'Abdullah ibn Fulan, from Umm Sulaym bint Abi Hakim, that she said: I lived to see the elderly women praying with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"23079","Umm Haram bint Milhan al-Ansariyyah, maternal aunt of Anas ibn Malik, wife of 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, and she is al-Rumaysa'. 316 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Kurayb: narrated to us Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Ju'fi, from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, he said: The grave of Umm Haram bint Milhan is in Cyprus, and they say: This is the grave of the righteous woman."
"23080","317 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Hudbah ibn Khalid: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Thabit, from Anas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I entered Paradise and heard a faint sound. I said: What is this? They said: Al-Rumaysa' bint Milhan."
"23081","318 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi, from Humayd, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I entered Paradise and heard ahead of me a faint sound, and behold, it was al-Ghumaysa' bint Milhan."
"23082","319 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us 'Arim Abu al-Nu'man: narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Umm Haram narrated to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day took a midday nap in her house, then woke up laughing. She said: O Messenger of Allah, what makes you laugh? He said: I marvel at people of my nation riding the sea like kings upon couches. She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make me among them. He said: You are among them. Then he slept and woke up laughing, and he said to me the like of that twice or thrice, while I kept saying: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make me among them. And he would say: You are among the first ones. He said: Then 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit married her and took her out on campaign. He said: When she returned, an animal was brought near for her to ride, and she fell and her neck broke."
"23083","320 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, he said: I read in the book of my father ... that Ibn al-Ashja' gave me books from his father, and among them was from Sufyan, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya, from Anas ibn Malik, from Bint Milhan, she said: I was sleeping near the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not far from him, and he woke up glorifying Allah. I asked him, and he said: There were displayed to me people of my nation riding the green sea like kings upon couches. I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make me among them, so he supplicated to Allah, mighty and majestic."
"23084","321 - Narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Hamdawayh al-Saffar: narrated to us 'Affan ibn Muslim ... And narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati: narrated to us Musa ibn Isma'il, both said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah: Yahya ibn Shu'ayb informed us, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Anas, from Umm Haram, she said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taking a midday rest in my house, he woke up laughing. I said: May my father be sacrificed for you, what makes you laugh? He said: There were displayed to me people of my nation riding the surface of this sea like kings upon couches. I said: Supplicate to Allah to make me among them. He said: O Allah, make her among them. Then he slept again and woke up laughing. I said: May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, what makes you laugh? He said: There were displayed to me people of my nation riding the surface of this sea like kings upon couches. I said: Supplicate to Allah to make me among them. He said: You are among the first ones. Then she campaigned with her husband 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, and when she returned, a grey mule of hers kicked her and she died."
"23085","322 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Kurayb ... And narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi: narrated to us my uncle Musa ibn 'Abd al-Rahman: narrated to us Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Ju'fi, from Za'idah, from al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reclined at the house of Bint Milhan and dozed, then woke up laughing. I said: O Messenger of Allah, at what do you laugh? He said: At people of my nation riding, campaigning on this green sea, their likeness like kings upon couches. I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make me among them. He said: You are among them. Then she married 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit and went out with Ibn Qirta?; when she mounted, her animal threw her off, and she died, then was buried."
"23086","323 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi: narrated to us Hisham ibn 'Ammar: narrated to us Yahya ibn Hamzah: narrated to us Thawr ibn Yazid, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from 'Amr ibn al-Aswad: that he narrated to him that he came to 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit while he was on the coast of Hims in a building of his, with his wife Umm Haram with him. 'Amr said: So Umm Haram narrated to us that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The first army of my nation to campaign on this sea have made it incumbent. Umm Haram said: O Messenger of Allah, am I among them? He said: You are among them. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The first army of my nation to campaign against the city of Caesar are forgiven. Umm Haram said: O Messenger of Allah, am I among them? He said: No."
"23087","324 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi: narrated to us my father ... And narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Khallal al-Makki: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi 'Umar al-'Adani, both said: narrated to us Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah al-Fazari, from Hilal ibn Maymun, from Abu Thabit Ya'la ibn Shaddad: he narrated to me, from Umm Haram, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned the sea campaign and said: For the one who suffers seasickness is the reward of a martyr, and for the one who drowns is the reward of two martyrs. I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make me among them. He said: O Allah, make her among them. So she rode the sea, and when she came out she mounted an animal and fell and died."
"23088","325 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn 'Asim: narrated to us Hafs ibn Maysarah, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from a woman who was once with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was sleeping, then he woke up laughing. She said: O Messenger of Allah, perhaps you laugh at my head? He said: No; there were displayed to me people of my nation riding the sea, campaigning in the path of Allah, their likeness like kings upon couches. She said: Then he slept and woke up laughing. She said: What makes you laugh, O Messenger of Allah? He said: There were displayed to me people riding the sea, campaigning in the path of Allah, then returning with little spoils, forgiven. I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make me among them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, make her among them."
"23089","Umm Waraqah bint 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith al-Ansariyyah. 326 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn: narrated to us al-Walid ibn 'Abdullah ibn Jumay' al-Zuhri: my grandmother narrated to me, from Umm Waraqah bint 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith al-Ansari: and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to visit her and call her the martyr. She had collected the Qur'an. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) campaigned at Badr, she said to him: Permit me to go out with you to treat your wounded and nurse your sick, that Allah may bestow martyrdom upon me. He said: Allah will bestow martyrdom upon you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had commanded her to lead the people of her household in prayer, and she had a caller to prayer, and she used to lead the people of her household until a slave-girl and a slave-boy of hers, whom she had promised freedom after her death, attacked her and killed her during the rule of 'Umar. It was said to him: Umm Waraqah has been killed by her slave-boy and slave-girl. He stood among the people and said: Umm Waraqah was attacked by her slave-boy and slave-girl and they killed her, and they fled; they were brought and he crucified them, and they were the first two crucified in Madinah. 'Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke the truth; he used to say: Come, let us visit the martyr."
"23090","327 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Waki': narrated to us al-Walid ibn 'Abdullah ibn Jumay': my grandmother narrated to me, and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khallad al-Ansari, from Umm Waraqah bint Nawfal, she said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) campaigned at Badr, I said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to campaign with you to treat your wounded and nurse your sick, that Allah may grant me martyrdom. He said: Remain in your house, for Allah will grant you martyrdom. She was called the martyr, and she had recited the Qur'an. She sought permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to designate a place in her house for prayer, and he permitted her."
"23091","Umm Ayyub bint Qays ibn 'Amr ibn Imri' al-Qays al-Ansariyyah, then al-Khazrajiyyah, wife of Abu Ayyub. 328 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani: narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, from Wasil, freedman of Abi 'Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn 'Abbas: that Abu Ayyub intended to divorce Umm Ayyub, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Ayyub, divorcing Umm Ayyub is a sin."
"23092","What Umm Ayyub transmitted. 329 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Madini ... And narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah ... And narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi, he said: narrated to us Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father, from Umm Ayyub: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed as her guest. She said: So we prepared for him food containing some of these greens. Sufyan said: Leeks and the like. He disliked it and said to his companions: Eat, for I am not like any of you; I fear I may harm my companion."
"23093","Umm Hani' al-Ansariyyah. 330 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn al-Husayn al-Missisi: narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab: narrated to us Ibn Lahi'ah: narrated to me Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal: that he heard Durrah bint Mu'adh narrating from Umm Hani' al-Ansariyyah: that she asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall we visit one another when we die, and will some of us see others? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The soul becomes a bird that clings to the trees, until when the Day of Resurrection comes, every soul enters its body."
"23094","Umm Sa'd al-Ansariyyah, wife of Zayd ibn Thabit. 331 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Zakariyya, from 'Anbasah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Zadhan, from Umm Sa'd al-Ansariyyah, wife of Zayd ibn Thabit, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no zakat upon one who acquires wealth until a year passes over it."
"23095","Umm Ziyad al-Ashja'iyyah. 332 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab: narrated to us Rafi' ibn Salamah al-Ashja'i: narrated to us Hashraj ibn Nubatah al-Ashja'i, from his grandmother, mother of his father: that she campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Khaybar as the sixth of six women. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent for them and said: With whose permission did you go out? And I saw anger in him. We said: O Messenger of Allah, we went out with medicine with which to treat the wounded, and to hand arrows, and to give sawiq to drink, and to spin hair, and to assist in the path of Allah. He said to us: Remain. When Allah granted him victory over Khaybar, he allotted for us as he allotted for the men. I said: And what was that? She said: Dates."
"23096","Umm al-Hakam. 333 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Madini: narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab, from 'Ayyash ibn 'Uqbah: narrated to me al-Fadl ibn al-Hasan ibn Umayyah: narrated to me Ibn Umm al-Hakam: my mother Umm al-Hakam informed me, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived from a campaign having acquired captives. I and my sister went and entered upon Fatimah, then we went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him to grant us servants, mentioning our needs. He said: The orphans of the people of Badr have preceded you."
"23097","Umm Layla al-Ansariyyah, grandmother of Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, the jurist judge. 334 - Narrated to us Bishr ibn Musa and Musa ibn Harun, both said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi 'Imran ibn Abi Layla, he said: narrated to me my paternal aunt Hammadah bint Muhammad, from her paternal aunt Aminah bint 'Abd al-Rahman, from her grandmother Umm Layla, she said: We pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and among what he took upon us was that we dye deeply with henna, comb with honey, and not leave our hands without dye."
"23098","335 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani: narrated to us Hazim ibn Muhammad al-Ghifari, from his mother Hammadah bint Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, and she was the eldest child of Muhammad, she said: I heard my paternal aunt say: I lived to see Umm Layla dyeing her outer garment, her head-cover, and her sheet once every month, and dyeing her body and her feet with full immersion. She said: Upon this we pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She said: And I saw her with two gold bracelets on her hand, and they considered them to be from the spoils; and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla used to dye for her."
"23099","336 - Narrated to us Bishr ibn Musa and Musa ibn Harun, both said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Imran ibn Abi Layla: narrated to me my paternal aunt Hammadah bint Muhammad, from her paternal aunt Aminah bint 'Abd al-Rahman, from her grandmother Umm Layla, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us that when any of us is able to make for herself two bracelets of silver on her hand, let her do so; and if she is not able, let her dye her hands even with a strap. And he said: Do not resemble men."
"23100","Umm al-'Ala' al-Ansariyyah, wife of Zayd ibn Thabit. 337 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Suwayd al-Shibami: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from Umm al-'Ala', she said: 'Uthman ibn Maz'un died, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon him. I said: May Allah have mercy on you, O Abu al-Sa'ib; my testimony concerning you is that Allah has honored you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And what makes you know that Allah has honored him? I said: I do not know. He said: As for him, certainty has come to him from his Lord; and by Allah, I am the Messenger of Allah, yet I do not know what will be done with me nor with you. I said: By Allah, I shall never declare anyone pure after him. She said: Then I saw a flowing spring for 'Uthman in a dream, so I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: That is his deed."
"23101","338 - Narrated to us Mus'ab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri: narrated to me my father ... And narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq: narrated to us Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani ... And narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Khallal al-Makki: narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd, all said: narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, from al-Zuhri, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from Umm al-'Ala', and she was a woman of their women who had pledged allegiance to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: 'Uthman ibn Maz'un died, so we wrapped him in his garments. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, and I said: May Allah have mercy on you, O Abu al-Sa'ib; my testimony concerning you is that Allah has honored you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And what makes you know? As for him, certainty has come to him, and by Allah I hope good for him, yet I do not know what Allah will do with him. I said: By Allah, I shall never declare anyone pure after him ever again. His words silenced me. When I slept, I saw a flowing spring for 'Uthman ibn Maz'un, and I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: That is his deed."
"23102","339 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Ishaq ibn Musa al-Ansari: narrated to us Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Shihab, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Umm al-'Ala', she said: When the Muslims arrived at Madinah, they drew lots for dwellings, so this one would stay with that one. Among them was 'Uthman ibn Maz'un, who stayed with a woman of the Ansar. He fell ill, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would visit him, until it coincided that he passed away. The woman said: My testimony concerning you, O Abu al-Sa'ib, is that Allah has honored you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: And what makes you know?"
"23103","340 - Narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati: narrated to us Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi: narrated to us Abu 'Awanah, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from a woman of them called Umm al-'Ala': that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her during her illness and said: Rejoice, O Umm al-'Ala', for the illness of the Muslim removes his sins as fire removes the dross of iron."
"23104","Umm Hisham bint Harithah ibn al-Nu'man. 341 - Narrated to us Abu Muslim al-Kashshi: narrated to us Hajjaj ibn Nusayr: narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Huna'i: narrated to us Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from the daughter of Harithah ibn al-Nu'man, she said: I memorized {Qaf, by the Glorious Qur'an} from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would indeed recite it in the Friday sermon upon the pulpit."
"23105","342 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi: narrated to us my father: narrated to us al-Walid ibn Muslim and 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Wahid, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, from the daughter of Harithah ibn al-Nu'man, she said: We and the household of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained for a month with nothing for us and them except a single oven in which we baked. So I memorized {Qaf} from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from what he would recite upon the pulpit on Friday."
"23106","343 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Numayr, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd ibn Zurarah, from Umm Hisham bint Harithah, she said: I memorized {Qaf} from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the dawn prayer."
"23107","344 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Numayr, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd ibn Zurarah, from Umm Hisham bint Harithah, she said: We remained for two years, or a year and part of a year, with our oven and the oven of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) being one; and I memorized {Qaf, by the Glorious Qur'an} only from the tongue of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), reciting it to the people every Friday when he preached."
"23108","345 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Khattab al-Tustari: narrated to us 'Ubaydullah ibn Sa'd: narrated to us my uncle: narrated to us my father, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq ... And narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm al-Ansari, from Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn As'ad ibn Zurarah, from Umm Hisham bint Harithah ibn al-Nu'man, she said: Our oven and the oven of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were one for two years, or a year and part of a year; and I took {Qaf, by the Glorious Qur'an} only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who would recite it every Friday when he preached to the people."
"23109","Umm al-Tufayl, wife of Ubayy ibn Ka'b al-Ansariyyah. 346 - Narrated to us Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj: narrated to us Yahya ibn Bukayr ... And narrated to us Ahmad ibn Rushdin: narrated to us Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Ju'fi and Ahmad ibn Salih, all said: narrated to us Ibn Wahb: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal narrated to him that Marwan ibn 'Uthman narrated to him, from 'Umarah ibn 'Amir ibn Hazm al-Ansari, from Umm al-Tufayl, wife of Ubayy ibn Ka'b, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I saw my Lord in a dream in the form of a dignified young man in greenery, wearing sandals of gold, with a covering of gold upon His face ... the hadith."
"23110","347 - Narrated to us 'Abd al-Malik ibn Yahya ibn Bukayr: narrated to me my father: narrated to us Ibn Lahi'ah, from Bukayr, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ubayy ibn Ka'b, from Umm al-Tufayl: that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and Ubayy ibn Ka'b disputed concerning a pregnant woman whose husband dies and she gives birth. Umm al-Tufayl said to Ubayy ibn Ka'b: Will you not inform 'Umar of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Subay'ah bint al-Harith when she gave birth days after her husband's death, whereupon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her off to a man?"
"23111","Umm Tariq, freedwoman of Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah al-Ansariyyah. 348 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi: narrated to us Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr, from Abu Ishaq al-Fazari, from al-A'mash, from Ja'far ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Tariq, freedwoman of Sa'd, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us and sought permission repeatedly, but he was not answered, so he returned. Sa'd said: Go to the Messenger of Allah, convey greetings of peace to him, and inform him that we kept silent toward him only so that he would add more. So I came to him, and while I was sitting with him, something came and sought permission at the door. He said: Who are you? She said: Umm Mildam. He said: No welcome and no greeting."
"23112","349 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd: narrated to us al-A'mash, from Ja'far ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Tariq, freedwoman of Sa'd, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and sought permission, and Sa'd kept silent; then he returned and Sa'd kept silent, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left. She said: Sa'd sent me to him, saying that nothing prevented us from admitting you except that we wanted you to add more. She said: I heard a voice at the door seeking permission though I saw nothing. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who are you? She said: I am Umm Mildam. He said: No welcome and no greeting; are you heading to the people of Quba'? She said: Yes. He said: Then go to them."
"23113","350 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Jarir, from al-A'mash, from Ja'far ibn Yazid, from Umm Tariq, freedwoman of Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah and greeted, but he did not answer him and kept silent. She said: Sa'd regretted and said to me: Go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and inform him that we kept silent toward him hoping he would add more. She said: He came and greeted at the door; I heard his voice but did not see him. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What are you? She said: I am Umm Mildam. He said: Is it you? Then no welcome to you and no greeting."
"23114","Umm Malik al-Ansariyyah. 351 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Fudayl, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, from a man who narrated to him, from Umm Malik al-Ansariyyah: that she brought a skin of ghee to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Bilal, who squeezed it, then handed it back to her. She returned, and behold it was full. She came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Has anything been revealed concerning me, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And what is that, O Umm Malik? She said: Why did you return my gift to me? He called Bilal and asked him about that. He said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I squeezed it until I was embarrassed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Enjoy it with blessing, O Umm Malik; this is a blessing whose reward Allah has hastened. Then he taught her after every prayer: Glory be to Allah ten times, praise be to Allah ten times, and Allah is Greatest ten times."
"23115","Umm Mu'adh al-Ansariyyah. 352 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qatawani: narrated to us Yunus ibn Bukayr: narrated to me Muhammad ibn Ishaq: narrated to me 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Salim Abu al-Nadr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon 'Uthman ibn Maz'un while he was dying, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a cloth, and he was covered with it. 'Uthman was staying with a woman of the Ansar called Umm Mu'adh. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained with him a long stay, with his companions with him, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) withdrew and wept; when he wept, the people of the house wept. He said: May Allah have mercy on you, O Abu al-Sa'ib, and al-Sa'ib had witnessed Badr with him. He said: Umm Mu'adh said: Glad tidings of Paradise to you, O Abu Sa'ib. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And what makes you know, O Umm Mu'adh? As for him, certainty has come to him, and we know naught but good. She said: By Allah, I shall never say it for anyone after him ever."
"23116","Umm Ma'bad ibn Ka'b. 353 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Khallal al-Makki: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi 'Umar al-'Adani: narrated to us Sufyan, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b, from his mother, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding mixed drinks: dates and unripe dates, and fresh dates. He said: Drink each one of them separately."
"23117","354 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Raqashi: narrated to us Yazid ibn Zuray', from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b, from his mother, and she had prayed toward both qiblas, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not steep dates and raisins together; steep each one of them separately."
"23118","Umm 'Uthman bint Abi al-'As al-Thaqafi. 355 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Khallal al-Makki: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Jawwaz: narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri: narrated to us 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Imran, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, from his father, from Ibn Abi Suwayd al-Thaqafi, he said: I heard 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As say: My mother informed me, she said: I witnessed Aminah when she gave birth to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When labor seized her, I looked at the stars descending until I said they would fall upon me. When she gave birth, there came forth from her a light that illuminated the house we were in and the dwelling; there was nothing I looked at except light."
"23119","Umm Humayd al-Ansariyyah. 356 - Narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb al-Misri: narrated to us 'Uthman ibn 'Imran al-Ramli: narrated to us Ibn Lahi'ah: narrated to me 'Abd al-Hamid ibn al-Mundhir al-Sa'idi, from his father, from his grandmother Umm Humayd, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, our husbands prevent us from praying with you, while we love to pray with you? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your prayer in your houses is better than your prayer in your chambers, and your prayer in your chambers is better than your prayer in your courtyards, and your prayer in your courtyards is better than your prayer in congregation."
"23120","Umm 'Amir bint Yazid ibn al-Sakan al-Ansariyyah. 357 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farawi ... And narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-San'ani: narrated to us Isma'il ibn Abi Uways, both said: narrated to us Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Habibah al-Ashhali, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit ibn Samit, from Umm 'Amir bint Yazid ibn al-Sakan, and she was among those who pledged allegiance: that she brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a bone with meat, and he picked meat from it while in the mosque of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal, then stood and prayed without performing ablution."
"23121","Umm Anas al-Ansariyyah, and she is not the mother of Anas ibn Malik. 358 - Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari: narrated to us Hisham ibn 'Ammar: narrated to us al-Walid ibn Muslim, from 'Anbasah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Zadhan, from Umm Sa'd, wife of Zayd ibn Thabit, from Umm Anas, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, my eyes overcome me regarding the last evening prayer? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hasten it, O Umm Anas; when the night fills the belly of every valley, the time of prayer has come, so pray, and there is no sin upon you."
"23122","359 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Kurayb: narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab: narrated to us 'Abd al-Malik ibn al-Hasan al-Ahwal, freedman of Marwan ibn al-Hakam: narrated to me Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Ansari, from Yunus ibn 'Imran ibn Abi Anas, from his grandmother Umm Anas, she said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: May Allah place you in the highest companionship of Paradise, and me with you. And I said: O Messenger of Allah, teach me a righteous deed to perform. He said: Establish prayer, for it is the best jihad; abandon acts of disobedience, for it is the best emigration; and remember Allah much, for it is the most beloved of deeds to Allah that you meet Him with."
"23123","Umm Malik al-Bahziyyah of Banu Sulaym. 360 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us Musaddad: narrated to us 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Tawus, from Umm Malik al-Bahziyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of people in tribulation is a man withdrawn with his wealth, worshipping his Lord and rendering its due, and a man who has seized the head of his horse in the path of Allah, frightening them and being frightened by them."
"23124","361 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us 'Amr ibn 'Awn al-Wasiti: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah informed us, from Layth, from Tawus, from Umm Malik al-Bahziyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which people are best in tribulation? He said: A man who has seized the head of his horse; he has frightened the enemy and they have frightened him. A man said to Tawus: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, which enemy? He said: The polytheists."
"23125","362 - Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari: narrated to us 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Jarir, from Layth, from Tawus, from Umm Malik al-Bahziyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned a tribulation and said: The best of you in it, and the best of people in it, is a man withdrawn with his wealth, worshipping Allah and giving its due, and a man who has seized the head of his horse, frightening the enemy and being frightened by them."
"23126","Umm Aws al-Bahziyyah. 363 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari: narrated to us 'Ismah ibn Sulayman al-Khazzaz: narrated to us Khalaf ibn Khalifah, from Abu Hashim al-Rummani, from Aws ibn Khalid, from Umm Aws al-Bahziyyah: that she melted ghee of hers and put it into a skin bag, then gifted it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He accepted it, took what was in it, and supplicated for her for blessing. They returned it to her while it was full of ghee. She thought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not accepted it, so she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) crying out. He said: Inform her of the story. She ate from it for the remainder of the life of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the rule of Abu Bakr, and the rule of 'Umar, and the rule of 'Uthman, until there occurred between 'Ali and Mu'awiyah what occurred."
"23127","Umm Ma'qil al-Asadiyyah. 364 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal: narrated to me Abu Kamil al-Jahdari: narrated to us Abu 'Awanah, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, he said: The messenger of Marwan whom he sent to Umm Ma'qil informed me, he said: She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a woman who has grown old and fallen ill; is there a deed that suffices me in place of my Hajj? He said: An 'umrah in Ramadan suffices you."
"23128","365 - Narrated to us al-Miqdam ibn Dawud: narrated to us Asad ibn Musa: narrated to us Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, from Ibn Umm Ma'qil, from Umm Ma'qil: that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What equals Hajj? He said: An 'umrah in Ramadan equals a Hajj."
"23129","366 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Numayr, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from 'Isa ibn Ma'qil, from Abu Ma'qil al-Asadi, Asad Khuzaymah, from Yusuf ibn 'Abdullah ibn Salam, from his grandmother Umm Ma'qil, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Ma'qil has perished, and we had a camel, and Abu Ma'qil bequeathed it in the path of Allah. He said: Why did you not go out upon it? Meaning for Hajj."
"23130","367 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Hisham ibn Abi al-Dumayk al-Mustamli: narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Madini: narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd: narrated to me my father, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq: narrated to me Yahya ibn 'Abbad ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Harith ibn Abi Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from his father, he said: I was among those who rode with Marwan ibn al-Hakam to Umm Ma'qil, and I was among those of the people who entered upon her with him when she narrated this hadith."
"23131","368 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Khallal al-Makki: narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd: narrated to us Waki', from al-A'mash, from 'Umarah ibn 'Umayr, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Umm Ma'qil: that she vowed to perform Hajj with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but it was not facilitated for her. She asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What suffices her? He said: An 'umrah in Ramadan."
"23132","369 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Khallal: narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Nafi', from Malik, from Sumayy, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Ma'qil: that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: I had prepared for Hajj, but something intervened. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Perform 'umrah in Ramadan, for an 'umrah in it is like a Hajj."
"23133","370 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Aban al-Asbahani: narrated to us Humayd ibn Mas'adah: narrated to us 'Umar ibn 'Ali al-Muqaddami, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from 'Isa ibn Ma'qil, from his grandmother Umm Ma'qil, she said: Abu Ma'qil died leaving a camel he had dedicated in the path of Allah. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Ma'qil has perished leaving a camel he dedicated to the Path, and I owe a Hajj? He said: O Umm Ma'qil, perform Hajj upon your camel, for Hajj is of the path of Allah."
"23134","371 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal: narrated to me my father ... And narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Khallal al-Makki: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi 'Umar al-'Adani: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from a woman of Banu Asad called Umm Ma'qil, she said: I intended Hajj but my camel strayed. I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Perform 'umrah in the month of Ramadan; an 'umrah in the month of Ramadan equals a Hajj."
"23135","372 - Narrated to us Abu Zur'ah 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr al-Dimashqi: narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak al-Suri: narrated to us Mu'awiyah ibn Sallam, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Ibn Umm Ma'qil, from his mother, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have vowed to perform Hajj, and I have a lean camel. He said: Perform 'umrah in Ramadan, for an 'umrah in Ramadan is like a Hajj."
"23136","373 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi: narrated to us my father: narrated to us al-Walid ibn Muslim: narrated to us al-Awza'i: Yahya narrated to me, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, he said: Ibn Umm Ma'qil narrated to me, from his mother, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I intend Hajj but my camel is lean. He said: Perform 'umrah in Ramadan, for an 'umrah in Ramadan is like a Hajj."
"23137","374 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal: narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi: narrated to us Muslim ibn Khalid, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from a woman called Umm Ma'qil, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform 'umrah in Ramadan, for it equals a Hajj."
"23138","Umm Muslim al-Ashja'iyyah. 375 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham: narrated to us Sufyan, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from a man, from Umm Muslim al-Ashja'iyyah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me while I was in a tent of mine of tanned leather, and he said to me: How beautiful it is, were there no dead animal skin in it. So I began to tear it apart."
"23139","376 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us Musaddad: narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from a man, from Umm Muslim al-Ashja'iyyah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me while I was in a tent, and he said: What a fine tent, were there no dead animal skin in it."
"23140","Umm al-Husayn al-Ahmasiyyah. 377 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Raja': narrated to us Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from Yahya ibn al-Husayn, from his grandmother Umm al-Husayn, she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preaching at Mina, wrapped in his garment, with the muscle of his upper arm trembling, saying: O people, fear Allah and hear and obey, even if an Abyssinian mutilated slave is appointed over you; hear and obey so long as he upholds among you the Book of Allah, exalted."
"23141","378 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani: narrated to us my father: narrated to us Zuhayr: narrated to us Abu Ishaq, from Yahya ibn al-Husayn, from his grandmother Umm al-Husayn, she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Pilgrimage while upon his saddle and his mount, with Husayn in my lap, having inserted his garment under his armpit, saying: O people, fear Allah and hear and obey whoever is over you, even if an Abyssinian mutilated slave; hear and obey so long as he upholds among you the Book of Allah."
"23142","379 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us Musaddad: narrated to us Abu al-Ahwas: narrated to us Abu Ishaq, from Yahya ibn Husayn, from his mother Umm Husayn, she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Pilgrimage and heard him reminding them and commanding them; among what I heard him say was: If an Abyssinian mutilated slave is appointed over you and upholds among you the Book of Allah, hear him and obey him."
"23143","380 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Khashshab al-Raqqi: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far al-Raqqi: narrated to us 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Yahya ibn Husayn, from his grandmother Umm al-Husayn, she said: I performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Farewell Pilgrimage, and I saw Bilal and Usamah, Bilal leading his mount by its bridle and the other lifting his garment shading him from the heat until he stoned the Aqabah pillar. Then he turned, having placed his garment under his right armpit over his left shoulder, and I saw beneath the joint of his right shoulder something like a gathered mass. He stood for the people and spoke many words; among what he said was: If an Abyssinian black slave is appointed over you leading you by the Book of Allah, hear and obey."
"23144","381 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Sulayman, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash, from Abu Ishaq: Yahya ibn al-Husayn and al-'Ayzar ibn Hurayth narrated to me, from Umm al-Husayn, she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the eve of 'Arafah upon a camel, standing and holding his cloak thus, baring his right upper arm. She said: I heard him say: O people, hear and obey, even if an Abyssinian mutilated slave is appointed over you, so long as he upholds among you the Book of Allah."
"23145","382 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Waki', from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from al-'Ayzar ibn Hurayth, from Umm al-Husayn, she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), wrapped in a cloak, saying: If an Abyssinian mutilated slave is appointed over you, hear him so long as he upholds among you the Book of Allah, exalted."
"23146","383 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi: narrated to us Hudbah ibn Khalid: narrated to us Harun ibn Musa al-Nahwi: narrated to us Isma'il ibn Muslim, from Abu Ishaq, from Ibn Umm al-Husayn, from his grandmother Umm al-Husayn: that she used to pray behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a row of women, and she heard him say: {Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, Master of the Day of Judgment} until: {nor of those astray}; he said: Amen, until I heard him while in the row of women. He would say the takbir when prostrating and when rising."
"23147","384 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us Muslim ibn Ibrahim: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Yahya ibn al-Husayn, from his grandmother, she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Hear and obey, even if a slave leading you by the Book of Allah is appointed over you. And I heard him say: O Allah, forgive those who shave. He said it three times. They said: And those who shorten? He said after the third: And those who shorten."
"23148","Umm Jundub al-Azdiyyah. 385 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us Muslim ibn Ibrahim ... And narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati: narrated to us Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi, both said: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Sulayman ibn 'Amr ibn al-Ahwas, from his grandmother: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came while the people were crowding upon him at the pillar, or the pillars, and said: O people, do not destroy yourselves; stone the pillars with pebbles like chickpea-sized flinging stones."
"23149","386 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Musa ibn Harun, both said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami: narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, from Mufaddal ibn Fudalah, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Sulayman ibn 'Amr ibn al-Ahwas, he said: Umm Jundub narrated to me that she saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the pillar saying: Let not some of you kill others; stone with pebbles like flinging stones."
"23150","387 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah ... And narrated to us 'Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri: narrated to us Yusuf ibn 'Adi al-Kufi, both said: narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Sulayman ibn 'Amr ibn al-Ahwas, from his mother Umm Jundub, she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stone the Aqabah pillar from the bottom of the valley on the Day of Sacrifice while upon a mount, then he left. A woman of Khath'am followed him with a boy of hers afflicted with an ailment; she said: O Messenger of Allah, this is my son and the remainder of my family, and he suffers an ailment and does not speak. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring me some water. He washed his hands and rinsed his mouth, then gave it to her and said: Give him to drink of it and pour upon him of it, and seek healing from Allah for him. She said: I met the woman and said: If only you would give me some of it. She said: It is only for this afflicted one. She said: I met the woman the following year and asked her about the boy. She said: He recovered and gained an intellect not like the intellects of people."
"23151","388 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Minjab and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, both said: 'Ali ibn Mushir informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad: Sulayman ibn 'Amr ibn al-Ahwas narrated to me, from his mother, she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of Sacrifice at the Aqabah pillar while riding upon his mule, with a man behind him shielding him from the people. I asked about the man, and it was said to me: This is al-Fadl ibn al-'Abbas. The people crowded upon him, and he said: O people, let not some of you kill others; and when you stone the pillars, stone with pebbles like flinging stones. Then he went deep into the valley and faced the pillar, stoning it from the bottom of the valley with seven pebbles, saying the takbir with each pebble, then left."
"23152","389 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Fudayl, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Sulayman ibn 'Amr ibn al-Ahwas, from his mother, she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoning the Aqabah pillar on the Day of Sacrifice from the bottom of the valley, saying: O people, let not some of you kill others, and stone with pebbles like flinging stones. He threw seven, then left without stopping, with a man behind him shielding him from the people. I said: Who is this? They said: Al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas."
"23153","Umm Nasr al-Muharibiyyah. 390 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani: narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mukhtar al-Razi, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah, from Umm Nasr al-Muharibiyyah, she said: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the meat of domestic donkeys. He said: Does it not graze pasture and eat trees? He said: Yes. He said: Then partake of its meat."
"23154","Umm Sufyan. 391 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal: narrated to us Hudbah ibn Khalid: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata', from Musa ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Sufyan: that a Jewish woman used to visit 'A'ishah and talk with her; when she stood, she would say: May Allah protect you from the torment of the grave. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, she informed him of that. He said: She has lied; that is only for the People of the Book. Then the sun eclipsed, and he said: I seek refuge with Allah from the torment of the grave. Then he said the takbir and stood, prolonging the standing, then bowed, prolonging the bowing, then raised his head and stood, prolonging the standing, then bowed, prolonging the bowing, lesser than the first bowing, then bowed two bowings and prostrated two prostrations, standing in them like his standing and bowing like his bowing."
"23155","Umm Hakim bint Wada' al-Khuza'iyyah. 392 - Narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati: narrated to us Musa ibn Isma'il: Hubabah bint 'Ajlan al-Khuza'iyyah narrated to us, she said: narrated to me my mother Hafsah, from Safiyyah bint Jarir, from Umm Hakim bint Wada' al-Khuza'iyyah, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the recompense of the rich toward the poor? He said: Sincere advice and supplication. I said: O Messenger of Allah, we dislike refusing kindness. He said: How ugly it is; were a trotter gifted to me, I would accept, and were I invited to a foreleg, I would answer."
"23156","393 - Narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl: narrated to us Musa ibn Isma'il: Hubabah bint 'Ajlan al-Khuza'iyyah narrated to us, she said: narrated to me my mother Hafsah, from Safiyyah bint Jarir, from Umm Hakim bint Wada' al-Khuza'iyyah, she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Exchange gifts, for a gift multiplies love and removes the rancor of the chest."
"23157","394 - Narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl: narrated to us Musa ibn Isma'il: Hubabah bint 'Ajlan narrated to us: narrated to me my mother Hafsah, from Safiyyah bint Jarir, from Umm Hakim bint Wada', she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The supplication of a parent pierces through to the Veil."
"23158","395 - Narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl: narrated to us Musa ibn Isma'il: Hubabah bint 'Ajlan narrated to us: narrated to me my mother Hafsah, from Safiyyah bint Jarir, from Umm Hakim bint Wada', she said: I heard the Prophet say: Hasten the breaking of the fast and delay the pre-dawn meal."
"23159","Umm Sunbulah al-Aslamiyyah. 396 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Madini ... And narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Kurayb, both said: narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab: narrated to us 'Amr ibn Qayzi ibn Shaddad ibn Usayd al-Madani: Sulayman, Zur'ah, and Muhammad, sons of al-Husayn ibn Sinan, informed me: that their grandmother Umm Sunbulah said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a gift, but the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused to take it, saying: We do not take gifts. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and said: Take the gift of Umm Sunbulah, for she is our desert-dweller and we are her townsfolk. He gave her such-and-such valley; 'Abdullah ibn Hasan ibn Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Talib bought it from her. He said: So he gave her a herd of camels. 'Amr ibn Qayzi said: I saw some of them. Abu Kurayb said: I said to Zayd ibn al-Hubab: Who gave her? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"23160","Umm Bilal bint Hilal al-Aslamiyyah. 397 - Narrated to us Mus'ab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri: narrated to us my father: narrated to us Anas ibn 'Iyad ... And narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us Musaddad: narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, both from Muhammad ibn Abi Yahya al-Aslami: my father narrated to me, from a woman called Umm Bilal, whose father was at al-Hudaybiyyah with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sacrifice the young sheep of sheep, for it is permissible."
"23161","Umm Kurz al-Khuza'iyyah. 398 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, both said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn 'Abdullah al-Wasiti: narrated to us my father, from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, from Qatadah, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, from Umm Kurz al-Khuza'iyyah: that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the 'aqiqah. He said: For the boy, two equal sheep, and for the girl, one sheep."
"23162","399 - Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari: narrated to us Isma'il ibn 'Abdullah ibn Zurarah al-Raqqi: narrated to us Suwayd ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from Umm Kurz al-Khuza'iyyah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In the 'aqiqah, for the boy two sheep, and for the girl one sheep."
"23163","400 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us: 'Ata' informed me, from Habibah bint Maysarah ibn Abi Khuthaym, from Umm Bani Kurz al-Ka'biyyin: that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the 'aqiqah. He said: Upon the boy, two equal sheep, and upon the girl, one sheep. I said: What are the two equal ones? He said: The two alike."
"23164","401 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from 'Ata', from Habibah bint Maysarah, from Umm Kurz, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: For the boy, two equal sheep, and for the girl, one sheep."
"23165","402 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Najiyah: narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa: narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Maghra', from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from 'Ata', from Habibah bint Maysarah, from Umm Kurz, she said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the 'aqiqah. He said: For the boy, two equal sheep, and for the girl, one sheep."
"23166","403 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Mustamli: narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Madini: narrated to us Wahb ibn Jarir: narrated to me my father, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from 'Ata', from Umm 'Uthman bint Khuthaym, from Umm Kurz: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In the 'aqiqah, for the boy two equal sheep, and for the girl one sheep."
"23167","Umm 'Uthman Habibah bint Maysarah ibn Khuthaym, and it is said Ibn Abi Khuthaym, and it is said Bint Abi Khuthaym. 404 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us Mu'alla ibn Asad al-'Ammi: narrated to us Ibn Abi Muti', from Matar al-Warraq, from 'Ata', from Umm Kurz: that she heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: For the boy two sheep, and for the girl one sheep."
"23168","405 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid informed me that Siba' ibn Thabit claimed that Muhammad ibn Thabit ibn Siba' informed him that Umm Kurz informed him: that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the 'aqiqah. He said: For the boy two sheep, and for the girl one sheep; and it does not harm you whether they are male or female."
"23169","406 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and Yahya al-Himmani, both said: narrated to us Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father, from Siba' ibn Thabit, from Umm Kurz: that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the 'aqiqah. He said: For the boy two sheep, and for the girl one sheep; and it does not harm you whether they are male or female."
"23170","407 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna: narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Madini ... And narrated to us Talib ibn Qurrah al-Adhani: narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Tabba' ... And narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr, and Yahya al-Himmani, all said: narrated to us Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from Siba' ibn Thabit: that he heard Umm Kurz al-Ka'biyyah say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Leave birds upon their roosts."
"23171","408 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal: narrated to me my father: narrated to us Abu Bakr al-Hanafi: narrated to us Usamah ibn Zayd, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Umm Kurz al-Khuza'iyyah, she said: A boy was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he urinated upon him, so he ordered and it was sprinkled; and a girl was brought and she urinated upon him, so he ordered and it was washed."
"23172","Umm Subayyah al-Juhaniyyah. 409 - Narrated to us Hafs ibn 'Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi: narrated to us Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah: narrated to us Sufyan, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from al-Nu'man ibn Kharrbudh, from Umm Sumaytah (Subayyah), she said: I would sometimes share ablution water with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from a single vessel."
"23173","Umm 'Afif al-Nahdiyyah. 410 - Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal: narrated to me Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami ... And narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us 'Asim ibn 'Ali, both said: narrated to us 'Abd al-Mun'im Abu Sa'id al-Harrani al-Uswari, from al-Salt ibn Dinar, from Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, from a woman of them called Umm 'Afif, she said: We pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the women pledged allegiance; he took upon them that they not speak to a man except a close relative, and he commanded us to recite over our dead the Opening of the Book."
"23174","Umm Ishaq al-Ghanawiyyah. 411 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Jarudi al-Asbahani: narrated to us al-Sakan ibn Sa'id al-Qadi: narrated to us 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Abd al-Warith: narrated to us Bashshar ibn 'Abd al-Malik: my grandmother Umm Hakim bint Dinar narrated to me, from her freedwoman Umm Ishaq al-Ghanawiyyah, she said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a bowl of tharid was brought. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate, and I ate with him, and Dhu al-Yadayn ate with us. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a bone and handed it to me, saying: What is with you? I said: I was fasting but forgot. Dhu al-Yadayn said: Now, after you have eaten your fill? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No harm upon you; it is only provision Allah drove to you, so complete your fast."
"23175","Umm Shihab al-Ghanawiyyah. 412 - Narrated to us Sahl ibn Musa Shiran al-Ramahurmuzi: narrated to us 'Isa ibn Shadhan: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad Abu Ahmad al-A'rabi: Nadiyah bint Abi Majidah narrated to me, she said: narrated to me my mistress Umm Shihab al-Ghanawiyyah, she said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered for me barley flour-drink and clothed me a garment."
"23176","Umm Hufayd, also called Umm Haqiq, bint al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar al-Khuza'iyyah, sister of Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 413 - Narrated to us Abu Muslim al-Kashshi: narrated to us Abu 'Amr ibn Marzuq: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: My maternal aunt Umm Hufayd gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ghee, dried yoghurt, and desert lizards. He ate of the ghee and dried yoghurt and left the lizards out of aversion, though had it been unlawful it would not have been eaten upon the table of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"23177","Umm Ma'bad, freedwoman of Qarazah ibn Ka'b al-Ansari. 414 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani: narrated to us Musa ibn Muhammad al-Ansari, from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Umm Ma'bad, freedwoman of Qarazah, she said: I used to give drink to people among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Zayd ibn Thabit and Mu'adh ibn Jabal."
"23178","415 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani: narrated to us Musa ibn Muhammad al-Ansari, from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Umm Ma'bad, freedwoman of Qarazah ibn Ka'b, she said: As for dubba', it is the pumpkin which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade."
"23179","416 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani: narrated to us Musa ibn Muhammad al-Ansari, from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Umm Ma'bad, freedwoman of Qarazah, she said: O my son, one who declares lawful what Allah has made lawful is like one who declares lawful what Allah has made unlawful."
"23180","417 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani: narrated to us Musa ibn Muhammad al-Ansari, from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Umm Ma'bad, freedwoman of Qarazah ibn Ka'b, she said: Hantam are glazed vessels made in the land of the non-Arabs; this is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade."
"23181","418 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani: narrated to us Musa ibn Muhammad al-Ansari: narrated to us Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Umm Ma'bad, freedwoman of Qarazah ibn Ka'b, she said: Naqir is the hollowed roots of palm trunks planted? which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade."
"23182","419 - And with the same chain, she said: I used to give drink to people among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Zayd ibn al-Arqam and Mu'adh ibn Jabal."
"23183","Umm al-Azhar. 420 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Marzuq: Bint Munqidh al-'A'ishiyyah narrated to me, from her grandmother Zaynab bint al-Zibriqan, from Umm al-Azhar: that her father took her to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he wiped with his hand upon her and blessed her. She said: She was a devout righteous woman."
"23184","Umm al-Walid bint 'Umar ibn al-Khattab. 421 - Narrated to us Abu 'Aqil Anas ibn Salim al-Khawlani: narrated to us Abu Umayyah 'Amr ibn Hisham al-Harrani: narrated to us 'Uthman ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Tara'ifi, from al-Wazi' ibn Nafi', from Salim ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, from Umm al-Walid bint 'Umar, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked out one evening and said: O people, do you not feel shame? They said: Of what, O Messenger of Allah? He said: You gather what you do not eat, build what you do not inhabit, and hope for what you do not attain. Do you not feel shame at that?"
"23185","Umm al-Hakam bint 'Abbas. 422 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr, both said: narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab: 'Ayyash ibn 'Uqbah narrated to me: al-Fadl ibn Hasan ibn 'Amr ibn Umayyah narrated to me, from Ibn Umm al-Hakam: that his mother Umm al-Hakam narrated to him that she went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him to grant them servants, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had arrived from his journey having acquired captives. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The orphans of the people of Badr have preceded you."
"23186","Umm al-Harith bint 'Ayyash. 423 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Dirar ibn Surad: narrated to us Mus'ab ibn Sallam, from Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hassan, from Umm al-Harith bint 'Ayyash, she said: I saw Budayl ibn Warqa' at Jam' following the people and calling out: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commands you not to fast these days, for they are days of eating and drinking."
"23187","Umm Sinan al-Aslamiyyah. 424 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Abu Kurayb: narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Umar ibn Salih: narrated to us Abu Sinan Yazid ibn Hurayth, from Thubaytah bint Hanzalah, from her mother Umm Sinan al-Aslamiyyah, among those who pledged allegiance, she said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have come to you bashfully, and I came to you only driven by need. He said: Had you sufficed yourself, it would have been better for you."
"23188","Umm Taliq. 425 - Narrated to us 'Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri: narrated to us Yusuf ibn 'Adi: narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman, from al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful, from Talq ibn Habib, from Abu Taliq: that his wife Umm Taliq said: O Prophet of Allah, what equals Hajj with you? He said: An 'umrah in Ramadan."
"23189","Umm 'Abd bint al-Harith ibn Qudayd al-Hudhaliyyah, mother of 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. 426 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Waki', from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd: that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, assigned stipends among the emigrant women, one thousand each, among them Umm 'Abd."
"23190","427 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us Salm ibn Junadah: narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab, from al-Mas'udi, from his brother 'Utbah, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab: that he waited for Umm 'Abd until she prayed over 'Utbah."
"23191","Umm 'Abdullah, sister of Shaddad ibn Aws al-Ansariyyah. 428 - Narrated to us Abu Zur'ah 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr al-Dimashqi: narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak al-Suri: narrated to us al-Walid ibn Muslim, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Damrah ibn Habib, from Umm 'Abdullah, sister of Shaddad ibn Aws: that she sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a cup of milk at his breaking of fast while he was fasting, during the long day and intense heat. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to her asking: Whence did you get this milk? She said: From a sheep of mine. He sent back her messenger asking: Whence did you get this sheep? She said: I bought it with my money. So he took it from her. On the morrow, Umm 'Abdullah came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I sent to you the milk out of compassion for you due to the long day and intense heat, yet you sent back the messenger concerning it. He said to her: Thus have the messengers been commanded: to eat only what is pure and to do only what is righteous."
"23192","Umm 'Abdullah, wife of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari. 429 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Sahm ibn Minjab, from al-Qartha', he said: When Abu Musa became gravely ill, his wife cried out. He said: Do you not know what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said? She said: Yes. Then she fell silent. It was said to her afterwards: What did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say? She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has cursed whoever shaves, or tears, or wails."
"23193","430 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami: narrated to us 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Jarir, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Yazid ibn Aws, he said: I entered upon Abu Musa while he was critically ill, and his wife went to weep or began to do so. Abu Musa said to her: Have you not heard what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said? She said: Yes. So she fell silent. When Abu Musa died, Yazid said: I met his wife and said to her regarding Abu Musa's words: Have you not heard what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said? Then she fell silent. She said: Yes; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who shaves, or wails, or tears."
"23194","Umm Kabshah al-'Udhriyyah. 431 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam and Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami, both said: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: narrated to us Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ru'asi, from Hasan ibn Salih, from al-Aswad ibn Qays: Sa'id ibn 'Amr al-Qurashi narrated to me that Umm Kabshah, a woman of 'Udhrah of Quda'ah, said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to go out in such-and-such army. He said: No. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I do not intend to fight; I only intend to treat the wounded and the sick, or to give drink to the sick. He said: Were it not that it would become a precedent, and it would be said: So-and-so woman went out, I would have permitted you; but remain seated."
"23195","Umm Mughith. 432 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn 'Uthman ibn Salih: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam: narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn 'Umar, from Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf, from his father, from Umm Mughith: that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding mixed drinks. I said: And what are they? He said: Dates and raisins. Umm Mughith was the grandmother of Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, and she had prayed toward both qiblas in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"23196","433 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn 'Uthman ibn Salih: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam: narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn 'Umar, from Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf, from his father, from Umm Mughith, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Every intoxicant is unlawful."
"23197","What Wabisah ibn Ma'bad al-Asadi transmitted from Umm Qays: Umm Qays bint Mihsan al-Asadiyyah, sister of 'Ukkashah. 434 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi: narrated to us 'Abd al-Salam ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sakhr al-Wasibi: narrated to me my father, from Shayban ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Nahwi, from Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, he said: We came to Wabisah ibn Ma'bad standing, leaning upon a staff, and he was asked: What made you take a staff? He said: Umm Qays bint Mihsan narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he aged and carried flesh, leaned upon it."
"23198","'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah, from Umm Qays. 435 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, Ibn Jurayj, and Ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah informed me: that Umm Qays bint Mihsan, and she was among the first emigrant women who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed me that she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a son of hers who had not yet reached eating food, and she had treated his throat ailment by pressing. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why do you torment your children with these pressings? Upon you is this Indian wood, meaning costus, for in it are seven cures, among them pleurisy. 'Ubaydullah said: Umm Qays informed me that her son urinated in the lap of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for water and poured it over his urine without washing it."
"23199","436 - Narrated to us Abu Muslim al-Kashshi: narrated to us Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal and 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi ... And narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, all said: narrated to us Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan: that she entered with a son of hers who had not eaten food, and he urinated upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he called for water and sprinkled it upon him."
"23200","437 - Narrated to us 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: narrated to us al-Qa'nabi, from Malik ... And narrated to us Bakr ibn Sahl: narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf: Malik informed us ... And narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-San'ani: narrated to us Isma'il ibn Abi Uways: Malik narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan: that she came with a small son of hers who had not eaten food to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she seated him in his lap, and he urinated upon his garment. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for water and sprinkled it without washing it."
"23201","438 - 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Miqlas narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, 'Amr ibn al-Harith, Yunus ibn Yazid, and Ibn Sam'an informed me that Ibn Shihab narrated to them, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan, that she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a small son of hers who had not eaten food; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated him in his lap and he urinated on him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for water and sprinkled it over him and did not wash it."
"23202","439 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, Shu'ayb ibn Yahya narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed us; (ha) and Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utbah, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan, that she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a small son of hers who had not eaten food; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated him in his lap and he urinated on him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for water and sprinkled it and did not wash it."
"23203","440 - Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utbah, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan, that she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a small son of hers for whom she had hung something because of al-'udhrah; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Why do you torment your children with these suspensions? Upon you is this Indian wood - meaning costus - for in it are seven cures, among them pleurisy."" 'Ubayd Allah said: And Umm Qays informed me that her son urinated in the lap of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for water and poured it over his urine and did not wash it."
"23204","441 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Hashim al-Bayruti narrated to us, al-Hiql ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utbah, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan, she said: We entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a son of mine who had not tasted food; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed him in his lap and he urinated on his garment, so he ordered water and sprinkled it over it and did not wash it."
"23205","442 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Bara' narrated to us, al-Mu'afa ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utbah, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan al-Asadiyyah, sister of 'Ukkashah ibn Mihsan - and she was among the first Muhajir women who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Upon you is this Indian wood - meaning al-saqt - for in it are seven cures, among them pleurisy."""
"23206","443 - Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Makhshiyy al-Farghani narrated to us, 'Ubayd Allah ibn Sa'id ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, my father narrated to me, my grandfather narrated to me - the maternal uncle of al-Mughirah ibn al-Husayn ibn Rashid al-Hashimi - Yahya ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Salim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab narrated to me, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah, that Umm Qays bint Mihsan said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a son of mine who had not eaten food; he placed him in his lap and he urinated on the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he called for water and sprinkled it on his garment and did not wash it."
"23207","444 - al-Mufaddal ibn Muhammad al-Janadi said, Abu Humah Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Abu Qurrah narrated to us, he said: Zam'ah ibn Salih mentioned, from Ya'qub ibn 'Ata', from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan - and she was among the first Muhajir women who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she is the sister of 'Ukkashah ibn Mihsan - that she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a son of hers who had not reached eating food; he urinated in the lap of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for water and sprinkled it over his urine and did not wash it."
"23208","Nafi', freedman of Hamnah bint Shuja', from Umm Qays. 445 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak al-'Ayshi narrated to us; (ha) and 'Abd Allah ibn Najiyah narrated to us, 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, they said: Sa'd Abu 'Asim narrated to us, Nafi', freedman of Hamnah bint Shuja', narrated to us, he said: Umm Qays said to me: If only you had seen me with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) holding my hand in a lane of the lanes of Madinah in which there was no house, until he ended at Baqi' al-Gharqad; he said to me: ""O Umm Qays,"" I said: At your service and blessedness, O Messenger of Allah. He said: ""You shall see this graveyard; Allah will raise from it on the Day of Resurrection seventy thousand in the image of the moon on the night of full moon, entering Paradise without reckoning."" Then 'Ukkashah stood and said: And I, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""And you."" Then another stood and said: And I, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""'Ukkashah has preceded you to it."""
"23209","Abu al-Hasan, freedman of Umm Qays, from Umm Qays. 446 - Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Abu al-Hasan, freedman of Umm Qays bint Mihsan, from Umm Qays, that she said: My son died and I grieved intensely over him; I said to the one washing him: Do not wash my son with cold water, lest you kill him. So 'Ukkashah ibn Mihsan went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of her words; he smiled and then said: ""What did she say? May her life be long."" And we do not know any woman who lived as long as she lived."
"23210","'Adi ibn Dinar from Umm Qays. 447 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, al-Thawri informed us, from Abu al-Miqdam Thabit ibn Hurmuz, from 'Adi ibn Dinar, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan, that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about menstrual blood touching a garment; he said: ""Wash it with water and lote, and scrape it with a rib."""
"23211","Unnamed women among those who have Companionship. 448 - Idris al-'Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Bushayr ibn Yasar; (ha) and 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, that Husayn ibn Mihsan informed him that his paternal aunt entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day, and he said: ""Are you a woman with a husband?"" She said: Yes. He said: ""How are you toward him?"" She said: O Messenger of Allah, I do not fall short regarding him except what I am unable to do. He said: ""Consider how you are toward him, for he is only your Paradise and your Fire."""
"23212","449 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Husayn ibn Mihsan, from his paternal aunt, that she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a need, and he fulfilled her need for her; then he said: ""Are you a woman with a husband?"" She said: Yes. He said: ""How are you toward him?"" She said: I do not fall short regarding him except what I am unable to do. He said: ""Consider how you are toward him, for he is your Paradise and your Fire."""
"23213","450 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Husayn ibn Mihsan, from his paternal aunt, that she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: ""Do you have no husband?"" She said: Yes. He said: ""How are you toward him?"" She said: I do not fall short except what I am unable to do. He said: ""Know that he is your Paradise and your Fire."""
"23214","451 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, they said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, al-Hajjaj ibn Safwan narrated to us, from Usayd ibn Abi Usayd al-Barrad, from a woman among those who pledged allegiance, she said: Among what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took from us was that we would not disobey him in what is right, and that we would not scratch a face, nor dishevel hair, nor tear a collar, nor call out woe."
"23215","452 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; (ha) and 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, they said: Sharik informed us, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Isa, from Musa ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid, from a woman of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal, she said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, we have foul-smelling roads to the mosque. He said: ""Is there not after them roads cleaner than them?"" We said: Yes indeed. He said: ""Then this is for that."""
"23216","453 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Qays ibn al-Rabi', from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Isa, from Salim ibn 'Abd Allah, from a woman of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we have a foul road in the rain. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Is there not before it a good road?"" I said: Yes indeed. He said: ""Then that is for that."""
"23217","454 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Abu Musa 'Isa ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Ghazwan, companion of al-Narsi, narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Alqamah, Muhammad ibn 'Umarah ibn 'Amir narrated to me, from Zaynab bint Nubayt ibn Jabir, from her mother or her maternal aunt, daughters of Abu Umamah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entrusted me with his daughters - meaning Abu Umamah As'ad ibn Zurarah - and they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) adorned us with eardrops of gold."
"23218","455 - Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Marwan ibn 'Uthman, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, my maternal aunt narrated to me, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) certainly taught us the verse of stoning: ""The old man and the old woman, if they commit adultery, stone them outright, for what they have concluded of pleasure."""
"23219","456 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn al-'Ala' ibn Zibriq al-Himsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Damdam ibn Zur'ah, from Shurayh ibn 'Ubayd, from the hadith of Habib ibn 'Ubayd, from Hurayth ibn al-Abahh al-Sulayhi, that a woman of Banu Asad said: I was one day with Zaynab, wife of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while we were dyeing garments with red ochre; while we were thus, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked in upon us, and when he saw the red ochre he turned back. When Zaynab saw that, she knew that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had disliked what she did, so she took water and washed her garments and concealed every redness; then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned and looked in, and when he did not see anything he entered."
"23220","457 - Muhammad ibn Aban al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abbadah al-Bahili narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Zuhri narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Imran narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, from his father, from Ibn Abi Suwayd al-Thaqafi, he said: I heard 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As say: My mother informed me, she said: I witnessed Aminah when she gave birth to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when labor seized her, I looked at the stars hanging low until I said they would fall upon me. When she gave birth to him, there came forth from her a light that illuminated for him the house we were in and the dwelling, and there was nothing I looked at but light."
"23221","458 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, from a woman - an orphan who was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - of Thaqif, that she narrated to Safiyyah bint Abi 'Ubayd that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever among you is able to die in Madinah, let him die, for whoever dies in it, I shall be a witness for him, or an intercessor on the Day of Resurrection."""
"23222","459 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn 'Uthman informed us, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, freedman of the family of Talhah, from 'Isa ibn Talhah, the wet-nurse of Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to me, she said: When Muhammad ibn Talhah was born, I brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: ""What did they name him?"" We said: Muhammad. He said: ""This is my name, and his kunya is Abu al-Qasim."""
"23223","460 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd al-Fa'ishi, from the daughter of Khabbab, she said: My father went out on a campaign in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to look after us and milk a goat of ours; he would milk it into a large bowl and it would fill. When Khabbab returned, he would milk it, and its yield returned to the first yield. And Allah knows best what is correct, and may Allah bless our master Muhammad and his family and Companions and grant peace."
"23224","The long hadiths. The Islam of 'Adi ibn Hatim, kunya Abu Tarif, may Allah be pleased with him. 1 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Idris al-Uswari narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn 'Alqamah al-Mazini narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from 'Amir al-Sha'bi, from 'Adi ibn Hatim, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah and emigrated to it, he began dispatching raiding parties, and the camels of a people would ever be raided by his horsemen. When I saw that, I said: By Allah, if only I had left behind some of my camels so they would be near. By Allah, one day I was unaware when the camel-herder came running with his staff. I said: Woe to you, what is wrong with you? He said: By Allah, the livestock has been raided. I said: Who raided them? He said: The horsemen of Muhammad. I said to myself: This is what I feared. So I leapt to saddle my camels to escape with my family. I was a Christian, and I had a paternal aunt; I went in and said: What do you think should be done with her? I carried my wife, and my paternal aunt came to me and said: O 'Adi, do you not fear Allah, that you escape with your wife and leave your paternal aunt? I said: What could they do with her? A woman well past her prime. So I went on and did not turn to her until I reached Syria, ending up with Caesar, who was then at Hims. I said: I am a man of the Arabs and I am upon your religion, and this man keeps attacking us; flight to you was the way out. He said: Go and settle at such-and-such place until we see what to think of you. So I went and settled at the place he told me, and I stayed there a while. While I was there one day, I saw a woman in a howdah heading toward us until she reached our dwellings, and behold it was my paternal aunt. She said to me: O 'Adi, did you not fear Allah, that you escaped with your wife and left your paternal aunt? I said: That has happened; tell us what came after us. She said: After you left, the horsemen came to us and took us captive, and I was taken among the captives until I reached Madinah. We were at a side of the mosque when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by us at midday with a man following behind him, 'Ali ibn Abi Talib. That man gestured to me to speak to him, so I called out to him: O Messenger of Allah, the child has perished and the delegate is absent, so bestow favor upon me, may Allah bestow upon you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""And who is your delegate?"" I said: 'Adi ibn Hatim. He said: ""The one who fled from Allah and His Messenger."" Then he went on and did not turn to me until the next day, when he passed by me near that hour with that man behind him; he gestured to me to speak to him, so I called out: O Messenger of Allah, the child has perished and the delegate is absent, so bestow favor upon me, may Allah bestow upon you. He said: ""And who is your delegate?"" I said: 'Adi ibn Hatim al-Ta'i. He said: ""The one who fled from Allah and His Messenger,"" and did not turn to me. On the third day, near that hour, he passed with that one behind him - meaning 'Ali - and he gestured to speak to him; I gestured with my hand that I had already spoken to him twice, but he gestured: speak to him again. So I called out: O Messenger of Allah, the child has perished and the delegate is absent, so bestow favor upon me, may Allah bestow upon you. He said: ""And who is your delegate?"" I said: 'Adi ibn Hatim. He said: ""The one who fled from Allah and His Messenger."" Then he said: ""Go, for you are free for the sake of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic; when you find someone coming to your people, inform me and we shall carry you to your people."" She said: So I set out and found a caravan of Tanukh carrying oil; they had sold their oil and were returning, so they mounted me on this camel and provisioned me. 'Adi said: Then my paternal aunt said to me: You are a foolish man; one of your people who is not your like has surpassed you in honor. Go to this man and take your share. I said: My paternal aunt has indeed advised me well. By Allah, if I go to this man and see what pleases me I shall take it, and if I see otherwise I shall return - and I was protective of my religion. So I came until I reached Madinah without protection, ending at the mosque, where I found a great circle, and I had never been among a people but I was recognized. When I reached the circle I greeted, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: ""Who are you?"" I said: I am 'Adi ibn Hatim al-Ta'i. The most beloved thing to him was that the nobles and chiefs of the Arabs should greet him with peace. He leapt from the circle, took my hand, and led me to his house. While he was walking with me, a woman called to him with a boy with her: O Messenger of Allah, we have a need of you. He stood apart with them at length until I felt pity for him from the long standing. I said to myself: I bear witness that you are free of my religion and the religion of al-Nu'man ibn al-Mundhir; had you been a king, no boy and woman would stand with him as long as I see. Then Allah cast love for him into my heart, until I reached his house. He threw to me a cushion stuffed with palm fiber; I sat on it and he sat on the ground. I said to myself: And this. Then he said to me: ""Nothing has made you flee from the Muslims but that you heard them say: There is no god but Allah? And is there any god but Allah? And nothing has made you flee from the Muslims but that you heard them say: Allah is Greater? And do you know anything greater than Allah, the Mighty and Majestic?"" He kept on until I accepted Islam, and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, removed what was in my heart of love for Christianity. Then I asked: O Messenger of Allah, I am in a land of hunting, and one of us shoots game with his arrow and does not follow its trace for a day or two, then finds it dead with his arrow in it - may he eat it? He said: ""Yes, if he wishes."""
"23225","2 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hammad al-Sha'bi narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu 'Ubaydah ibn Hudhayfah, from a man called Ayman, that he entered upon 'Adi ibn Hatim and said: A hadith has reached me from you, and I would love to hear it from you. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent, and I was among the people most hateful toward him; I was in the farthest land of the Arabs bordering Byzantium, and I disliked my place more than I had disliked my first place. So I said: I shall surely come to this man; if he is truthful it will not be hidden from me, and if he is a liar it will not be hidden from me. So I came to Madinah, and the people looked up at me, saying: 'Adi ibn Hatim, 'Adi ibn Hatim. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: ""O 'Adi, accept Islam and you will be safe."" I said: I have a religion. He said: ""I know your religion better than you, and you do not know my religion better than I. Do you not head your people?"" He said: I said: Yes indeed. He said: ""Do you not take the mirba' (the quarter of the spoils)?"" I said: Yes. He said: ""That is not lawful for you in your religion."" He said: That removed some of what was in my soul. He said: ""What prevents you from accepting Islam is your reckoning of those you see around us, and that you see the people united against us as one?"" I said: Yes. He said: ""Have you been to al-Hirah?"" I said: No, though I know its place. He said: ""A woman traveler shall soon come forth from al-Hirah until she circumambulates the House without protection, and the treasures of Kisra ibn Hurmuz shall soon be opened."" I said: The treasures of Kisra ibn Hurmuz?! He said: ""The treasures of Kisra; and a man shall soon bring forth wealth from his wealth and find no one to accept it."" He said: I have seen the woman traveler come forth from al-Hirah until she circumambulated the House without protection, and I was among the first horsemen to raid al-Sawad; by Allah, the third shall surely be, by the word of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"23226","The Islam of Jarir ibn 'Abd Allah al-Bajali, may Allah be pleased with him. 3 - Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Mihran al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn 'Abbad al-Madhhiji narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Musa, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utbah, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the morning prayer, he would sit in his place of prayer until the sun rose. One day he said: ""There shall come upon you from this mountain pass one of the best of Dhu Yaman, upon him the touch of an angel."" Then Jarir ibn 'Abd Allah al-Bajali came with eleven riders of his people; they tied their mounts, then drew near. Jarir said: Peace be upon you, O company of Quraysh; where is the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? The Prophet of Allah - peace be upon him - said: ""O Jarir, accept Islam and you will be safe. The hardness of hearts, harshness, and sin are among the people of tents of hair and wool. O Jarir, you shall not attain the reality of Islam nor complete faith thereafter until you abandon the worship of idols. O Jarir, I warn you against the world and the sweetness of its suckling and the bitterness of its weaning."" Jarir said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah to expand my breast for Islam. He said: ""O Allah, expand his breast for Islam, and do not make him among the people of apostasy, and do not grant him abundance lest he transgress, and do not grant him respite lest he forget."" Jarir said: Then what is it that I came with, while I wish to ask you about it? He said: ""You came wishing to ask me about the right of the father over his child. Among the right of the father over his child is that he humble himself to him in anger, and prefer him in contentment; and among the right of the child over his father is that he cultivate his manners well and not deny his lineage. The requiter is not the joiner; the joiner is only the one who, when his kinship is severed, joins it."" He said: By the One who sent you with the truth, this is what I wished to ask you about; I did not wish to ask you about anything else. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that you are His servant and Messenger. He said: ""Where do you settle, O Jarir?"" He said: In the environs of Bishah, between salam and arak trees, plain and rugged ground, sour herbage and tangled thickets, date-palms and wild olives, lote-trees and wormwood, star-wort and tamarisks; our winter is spring, our spring is swift, our water is inaccessible - its drawer cannot be matched, its morning drinker does not weary, its pasturing flocks do not stray. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Indeed, the best water is al-shabim, the best wealth is sheep, and the best pasture is arak and salam; when it fails to leaf it is lajin, when eaten it is little milky, and when it falls it is darin."" Jarir said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me about the lowest heaven and the nethermost earth. He said: ""Allah created the lowest heaven from the folded surge, ringed it with stars, made them missiles against the devils and a guard against every accursed devil; and He created the nethermost earth from foaming froth and abundant water, and placed it upon a rock on the back of a whale, from which water issues - were a breach to open in it, the earth and all upon it would gush forth. Glory be to the Creator of light."" Jarir said: O Messenger of Allah, extend your hand that I may pledge to you. He extended his hand, and said: O Messenger of Allah, what shall I affirm? He said: ""You affirm that there is no god but Allah, and that I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger."" He said: Yes. He said: ""And that you establish prayer and pay zakat."" He said: Yes. He said: ""And that you fast Ramadan and perform pilgrimage to the House."" He said: Yes. He said: ""And that you bathe from major impurity."" He said: Yes. He said: ""And that you hear and obey, even if it be an Abyssinian slave."" He said: Yes."
"23227","4 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muqatil al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn 'Umar al-Ahmasi narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Jarir, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent, I came to him and he said: ""For what have you come, O Jarir?"" I said: I have come to accept Islam at your hands, O Messenger of Allah. He said: ""To the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah, and that you establish the prescribed prayer."" Then he cast his cloak to me, then turned to his Companions and said: ""When the noble of a people comes to you, honor him."""
"23228","The Islam of Abu Dharr al-Ghifari, may Allah be pleased with him. 5 - Abu 'Abd al-Malik Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Qurashi al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'A'idh narrated to us, he said: Abu Tarafah 'Abbad ibn al-Rayyan al-Lakhmi narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Urwah ibn Ruwaym al-Lakhmi say: 'Amir ibn Ludayn al-Ash'ari narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Layla al-Ash'ari say: Abu Dharr narrated to me, he said: The first thing that called me to Islam was that we were a Bedouin people, and famine struck us; so I carried my mother and my brother - his name was Unays - to in-laws of ours in upper Najd. When we settled with them they honored us. When a man of the quarter saw that, he went to my maternal uncle and said: Do you know that Unays visits your wife in your absence? He said: It weighed upon his heart. I returned from pasturing the camels and found him gloomy, weeping. I said: What is your weeping, O maternal uncle? He informed me of the news. I said: Allah has barred that; we abhor indecency even though the times have harmed us, and you have marred the good cheer with which you first met us; there is no way to staying together. So I carried my mother and my brother until we settled near Makkah. My brother said: I am challenging a man over water in poetry - and he was a poet. I said: Do not do it. But obstinacy drove him until he contended with Durayd ibn al-Simmah, flock against flock. By Allah, Durayd that day was more poetic than my brother. So we sought judgment from al-Khansa'; she judged for my brother against Durayd - because Durayd had proposed to her to her father, and she said: An old man, I have no need of him - so she held that against him. We joined his flock to ours, and we had a large herd. Then I came to Makkah and began at al-Safa, where there were men of Quraysh; it had reached me that there was a Sabian, madman, poet, or sorcerer there. I said: Where is this one you claim? They said: He is where you see. I turned to him, not going beyond them a stone's throw, when by Allah they fell upon me with every stone, bone, and clod, and stained me with my blood. I came to the House and entered between the curtains and the structure, and I remained shut in it for thirty days, eating and drinking nothing but Zamzam water. Then on a bright moonlit night, two women of Khuza'ah came and circumambulated the House, then mentioned Isaf and Na'ilah - two idols they used to worship. I stuck my head out from under the curtains and said: Load one of them upon the other. They grew angry and said: By Allah, had our men been present you would not have spoken thus. Then they turned away. I went out following their tracks until they met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: ""Who are you two? From where are you? From where have you come? And what has brought you?"" They informed him of the news. He said: ""Where did you leave the Sabian?"" They said: We left him between the curtains and the structure. He said to them: ""Did he say anything to you?"" They said: Yes, he spoke a word that fills the mouth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled, then they slipped away. I came forward until I reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and greeted him thereupon. He said: ""Who are you? From whom are you? From where have you come? And what has brought you?"" So I began informing him of the news. He said: ""What did you eat and drink from?"" I said: From Zamzam water. He said: ""It is indeed nourishing food."" With him was Abu Bakr - may Allah be pleased with him - who said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to host him. He said: ""Yes."" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out walking, and Abu Bakr took my hand until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped at the door of Abu Bakr. Then Abu Bakr entered his house and brought raisins of Ta'if raisins, throwing them to us handful by handful, and we ate of them until we were full. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: ""O Abu Dharr."" I said: At your service. He said: ""A land has been shown to me, a land of date-palms; I deem it none but Tihamah. Go forth to your people and call them to what you have entered."" He said: So I went out until I reached my mother and brother and informed them of the news; they said: We have no aversion to the religion you have entered. So they accepted Islam. Then we went out until we reached Madinah, and I informed my people; they said: We have believed you, but we shall meet Muhammad. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, we met him; Ghifar said to him: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Dharr has informed us of what you informed him, and we have accepted Islam and bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah. Then Aslam of Khuza'ah advanced and said: O Messenger of Allah, we have desired and entered into what our brothers and allies have entered. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Aslam - may Allah grant them safety - and Ghifar - may Allah forgive them."" Then Abu Bakr took my hand and said: O Abu Dharr. I said: At your service, O Abu Bakr. He said: Did you used to worship in your pre-Islamic days? I said: Yes; you would have seen me standing at the sun, ever praying until its heat hurt me, then falling as though a small hidden bird. He said to me: In which direction did you turn? I said: I know not, except where Allah directed me, until Allah brought Islam upon me."
"23229","The Islam of Zayd ibn Sa'nah, may Allah be pleased with him. 6 - Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Abbar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari al-'Asqalani narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hamzah ibn Yusuf ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Salam narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: When Allah the Exalted willed the guidance of Zayd ibn Sa'nah, Zayd ibn Sa'nah said: There is nothing of the signs of prophethood but I recognized it in the face of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when I looked at him, except two I had not tested in him: his forbearance precedes his anger, and the severity of ignorance against him only increases him in forbearance. I would be gentle with him to mix with him and know his forbearance from his anger. Zayd ibn Sa'nah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out one day from the chambers with 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him. A man on his mount, like a Bedouin, came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, Busra, the village of Banu so-and-so, have accepted Islam and entered Islam; I had told them that if they accepted Islam, provision would come to them. A year of famine, hardship, and lack of rain has struck them, and I fear, O Messenger of Allah, that they will leave Islam out of greed just as they entered it out of greed. If you see fit to send them something to aid them with, do so. He looked at a man beside him - I think it was 'Ali - who said: O Messenger of Allah, nothing remains of it. Zayd ibn Sa'nah said: So I drew near to him and said: O Muhammad, will you sell me known dates from the orchard of Banu so-and-so for such-and-such a term? He said: ""No, O Jew; but I shall sell you known dates for such-and-such a term, without naming the orchard of Banu so-and-so."" I said: Yes. So he transacted with me, and I loosened my purse and gave him eighty mithqals of gold for known dates for such-and-such a term; he gave it to the man and said: ""Hasten to them and aid them with it."" Zayd ibn Sa'nah said: Two or three days before the term fell due, I came to him, seized the collars of his shirt and cloak, and looked at him with a harsh face. I said to him: Will you not settle my right, O Muhammad? By Allah, I have known you, O Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib, to be delayers. I had knowledge through mixing with you. I looked at 'Umar ibn al-Khattab - may Allah be pleased with him - and his eyes were rolling in his face like the circling orb. Then he cast his glance at me and said: O enemy of Allah, do you say to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what I hear, and do to him what I see? By the One who sent him with the truth, were it not for what I fear of his strength, I would strike your head with my sword. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was looking at 'Umar in calmness, deliberation, and smiling. Then he said: ""O 'Umar, he and I were in greater need of other than this: that you command me to repay well, and command him to demand well. Go with him, O 'Umar, and give him his right, and add twenty sa's of dates in place of your frightening him."" Zayd said: So 'Umar - may Allah be pleased with him - went with me and gave me my right and added twenty sa's of dates. I said: What is this addition, O 'Umar? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to add to you in place of my frightening you. I said: Do you know me, O 'Umar? He said: No; who are you? I said: I am Zayd ibn Sa'nah. He said: The rabbi? I said: The rabbi. He said: What prompted you to do to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what you did and say to him what you said? I said: O 'Umar, there was nothing of the signs of prophethood but I recognized it in the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when I looked at him, except two I had not tested in him: his forbearance precedes his anger, and the severity of ignorance against him only increases him in forbearance. I have now tested them. I inform you, O 'Umar, that I am pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as Prophet; and I call you to witness that half my wealth - for I am among the wealthiest of them - is charity upon the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 'Umar said: Or upon some of them, for you cannot encompass them all. I said: And upon some of them. 'Umar and Zayd returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Zayd said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. He believed in him, affirmed him, pledged to him, and witnessed many campaigns with him. Then Zayd - may Allah be pleased with him - died in the campaign of Tabuk, advancing, not retreating, may Allah be pleased with him."
"23230","The Islam of 'Abd Allah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him. 7 - Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas al-Mu'addib, freedman of Banu Hashim, al-Baghdadi, narrated to us, 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit and Humayd, from Anas, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah, 'Abd Allah ibn Salam was informed of his coming while he was among his date-palms. He came to him and said: I shall ask you about things none knows but a prophet; if you inform me of them I shall believe in you, and if you do not know them I shall know you are not a prophet. He said: ""And what are they?"" He asked him about resemblance, about the first thing the people of Paradise eat, and about the first thing that gathers mankind. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Jibril - peace be upon him - informed me of them just now."" He said: He is the enemy of the Jews. He said: As for resemblance - when the man's fluid precedes the woman's fluid, it carries the resemblance; when the woman's fluid precedes the man's fluid, it carries the resemblance. As for the first thing the people of Paradise eat, it is the increased lobe of the whale's liver. As for the first thing in which mankind shall be gathered, it is a fire coming from the east that gathers them to the west. So he believed and said: I bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah. 'Abd Allah ibn Salam said: O Messenger of Allah, the Jews are a slanderous people; if they hear of my Islam they will slander me. Hide me with you and send to them and ask them about me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hid him and sent to them; they came. He said: ""What kind of man is 'Abd Allah ibn Salam among you?"" They said: Our best and son of our best, our master and son of our master. He said: ""What if he accepts Islam - would you accept Islam?"" They said: May Allah protect him from that. He said: ""O 'Abd Allah ibn Salam, come out to them and inform them."" He came out to them and said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. They said: Rather our worst and son of our worst, our ignorant one and son of our ignorant one. Ibn Salam said: I already told you, O Messenger of Allah, that the Jews are a slanderous people."
"23231","The Islam of Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him. 8 - Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki narrated to us, Qays ibn Hafs al-Darimi narrated to us, he said: Maslamah ibn 'Alqamah al-Mazini narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Salamah al-'Ijli, he said: A nephew of mine from the desert called Qudamah came to me and said to me - my nephew said to me: I would love to meet Salman al-Farisi and greet him. So we set out and found him at al-Mada'in, when he was over twenty thousand, and we found him on a couch weaving palm leaves. We greeted him. I said: O Abu 'Abd Allah, this is a nephew of mine who has come to me from the desert and wishes to greet you. He said: And upon him be peace and Allah's mercy. I said: He claims he loves you. He said: May Allah love him. He said: We conversed, and we said to him: O Abu 'Abd Allah, will you not narrate to us of your origin and from whom you are? He said: As for my origin and from whom I am, I am a man of the people of Ramahurmuz; we were a Magian people. A Christian man of the people of the Jazirah, whose mother was of us, came to us; he settled among us and took a monastery among us. I was in the Persian school, and a boy with me in the school would ever come beaten, weeping, for his parents had beaten him. I said to him one day: What makes you weep? He said: My parents beat me. I said: Why do they beat you? He said: I go to the master of this monastery; when they learn of that they beat me. If you were to come to him you would hear wondrous discourse from him. I said: Take me with you. We came to him, and he narrated to us of the beginning of creation, and of the beginning of the creation of the heavens, earth, Paradise, and Fire. He said: He narrated to us wondrous hadiths. I would frequent him with him, and some boys of the school noticed us and began coming with us. When the people of the village saw that, they came to him and said: O man, you have settled among us and seen nothing in our neighborhood but good, yet we see our boys frequenting you; we fear you will corrupt them against us. Leave us. He said: Yes. He said to that boy who used to come to him: Come out with me. He said: I cannot do that; you know my parents' severity toward me. I said: But I shall come out with you - and I was an orphan with no father. So I went out with him, taking the mountain of Ramahurmuz, walking and trusting in Allah and eating of the fruit of the trees, until we reached the Jazirah, arriving at Nasibin. My companion said to me: O Salman, here are a people who are the worshippers of the people of the earth, and I would love to meet them. He said: We came to them on a Sunday when they had gathered; my companion greeted them, and they greeted him and rejoiced at him, saying: Where has your absence been? He said: I was among brethren of mine from Persia. We conversed as we conversed. Then my companion said to me: Rise, O Salman, let us go. I said: No, leave me with these. He said: You cannot bear what these bear; these fast from Sunday to Sunday and do not sleep this night. Among them was a man of royal descent who had abandoned kingship and entered worship. I stayed among them until evening; they began going one by one to his cave wherein he stayed. In the evening, that man of royal descent said: This boy - do not leave him; let one of you take him. They said: Take him yourself. He said: Come, O Salman. He took me with him to his cave wherein he stayed, and said: O Salman, here is bread and condiment; eat when hungry, fast when energetic, pray as seems good to you, and sleep when weary. Then he rose to his prayer and spoke to me no more than that, nor looked at me. Grief seized me those seven days, with none speaking to me, until Sunday came; he turned to me, and we went to their gathering place. He said: They gather every Sunday, breaking fast therein, meeting one another and greeting one another, then not meeting until its like. He said: We returned to our dwelling, and he said to me as he said the first time: Here is bread and condiment; eat of it when hungry, fast when energetic, pray as seems good to you, and sleep when weary. Then he entered his prayer, not turning to me nor speaking to me until the next Sunday. Grief seized me, and I spoke to myself of fleeing; I said: I shall bear it one or two or three Sundays. When Sunday came we returned to them; they broke fast and gathered. He said to them: I wish to go to Bayt al-Maqdis. They said: What do you want with that? He said: I have not seen it for long. They said: We fear some mishap will befall you and another than us will take charge of you, while we love to take charge of you. He said: I have not seen it for long. When I heard him mention that, I rejoiced; I said: We shall travel and meet people, and the grief I felt shall leave me. We set out, he and I. He would fast from Sunday to Sunday, pray the whole night, and walk the day; when we camped he would rise praying. That remained his wont until we reached Bayt al-Maqdis. At its gate was a crippled man begging people; he said: Give me. He said: I have nothing with me. We entered Bayt al-Maqdis; when the people of Bayt al-Maqdis saw him they rejoiced at him and were glad of him. He said to them: This boy of mine - treat him well. They took me and fed me bread and meat, while he entered prayer and did not turn to me until the next Sunday. Then he turned and said to me: O Salman, I wish to lay my head; when the shade reaches such-and-such place, wake me. He laid his head and slept; the shade reached what he said, but I did not wake him out of pity for him for the striving and toil he tasted. He awoke startled and said: O Salman, did I not tell you: when the shade reaches such-and-such place, wake me? I said: Yes indeed, but what kept me from that was pity for what I saw of your wont. He said: Woe to you, O Salman; I dislike that anything of the age should pass me wherein I have not done good for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. Then he said: Know, O Salman, that the best religion today is Christianity. I said: And shall there be after today a religion better than Christianity? - a word cast upon my tongue. He said: Yes; a prophet is soon to be sent who eats the gift and does not eat charity; between his shoulders is the seal of prophethood. When you meet him, follow him and affirm him. I said: Even if he commands me to abandon Christianity? He said: Yes, for he is a prophet who commands only truth and says only truth. By Allah, were I to meet him and he commanded me to fall into the Fire, I would fall into it. Then we left Bayt al-Maqdis, passing that crippled man; he said to him: You entered and did not give me, and now you leave - so give me. He turned and found none around him. He said: Give me your hand. He took his hand and said: Rise by Allah's permission. He rose sound and whole, heading toward his people. My gaze followed him in wonder at what I saw. My companion left, hastening his walk, and I followed him. A party of Kalb Bedouins met me and took me captive; they carried me on a camel and bound me. Traders passed me along until I fell to Madinah. A man of the Ansar bought me and placed me in a walled garden of date-palms of his; I was in it. He said: Thence I learned the work of palm leaves: I would buy palm leaves for a dirham, work them, sell them for two dirhams, return one to the palm leaves and spend a dirham, loving to eat of my hand's work - while he was then a commander over twenty thousand. It reached us while in Madinah that a man had come forth at Makkah claiming Allah the Exalted had sent him. We stayed as long as Allah willed us to stay, then he emigrated to us and came to us. I said: By Allah, I shall surely test him. I went to the market and bought camel meat for a dirham, then cooked it, making a bowl of tharid; I carried it on my shoulder until I brought it to him and placed it before him. He said: ""What is this - charity or gift?"" I said: Rather charity. He said to his Companions: ""Eat in Allah's name,"" and refrained and did not eat. I waited days, then bought meat likewise for a dirham and made the like; I carried it until I brought it to him and placed it before him. He said: ""What is this - gift or charity?"" I said: Rather a gift. He said to his Companions: ""Eat in Allah's name,"" and ate with them. I said: This, by Allah, eats the gift and does not eat charity. I looked between his shoulders and saw the seal of prophethood like a pigeon's egg, so I accepted Islam. Then I said to him one day: O Messenger of Allah, what manner of people are the Christians? He said: ""There is no good in them."" I loved them with intense love for the striving I had seen of them. Then I asked him again after days: O Messenger of Allah, what manner of people are the Christians? He said: ""There is no good in them, nor in whoever loves them."" I said to myself: By Allah, I love them. He said: That was, by Allah, when he was dispatching raiding parties and unsheathing the sword - one party entering, another leaving, the sword dripping. I said: He shall now speak of me that I love them, and send to me and strike my neck. So I sat in the house. One day the messenger came to me and said: ""O Salman, answer."" I said: Who? He said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."" I said: This, by Allah, is what I feared. I said: Yes; go, until I catch up with you. He said: ""No, by Allah, until you come."" I spoke to myself that were he to leave I would flee. He took me, and I ended at him. When he saw me he smiled and said to me: ""O Salman, rejoice, for Allah has relieved you."" Then he recited to me these verses: {Those to whom We gave the Book before it - they believe in it * And when it is recited to them they say: We believe in it; it is the truth from our Lord; we were Muslims before it * Those shall be given their reward twice for what they patiently endured, and they repel evil with good, and spend of what We provided them * And when they hear vain talk they turn from it and say: To us our deeds and to you your deeds; peace be upon you; we seek not the ignorant}. I said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I heard him say: Were I to meet him and he commanded me to be cast into the Fire, I would be cast into it; he is a prophet who says only truth and commands only truth."
"23232","9 - Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Quddus narrated to us, he said: 'Ubayd al-Mukattib narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Tufayl narrated to me, he said: Salman al-Farisi narrated to me, he said: I was a man of the people of Jayy, and the people of my village worshipped piebald horses; I knew they were upon nothing. It was said to me: What you seek is in the West. So I set out until I reached al-Mawsil, asking for the best man there; I was directed to a man in a cell - then he mentioned the like. At its end he said: I said to my master: Sell me myself. He said: Yes, on condition that you plant for me a hundred date-palms; when they grow, bring me the weight of a date-stone of gold. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Purchase yourself for what he asked of you, and bring me a bucket of water from the well from which you used to water the date-palms."" He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for me therein, then I watered them. By Allah, I planted a hundred date-palms and did not leave one of them but it grew. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him that the date-palms had grown. He gave me a piece of gold; I went with it and placed it in the scale's pan, placing a date-stone on the other side. By Allah, the piece of gold did not lift from the ground. He said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he freed me."
"23233","The Islam of Abu Qirsafah, whose name is Jandarh ibn Khayshanah, may Allah be pleased with him. 10 - Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah al-'Asqalani narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn 'Ali ibn al-Haysam narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Sayyar narrated to us, he said: 'Azzah bint 'Iyad ibn Abi Qirsafah informed me that she heard her grandfather Abu Qirsafah, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: The beginning of my Islam was that I was an orphan between my mother and my maternal aunt, leaning most toward my maternal aunt. I would pasture some ewes of mine, and my maternal aunt would often say to me: O my son, do not pass by this man - meaning the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - lest he beguile and mislead you. I would go out until I reached the pasture, leave my ewes, then come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), remaining with him hearing from him. Then I would return with my sheep lean, their udders dry. My maternal aunt said to me: Why are your sheep dry-uddered? I said: I know not. Then I returned to him the second day, doing as on the first day, except that I heard him say: ""O people, emigrate and hold to Islam, for emigration does not cease so long as jihad continues."" Then I returned with my sheep as on the first day. Then I returned to him on the third day, remaining with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hearing from him until I accepted Islam, pledged, and shook his hand with my hand. I complained to him of my maternal aunt's affair and my little sheep's affair. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: ""Bring me the sheep."" I brought them to him; he wiped their udders and backs and supplicated for blessing in them, and they filled with fat and milk. When I entered upon my maternal aunt with them, she said: O my son, pasture thus. I said: O maternal aunt, I pastured nowhere but where I pasture every day; but let me inform you of my story. I informed her of the story, of my coming to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and informed her of his conduct and speech. My mother and maternal aunt said: Take us to him. I went, I and my mother and maternal aunt, and they accepted Islam and pledged the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), shaking him. This is what there was of the Islam of Abu Qirsafah and his emigration to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Ziyad said: Abu Qirsafah used to dwell in the land of Tihamah."
"23234","The Islam of 'Amr ibn 'Abasah al-Sulami - may Allah be pleased with him - kunya Abu Nujayh. 11 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid ibn Maymun ibn Mihran narrated to us, he said: 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar narrated to us, he said: Shaddad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Dimashqi narrated to me, he said: Abu Umamah al-Bahili narrated to us, he said: I said to 'Amr ibn 'Abasah: For what are you called a fourth of Islam? He said: In pre-Islamic times I saw people upon misguidance, seeing the idols as nothing. Then I heard men reporting news at Makkah, narrating reports. So I mounted my mount until I reached Makkah, and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in hiding, while his people were bold against him. I sought him gently and entered upon him. I said: What are you? He said: ""I am a prophet."" I said: And what is a prophet? He said: ""The Messenger of Allah."" I said: Has Allah sent you? He said: ""Yes."" I said: With what were you sent? He said: ""With the Oneness of Allah, associating nothing with Him, breaking the idols, and joining kinship."" I said: Who is with you upon this? He said: ""A freeman and a slave"" - with him being Abu Bakr ibn Abi Quhafah and a slave, Bilal, freedman of Abu Bakr. I said: I am with you, following you. He said: ""You cannot do that this day of yours; but return to your people, and when you hear that I have prevailed, join me."" So I returned to my people, having accepted Islam. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out emigrating to Madinah, and I kept seeking news until a party came from Yathrib. I said: What did this Makkan man who came to you do? They said: His people sought to kill him but could not; he was shielded from them, and people hastened to him swiftly. 'Amr ibn 'Abasah said: So I mounted my mount until I reached Madinah, entered upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, do you know me? He said: ""Yes; are you not the one who came to me at Makkah?"" I said: Yes indeed. Teach me of what Allah taught you. He said: When you pray the dawn prayer, refrain from prayer until the sun rises; when it rises, do not pray until it is high, for it rises between the two horns of Satan, and then the disbelievers prostrate to it. When it has risen a spear's length, pray, for prayer is witnessed and attended, until the spear stands upright with the shadow. Then refrain from prayer, for then Hell is stoked. When the shade inclines, pray, for prayer is witnessed and attended, until you pray 'Asr. When you have prayed 'Asr, refrain from prayer until the sun sets, for it sets between the two horns of Satan, and then the disbelievers prostrate to it. He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, inform me about ablution. He said: ""There is no man among you who draws near his ablution water, then rinses his mouth and sniffs water, but his sins come out of his mouth and nostrils with the water when he blows it out. Then he washes his face as Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commanded him, but the sins of his face come out from the edges of his beard with the water. Then he washes his hands to the elbows as Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commanded him, but the sins of his hands come out from the tips of his fingers with the water. Then he wipes his head as Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commanded him, but the sins of his head come out from the ends of his hair with the water. Then he washes his feet to the ankles as Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commanded him, but the sins of his feet come out from the tips of his toes with the water. Then he stands, praises Allah and extols Him with what He deserves, then bows two bowings, but he turns away from his sins as on the day his mother bore him."" Abu Umamah said: O 'Amr ibn 'Abasah, consider what you say - did you hear all this from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? Is a man given all this in his standing? 'Amr ibn 'Abasah said: O Abu Umamah, my age has grown, my bones have thinned, my term has neared, and I have no need to lie against Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Had I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but once or twice or thrice - I have heard it from him seven times or more."
"23235","The Islam of 'Amr ibn al-'As, may Allah be pleased with him. 12 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdous ibn Kamil al-Sarraj narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Rashid, freedman of Habib ibn Abi Aws, from Habib ibn Abi Aws, he said: 'Amr ibn al-'As narrated to me from his mouth to my ear, he said: When we returned from al-Ahzab, from the Trench, I gathered men of Quraysh and said to them: Do you see my view and hear from me? They said: Yes. I said: I see the affair of Muhammad rising over affairs with a mighty rise, and I have seen a view - what do you think of it? They said: And what is it? I said: I see that we join al-Najashi; if Muhammad prevails over our people, we shall be with al-Najashi, to be under his hand being dearer to us than to be under Muhammad's hand; and if our people prevail, we are of them, they know us, and nothing but good shall come to us from them. They said: This is indeed a view. I said: Gather gifts to gift him - the dearest gift to him from our land being leather. He said: We gathered much leather for him, then set out until we reached him. By Allah, while we were with him, 'Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri came - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had sent him to him concerning Ja'far and his companions. He said: They entered upon him, then left him. I said to them - meaning his companions: This is 'Amr ibn Umayyah; if we enter upon him, present our gifts to him, and ask him for him and he gives him to me, I shall kill him. If I do that, Quraysh will see that I have sufficed on their behalf when I kill the messenger of Allah's Messenger Muhammad. He said: I entered upon him, prostrating to him as we used to do with him. He said: Welcome to you; have you gifted me things from your land? I said: Yes; I have gifted you, O king, much leather. He said: I brought it near to him, and he desired and admired it. I said: O king, I saw a man leave from you, the messenger of a man who is our enemy; give him to me that I may kill him, for he has struck down our nobles and best. He said: He grew angry, stretched his hand, and struck his nose - his own nose - a blow I thought he had broken it. He said: Had the earth split for me that I might enter it, out of fear of him. I said: O king, by Allah, had I thought you disliked this, I would not have asked you it. He said: Do you ask me to give you a man to kill him, while he is the messenger of a man to whom comes the Greatest Namus who used to come to Musa? I said to him: Is it thus? He said: Yes. Then he said: Woe to you, O 'Amr, obey me and follow him; by Allah, he is upon the truth, and he shall surely prevail over whoever opposes him, as Musa - peace be upon him - prevailed over Pharaoh and his hosts. I said to him: Will you pledge to him for me upon Islam? He said: Yes. He extended his hand, and I pledged to him upon Islam. Then I left him for my companions, my view of 'Amr's view having changed from what it was, concealing my Islam from my companions. Then I set out heading for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to accept Islam. I met Khalid ibn al-Walid - that being shortly before the Conquest - coming from Makkah. I said: Where to, O Abu Sulayman? He said: By Allah, the course has become straight; the man is indeed upon the truth, and I go to accept Islam. I said: And I too. We reached Madinah and came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Khalid advanced, accepted Islam, and pledged. I advanced and said: And I pledge - mentioning what had preceded of my sin, not mentioning what came after. He said: ""Pledge, for Islam effaces what was before it, and emigration effaces what was before it."""
"23236","13 - Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Sarraj al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from 'Umayr ibn Ishaq, he said: Ja'far ibn Abi Talib sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Do you permit me to come to a land wherein I worship Allah fearing none? He said: He permitted him, and he came to al-Najashi. 'Umayr said: 'Amr ibn al-'As narrated to me, he said: When I saw his place, I envied him; I shall surely confront this one and his companions. I came to al-Najashi, entered upon him, and said: In your land is a man whose cousin is in our land; he claims there is for people but one god. If you do not kill him and his companions, neither I nor any of my companions shall ever again bring you this semen. He said: Call him. I said: He will not come with him, so send a messenger with me. He said: He came, and we ended at the door; I called out: Do you permit 'Amr ibn al-'As? He called from behind me: Do you permit the party of Allah? He heard his voice and permitted him first. He and his companions entered, then he permitted me and I sat. He mentioned where his seat was from the couch. He said: I went until I sat before him, placing him behind me, placing between every two of his companions a man of my companions. Al-Najashi said: Najjiru - 'Umayr said: meaning, speak. I said: In your land is a man whose cousin is in our land, claiming there is for people but one god. If you do not kill him and his companions, neither I nor any of my companions shall ever bring you this semen. Ja'far said: My cousin speaks truth, and I am upon his religion. He shouted a shout, saying: Awwah, until I said: Why does the son of the Abyssinian woman not speak? Then he said: Is it a Namus like the Namus of Musa? He said: What does he say of 'Isa son of Maryam? He said: He says: He is Allah's spirit and word. He took something from the ground and said: He has not erred in his affair by even this much. By Allah, were it not for my kingdom I would follow you. He said to me: I would not care whether you or any of your companions ever come to me. O man, you are safe in my land; whoever strikes you I shall kill, whoever reviles you I shall chastise. He said to his chamberlain: Whenever this one seeks your permission, permit him, unless I am with my family - then inform him I am with my family; if he refuses, permit him. So we parted, none I met being dearer to me than Ja'far. He met me on a road once; I looked behind him and saw none, looked behind me and saw none. I drew near to him and said to him: Know that I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. He said: Allah has guided you, so stand firm. He left me and went. I came to my companions as though they had witnessed him with me; they took a striped garment or cloth and cast it upon me until they smothered me with it. He said: I kept putting my head out from this side once and this side once, until I escaped with not a rag upon me. I passed an Abyssinian woman and took her head-cover, placing it upon my nakedness. I came to Ja'far and entered upon him. He said: What is wrong with you? I said: Everything of mine was taken; not a rag was left upon me. I came to an Abyssinian woman and took her head-cover, placing it upon my nakedness. He set out, and I set out with him, until we ended at the king's door. Ja'far said to his chamberlain: Seek permission for me. He said: He is with his family. He said: Seek permission for me. He permitted him. He said: 'Amr has followed me upon my religion. He said: No. He said: Yes indeed. He said to a man: Go with him; whatever he does, let him say nothing but you write it. He said: He came, and I said: Yes. I kept saying and he kept writing, until I wrote everything, even the cup. Had I wished to take anything of their wealth to my wealth, I would have done so."
"23237","The account of Ja'far ibn Abi Talib with 'Amr ibn al-'As and 'Umarah ibn al-Walid before al-Najashi. 14 - Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahim al-Dibaji al-Tustari narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Adam al-Massisi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn 'Amr al-Bajali al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Mujalid ibn Sa'id al-Hamdani narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Talib, from his father, he said: Quraysh sent 'Amr ibn al-'As and 'Umarah ibn al-Walid with a gift from Abu Sufyan to al-Najashi. They said to him, while we were with him: There have been sent to you some of their lowliest and most foolish; hand them to us. He said: No, until I hear their speech. He sent to us, saying: What do you say? We said: Our people worship idols, and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sent to us a Messenger; we believed in him and affirmed him. Al-Najashi said to them: Are they slaves of yours? They said: No. He said: Do you have a debt upon them? They said: No. He said: Then leave their way free. We left him. 'Amr ibn al-'As said: These say of 'Isa other than what you say. He said: If they do not say of 'Isa as I say, I shall not leave them in my land an hour of the day. He said: He sent to us - the second summons being harder upon us than the first - saying: What does your companion say of 'Isa son of Maryam? We said: He says: He is Allah's spirit and word, cast to the chaste Virgin. He said: He sent, saying: Call so-and-so the priest and so-and-so the monk. Some of them came to him. He said: What do you say of 'Isa son of Maryam? They said: You are the most learned of us - what do you say? Al-Najashi said, taking something from the ground, then said: Thus is 'Isa; he has not exceeded what these have said by even this much. Then he said to them: Does anyone harm you? They said: Yes. He ordered a caller, who called: Whoever harms any of them shall be fined four dirhams. Then he said: Does that suffice you? We said: No. So he doubled it. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) emigrated to Madinah and prevailed there, we said to him: Our companion has set out to Madinah, emigrated, and killed those of whom we told you; we wish to depart, so provision us. He said: Yes. He carried us, provisioned us, and gave us. Then he said: Inform your companion of what I did for you; this messenger of mine is with you, and I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that he is Allah's Messenger; tell him to seek forgiveness for me. He said: We set out until we reached Madinah; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me, embraced me, and said: ""I know not whether I rejoice more at the conquest of Khaybar or the coming of Ja'far."" Then he sat, and the messenger of al-Najashi stood and said: Here is Ja'far - ask him what our companion did with him. I said: Yes; he did with us - he did such-and-such, carried us and loaded us, provisioned us, aided us, bore witness that there is no god but Allah and that you are His Messenger, and said: Tell him to seek forgiveness for me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, performed ablution, then supplicated three times: ""O Allah, forgive al-Najashi."" The Muslims said: Amen. Ja'far said: I said to the messenger: Go and inform your companion of what you saw of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"23238","15 - Hafs ibn 'Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-'Ala' ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Talhah ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Qatadah, he said: When the delegation of al-Najashi came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood serving them himself. He said: ""They were honoring to our companions."""
"23239","16 - Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi the judge narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Umm Salamah, from Ja'far ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him: that al-Najashi asked him: What is your religion? He said: ""There has been sent among us a Messenger whose tongue, truthfulness, and fidelity we know. He called us to worship Allah alone, associating nothing with Him, and to cast off what our people and others worshipped besides Him. He commands us to right and forbids us wrong; he commanded us with prayer, fasting, charity, and joining kinship. He called us to what we know, reciting to us a revelation come from Allah, like which there is none other. We affirmed him, believed in him, and knew that what he brought is truth from Allah. Since then we parted from our people; they harmed and overpowered us. When they reached of us what we dislike and we could not defend ourselves from them, we came out to your land, preferring you over all besides you."" Al-Najashi said: Go, for you are siyum in my land - meaning, safe; whoever reviles you shall be fined."""
"23240","17 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) announced al-Najashi to his Companions while in Madinah; they prayed behind him, and he prayed over him, takbir four times."
"23241","18 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Ata' informed me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""A righteous man, Ashamah, has died today; come, form rows and pray over him."" So we formed rows, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, we with him."
"23242","The Islam of Qays ibn 'Asim al-Minqari, may Allah be pleased with him. 19 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdous ibn Kamil al-Sarraj narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ziyad al-Jassas narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: Qays ibn 'Asim al-Minqari narrated to me, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when he saw me I heard him say: ""This is the master of the people of the tents."" He said: When I settled, I came to him and began serving him. He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, wealth for which there shall be no consequence upon me, of injustice befalling me and numerous dependents. He said: ""Excellent wealth is forty; more is sixty; woe to the owners of two hundred, except one who gives in its milch and its aid, lends its back, slaughters its fat, feeding the content and the beggar."" He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, how noble and excellent are these manners! O Prophet of Allah, the valley I am in cannot be settled for the multitude of my camels. He said: ""How do you do?"" He said: The camels go out in the morning and the people go out; whoever wishes takes a camel's head and goes with it. He said: ""What do you do with lending the back?"" I said: I do not lend the small, nor borrow the aged, worn-out great. He said: ""Is your wealth dearer to you or your freedmen's wealth?"" I said: My wealth is dearer to me than my freedmen's wealth. He said: ""Of your wealth is only what you ate and consumed, wore and wore out, or gave and sent forth; otherwise it is for your freedmen."" He said: I said: By Allah, if I remain I shall surely consume their number. Al-Hasan said: By Allah, he did. When death came to Qays, he said: O my sons, take from me; none is more sincere to you than I. When I die, make your elders masters, and do not make your young masters, lest people deem you foolish; take it lightly upon yourselves. Upon you is to set wealth aright, for it alerts the noble and suffices against the mean. Beware asking, for it is the last earning of a man; none asks but he leaves his earning. When I die, do not wail over me, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid wailing. Shroud me in the garments wherein I used to pray and fast. When you bury me, do not bury me in a place where anyone looks upon me, for there were skirmishes between me and Bakr ibn Wa'il in pre-Islamic times, and I fear they will exhume me and do therein that which shall take your religion and world. Al-Hasan said: He advised them in life and advised them in death."
"23243","The account of Tha'labah ibn Hatib. 20 - Abu Yazid Yusuf ibn Yazid al-Qaratis narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Mu'an ibn Rifa'ah narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, that Tha'labah ibn Hatib al-Ansari came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah to grant me wealth. He said: ""Woe to you, O Tha'labah; little for which you render thanks is better for you than much you cannot bear."" Then he returned to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah to grant me wealth. He said: ""Woe to you, O Tha'labah; do you wish to be like the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? By Allah, had I asked Allah to make the mountains gold for me, I would have asked."" Then he returned to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah to grant me wealth; if He gives me wealth I shall surely give every rightful one his right. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O Allah, grant Tha'labah wealth; O Allah, grant Tha'labah wealth."" He said: He took wealth, and it grew as worms grow, until the lanes of Madinah straitened for it. He withdrew with it, attending prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then going out to it. Then it grew until the pastures of Madinah became impracticable for him; he withdrew with it, attending Friday prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then going out to it. Then it grew, and he withdrew with it, abandoning Friday and congregations. He would meet riders, asking: What news have you? What of people's affairs? Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sent down upon His Messenger {Take of their wealth charity to purify and cleanse them therewith}. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed two men over charities: a man of the Ansar and a man of Banu Salamah, writing for them the practice of charities and their ages, commanding them to take charity from people, and to pass by Tha'labah and take of his wealth's charity. They did, going to Tha'labah, reciting to him the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) letter. He said: Take charity from people; when you finish, pass by me. They did. He said: By Allah, this is nothing but the sister of the poll-tax. They went until they reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Allah, Praised be He, sent down {And of them are those who pledged to Allah: If He gives us of His bounty, we shall surely give charity} to His word {they lie}. An Ansari man, a kinsman of Tha'labah, mounted his mount until he came to Tha'labah, saying: Woe to you, O Tha'labah; you have perished - Allah has sent down concerning you such-and-such of the Qur'an. Tha'labah came, casting dust upon his head, weeping, saying: O Messenger of Allah, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not accept his charity until Allah took His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he came to Abu Bakr - may Allah be pleased with him - after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Abu Bakr, you know my standing among my people and my place with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); accept from me. He refused to accept it. Then he came to 'Umar - may Allah be pleased with him - who refused to accept from him. Then he came to 'Uthman - may Allah be pleased with him - who refused to accept from him. Then Tha'labah died in the caliphate of 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him."
"23244","The account of Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah on abandoning Friday prayer. 21 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Bukayr, from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-'Adawi al-Basri, from 'Ali ibn Zayd, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say upon his pulpit: ""O people, repent to Allah before you die, hasten with righteous deeds before you are preoccupied, join what is between you and your Lord with much remembrance of Him and much charity in secret and openly - you shall be rewarded, aided, and provided. Know that Allah has made Friday obligatory upon you, a written obligation in this station of mine, on this day of mine, in this month of mine, in this year of mine, until the Day of Resurrection, upon whoever finds a way to it. Whoever abandons it in my lifetime or after my death, denying it or belittling it, while he has a ruler, just or unjust - may Allah not unite his affair, nor bless him in his affair. Nay, he has no prayer, no zakat, no pilgrimage, no fasting, no piety, until he repents. Whoever repents, Allah turns to him. Nay, let no woman lead a man in prayer, let no Bedouin lead an emigrant, let no sinner lead a believer, except a ruler - unless he coerces him with authority, fearing his sword and whip."""
"23245","The account of Quss ibn Sa'idah al-Iyadi. 22 - Muhammad ibn al-Sari ibn Mihran al-Naqid al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Hassan al-Samti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Hajjaj al-Lakhmi narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: The delegation of 'Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: ""Which of you knows Quss ibn Sa'idah al-Iyadi?"" They said: We all know him, O Messenger of Allah. He said: ""What became of him?"" They said: He perished. He said: ""How I remember him at 'Ukaz in the sacred month, upon a red camel, addressing the people, saying: O people, gather, hear, and comprehend; whoever lives dies, whoever dies is gone, and all that is coming is coming. In the heaven is tidings, on earth are lessons; a bed laid out, a roof raised, stars swimming, seas that do not sink. Quss swore a true oath: if there be pleasure in the matter, after it shall surely be wrath. Allah has a religion dearer to Him than this world of yours upon which you are. Why do I see people going and not returning? Were they pleased with staying, so they stayed, or were they left, so they slept?"" Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Which of you recites his poetry?"" One of them recited to him verses on past generations."
"23246","The account of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) message to the kings of the earth. 23 - Hashim ibn Marthad al-Tabarani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from his father Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out to his Companions and said: ""Allah has sent me as a mercy to all people; convey from me, may Allah have mercy on you, and do not differ as the disciples differed over 'Isa - peace be upon him. He called them to what I have called you to; those near in place answered and submitted, those far in place disliked it. 'Isa son of Maryam complained of that to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and they awoke, each man of them speaking the tongue of the people to whom he was directed. 'Isa said to them: 'This is a matter Allah has decreed for you, so go and do.'"" The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We, O Messenger of Allah, shall convey from you; send us where you will. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent 'Abd Allah ibn Hudhafah al-Sahmi to Kisra, sent Salit ibn 'Amr to Hawdhah ibn 'Ali, master of al-Yamamah, sent al-'Ala' ibn al-Hadrami to al-Mundhir ibn Sawi, master of Hajar, sent 'Amr ibn al-'As to Jayfar and 'Iyadh, sons of Julanda, kings of 'Uman, sent Dihyah ibn Khalifah al-Kalbi to Caesar, sent Shuja' ibn Wahb al-Asadi to al-Mundhir ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Shimr al-Ghassani, and sent 'Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri to al-Najashi. They all returned before the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) death, except 'Amr ibn al-'As, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while he was at al-Bahrayn."
"23247","The account of Hiraql, king of Byzantium, with Abu Sufyan ibn Harb. 24 - Harun ibn Kamil al-Sarraj al-Misri narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utbah, that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas informed him that Abu Sufyan ibn Harb ibn Umayyah informed him: That Hiraql sent to him among a party of Quraysh, merchants in Syria during the truce in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had truced Abu Sufyan and the disbelievers of Quraysh. They came to him at Ilya; he summoned them in his council with the grandees of Byzantium around him, then called his interpreter, saying: Tell them: which of them is nearest in kinship to this man who claims he is a prophet? Abu Sufyan said: I am the nearest of them to him in kinship. He said: Draw near to me. They brought his companions near, placing them at his back. Then he said to his interpreter: Tell them: I shall ask this one about this man; if he lies to me, belie him. Abu Sufyan said: By Allah, were it not for shame that they report lies of me, I would have lied about him. Then he said: The first thing he asked me was: How is his lineage among you? I said: He is among us of lineage. He said: Has any of you said this word before him? I said: No. He said: Was any of his forefathers a king? I said: No. He said: Do the nobles of people follow him or their weak? I said: Rather their weak. He said: Do they increase or decrease? I said: Rather they increase. He said: Does any of them apostatize in wrath at his religion after entering it? I said: No. He said: Did you accuse him of lying? I said: No. He said: Does he betray? I said: No; we are with him in a truce, not knowing what he will do therein. He said: No word was possible for me to insert therein but this word. He said: Have you fought him? I said: Yes. He said: How was your fighting him? He said: I said: War between us and him is alternate fortunes; he gains from us and we gain from him. He said: What does he command you with? He said: I said: He says: Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, leave what your fathers worshipped, and he commands prayer, charity, chastity, and kinship. He said to the interpreter: I asked him of his lineage, and you claimed he is among you of lineage; thus are messengers sent among their people's lineage. I asked you: has any of you said this word before him? You claimed no. I said: Had any said this word before him, I would say: a man following a word said before him. I asked you: was any of his forefathers a king? You claimed no. I said: Had any of his forefathers been a king, I would say: a man seeking his forefathers' kingdom. I asked you: did you accuse him of lying before he said what he said? You claimed no. I know he would not abandon lying against people and lie against Allah. I asked you: do the nobles of people follow him or their weak? You claimed their weak are those who followed him; they are the followers of messengers. I asked you: do they increase or decrease? You claimed they increase; thus is faith until it is complete. I asked you: does any apostatize in wrath at his religion after entering it? You claimed no; thus is faith when its joy mixes with hearts. I asked you: does he betray? You claimed no; thus are messengers, they do not betray. I asked you: how is your fighting him? You claimed war between you is alternate fortunes; thus are messengers tried, then theirs is the outcome. I asked you of what he commands you with, and you claimed he commands you to worship Allah, associate nothing with Him, forbids you idol worship, and commands you prayer, charity, chastity, and kinship. If what you say is true, he shall soon possess the place of these two feet of mine. He is a prophet; I knew he would come forth, but I did not think he would be of you. Had I known I could reach him, I would seek meeting him; were I with him, I would wash his feet. He said: Then he called for the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) letter, which Dihyah al-Kalbi had borne to the chief of Busra, and it was handed to Hiraql; he read it, and in it was: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Muhammad, servant of Allah and His Messenger, to Hiraql, chief of Byzantium. Peace upon whoever follows guidance. As for what follows: I call you with the call of Islam; accept Islam and you shall be safe; accept Islam and Allah shall give you your reward twice. If you turn away, upon you is the sin of the Arisiyyin {Say: O People of the Book, come to a word equal between us and you: that we worship none but Allah, associate nothing with Him, and take none of us as lords besides Allah; if they turn away, say: Bear witness that we are Muslims}. Abu Sufyan said: When he said what he said and finished reading the letter, there was much clamor with him and voices rose. He said: We left. I said to my companions when we left: The affair of Ibn Abi Kabshah has risen, that the king of Banu al-Asfar fears him. He said: I remained certain he would prevail until Allah brought Islam upon me. Ibn Natoura, master of Ilya and Hiraql's bishop over the Christians of Syria, used to narrate that Hiraql, when he came to Ilya, awoke one day troubled in spirit. One of his patricians said to him: We have deemed your state strange. He said: Hiraql was a diviner looking at stars. He said to them when they asked him: I saw tonight, when I looked at the stars, that the king of circumcision has appeared. He said: Who circumcises among these nations? He said: The Jews circumcise. He said: Let their affair not concern you; write to the cities of your kingdom to kill whoever of the Jews is therein. While they were thus, there came to Hiraql a man sent by the king of Ghassan to inform him of the appearance of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), before the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) letter reached him. He said: Go and see: is he circumcised or not? They looked at him and told him he is circumcised. He asked him of the Arabs, and he informed him they circumcise. Hiraql said: This is the king of this nation. Then Hiraql wrote to a companion of his at Rome, his peer in knowledge, and Hiraql journeyed to Hims; he had not left Hims before his companion's letter came, agreeing with Hiraql's view on the coming-forth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and that he is a prophet. Hiraql permitted the grandees of Byzantium in a hall of his at Hims, then ordered the doors shut. Then he looked upon them, saying: O company of Byzantium, have you any desire for success, guidance, and your kingdom's continuance? Follow this Prophet. They rushed like wild asses to the doors, finding them shut. When Hiraql saw that and despaired of their faith, he said: Bring them back to me. He said: I said to you my word I said to you just now to test thereby your firmness upon your religion; I have seen of you what I love. They prostrated to him and were pleased with him. That was the last of his account."
"23248","The account of al-Bara' ibn 'Azib - may Allah be pleased with him - on the torment of the grave. 25 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, Sulayman al-A'mash narrated to me, al-Minhal ibn 'Amr narrated to me, he said: Zadham narrated to us, al-Bara' narrated to us, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the funeral of one of the Ansar. We ended at the grave, not yet niched. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began raising his head to the heaven, looking to the earth, speaking to himself. He said: Then he said: ""Seek refuge with Allah from the torment of the grave"" repeatedly. Then he said: The Muslim man, when he is at the approach of the Hereafter and severance from this world, angels from heaven appear to him, as though their faces are the sun; they sit for him as far as the eye sees, with shrouds of Paradise's shrouds and embalming of Paradise's embalming. The Angel of Death comes, sitting at his head, saying: Come forth, O tranquil soul, to forgiveness from Allah and pleasure. He said: It comes out, flowing as the drop flows from the waterskin. When he takes it, they rise to him, not leaving it in his hand the blink of an eye. He said: There comes from him like the sweetest scent of musk found upon the face of the earth. They ascend with him, passing no angel but it says: What is this good spirit? He said: They say: This is so-and-so. The doors of heaven are opened, and from every heaven its near ones escort him, until when he ends at the seventh heaven, it is said: Write his book in 'Illiyyin. He said: It is written. He said: Then it is said: Return him to the earth, for of it We created them, in it We return them, and of it We bring them forth another time. He is placed in his body. The angels come to him, saying to him: Sit. Who is your Lord? He says: My Lord is Allah. He said: They say: What is your religion? He says: He says: My religion is Islam. They say: Who is this man sent among you? He says: He is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They say: How do you know? He says: I read Allah's Book, believed, and affirmed. They call from heaven: He has spoken truth; spread for him from heaven, clothe him from Paradise, and show him his abode of Paradise. He said: He attains of its breeze, his grave is widened for him as far as his sight, and there appears to him a man fair of garments, sweet of scent, saying: Rejoice in what pleases you; this is your day you were promised. He says: Who are you, may Allah have mercy on you? Your face is one that has come with good. He said: He says: I am your righteous deed. He said: And if he be a disbeliever, angels from heaven descend to him, black-faced, with haircloths; they sit from him as far as the eye sees. He said: The Angel of Death comes, sitting at his head, saying: Come forth, O foul soul, to wrath from Allah and His anger. He said: It disperses in his body out of hatred for it. He said: He extracts it, veins and sinews severing with it, as wet wool is extracted with the skewer. When he takes it, they rise to him, not leaving it in his hand the blink of an eye. They take it in its shrouds in the haircloths. He said: There comes from him like the foulest stench of a carcass found upon the face of the earth. They ascend with it, passing no angel but it says: What is this foul spirit? He said: It is said: This is so-and-so, by his worst names. When he rises to heaven, they seek opening, and the doors are shut against him. They are called: Return him to the earth, for of it I created them, in it I return them, and of it I bring them forth another time. He is placed in his body. The angels come to him, saying: Sit. They say: Who is your Lord? He says: He says: Hah hah, I know not. They say: What is your religion? He says: Hah hah, I know not; I heard people saying, I know not. He said: They say: Who is this man sent among you? He says: He says: I know not; I heard people saying. He said: They call from heaven: He has lied; spread for him from the Fire, clothe him from the Fire, and show him his abode of the Fire. He said: He sees his abode of the Fire, attaining of its heat and scorch, his grave straitened upon him until his ribs differ. There appears to him a man foul of face, foul of garments, foul of smell, saying: Rejoice in what grieves you; this is your day you were promised. He says: Who are you, woe to you? By Allah, your face is one that has come to us with evil. He says: I am your foul deed. He says: O Lord, do not establish the Hour; O Lord, do not establish the Hour."
"23249","The account of Ruqayqah bint Abi Sayfi ibn Hashim on seeking rain. 26 - Muhammad ibn Musa ibn Hammad al-Barbari narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Yahya Abu al-Sakan al-Ta'i narrated to us, my father's uncle Zahr ibn Hisn narrated to me, from his grandfather Humayd ibn Munhib, he said: 'Urwah ibn Mudarris narrated to me, he said: Makhramah ibn Nawfal narrated to me, from his mother Ruqayqah bint Abi Sayfi ibn Hashim - his mother being co-born with 'Abd al-Muttalib - she said: Successive years of drought befell Quraysh, drying the udder and thinning the bone. While I lay wakeful with care, or careworn, a caller cried with a hoarse voice, saying: O company of Quraysh, this sent Prophet's days have overshadowed you; this is the season of his stars. Come to life and fertility: send forth a man of you, eminent, mighty, great-bodied, white with radiance, long-lashed, smooth-cheeked, high-bridged of nose, with pride he restrains, and a practice he guides to. Let him go apart, he and his child, and let a man of every clan descend to him. Let them sprinkle water, touch perfume, touch the Corner, then ascend Abu Qubays. Then let the man supplicate and the people say amen; you shall be rained upon as you wish. I awoke - Allah knows - terrified, my skin shivering, my mind distracted. I recounted my dream, sleeping in the ravines of Makkah. By the sanctity and sanctuary, no Bat'ha dweller remained but said: This is Shaybah al-Hamd. The men of Quraysh reached him, and a man of every clan descended to him. They sprinkled, touched perfume, touched the Corner, then ascended Abu Qubays, arraying around him as far as their striving reached a resting place. When they stood level upon the mountain's peak, 'Abd al-Muttalib stood, with him the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a boy grown or near grown. He raised his hands, saying: O Allah, Filler of need, Remover of distress, You are taught, not taught; asked, not stinted. These are Your male and female servants at the courtyards of Your sanctuary, complaining to You of their year, which has taken hoof and cloven hoof. O Allah, rain upon us copious, fertile rain. By the Lord of the Ka'bah, they had not moved before the heaven burst with its water, and the valley crowded with its torrent. I heard the elders and grandees of Quraysh - 'Abd Allah ibn Jud'an, Harb ibn Umayyah, Hisham ibn al-Mughirah - say to 'Abd al-Muttalib: Congratulations to you, O Abu al-Bat'ha - the people of al-Bat'ha live by you. Concerning that, Ruqayqah bint Abi Sayfi says verses."
"23250","The account of Anas - may Allah be pleased with him - on seeking rain. 27 - Abu Ya'la Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim Shadhan narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Mujashi' ibn 'Amr narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, 'Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: People suffered drought in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Muslims came to him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, rain has failed, trees have withered, livestock have perished, people are in famine; seek rain for us from your Lord. He said: ""When such-and-such a day comes, go out, bringing charities with you."" When that day came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with the people, walking, they walking, with tranquility and dignity upon them, until they reached the place of prayer. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advanced, praying with them two bowings, reciting aloud therein. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite in the two Eids and the rain-prayer, in the first bowing the Opening of the Book and {Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High}, and in the second bowing the Opening of the Book and {Has there reached you the account of the Overwhelming}. When he finished his prayer, he faced the people with his face and turned his cloak, he said: that drought may turn to fertility. Then he knelt upon his knees, raised his hands, and uttered a takbir before seeking rain. Then he said: ""O Allah, give us drink and succor us. O Allah, give us a succoring, spacious, spring rain - abundant, pouring, enveloping, drenching, general, wholesome, pleasant, fertile - downpouring, inclusive, trailing, covering, lasting, flowing, beneficial, not harmful, soon, not delayed. O Allah, give life thereby to lands, succor thereby servants, and make it a provision for the present of us and the desert-dweller. O Allah, send down upon us in our land its adornment, send down upon us in our land its ease. O Allah, send down upon us from heaven purifying water; give life thereby to a dead land, and give drink of what You created, cattle and many people."" He said: They had not moved before a fright of cloud advanced from the heaven, joining part to part. Then it rained upon them seven days and their nights, not lifting from Madinah. The Muslims came to him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, the earth has drowned, houses have collapsed, roads are cut; supplicate Allah for us to turn it from us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed upon the pulpit until his molars appeared, in wonder at the son of Adam's quick weariness. Then he raised his head, saying: O Allah, around us, not upon us. O Allah, upon the hilltops, the trees' growths, the valleys' bellies, and the highlands' backs. It split from Madinah, being like a shield over it, as a canopy; it rained its pastures and did not rain a drop therein."
"23251","28 - 'Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Rushd ibn Khuthaym al-Hilali narrated to us, he said: My uncle Sa'id ibn Khuthaym narrated to me, he said: Muslim al-Mula'i narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we have come to you with no camel grumbling, nor a boy crying out snoring. Then he recited verses. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, dragging his cloak, until he ascended the pulpit. Then he raised his hands to heaven, saying: ""O Allah, give us a succoring, pleasant, fertile, copious, enveloping rain - soon, not delayed - beneficial, not harmful, filling thereby the udder, growing thereby the crop, giving life to the earth after its death."" By Allah, he had not returned his hands to his breast before the heaven cast its downpours, and the people of the lowlands came clamoring: O Messenger of Allah, drowning, drowning. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O Allah, around us, not upon us."" The cloud dispersed until it encircled Madinah like a crown. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until his molars appeared, then said: ""Had Abu Talib been alive, his eyes would be cooled. Who recites to us his word?"" 'Ali ibn Abi Talib stood, saying: O Messenger of Allah, as though you meant his word on shelter and striving. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Yes."" A man of Kinanah stood, saying verses of thanks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""If he be a poet doing well, you have done well."""
"23252","The account of Hind ibn Abi Halah on the description of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. 29 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Isma'il al-Nahdi narrated to us, Jumay' ibn 'Umar al-'Ijli narrated to us, he said: A man at Makkah narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Halah al-Tamimi, from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: I asked my maternal uncle Hind ibn Abi Halah - and he was a describer - of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appearance, desiring he describe to me something to hold to. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was magnificent, venerated; his face shone like the moon on the night of full moon; taller than the medium, shorter than the lanky; great of head, wavy of hair; if his tress parted, he parted it, else not; his hair reaching his earlobes when he let it long; radiant of color, wide of forehead; long of eyebrows, thick, without joining; between them a vein that anger fills; aquiline of nose, with a light atop it, deemed by who does not contemplate him high-nosed; thick of beard, smooth of cheeks, wide of mouth, bright-toothed, gapped of teeth; fine of the hair-line; as though his neck were the neck of a statue in silver's purity; moderate of build, full-bodied, firm; level of belly and chest; broad of chest, wide between shoulders; large of joints; luminous of bare skin; joined between throat-pit and navel with hair running like a line; bare of breasts and belly save that; hairy of forearms, shoulders, and upper chest; long of forearms, wide of palm; long of bones, thick of hands and feet; smooth of extremities; hollow of soles; smooth of feet, water gliding off them. When he moved, he moved with force; stepping with humility, walking gently, swift of pace; when he walked as though descending a slope; when he turned, he turned wholly; lowering his gaze, his look to earth longer than to heaven; most of his look observation; preceding his Companions, greeting first whoever he met. I said: Describe to me his speech. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was ever-sorrowful, ever-thoughtful, with no rest; speaking not without need; long of silence; opening speech and sealing it with his lips' sides; speaking comprehensive words, decisive, neither excess nor short; gentle, neither harsh nor base; magnifying blessing though small, blaming nothing of it; blaming no taste nor praising it; the world and what is of it angered him not. When truth was violated, none knew him, and nothing stood before his anger until he avenged it - not for anger for himself, nor avenging it. When he gestured, he gestured with his whole hand; when he marveled, he turned it; when he spoke, he joined it, striking his right palm's inside upon his left thumb's inside. When he angered, he turned away; when he rejoiced, he lowered his gaze. Most of his laughter smiling; parting from like hailstones. He said: I concealed it from al-Husayn a time, then narrated to him, finding he had preceded me to it, asking him of what I asked him, finding he had asked his father of his entering, sitting, going out, and form, leaving nothing of it. Al-Husayn said: I asked my father of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entering. He said: His entering was for himself, permitted him therein. When he retired to his house, he divided his entering into three parts: a part for Allah, a part for himself, a part for his family. Then he divided his part between him and people, returning that to the common through the elite, storing nothing from them. Of his conduct in the ummah's part was preferring the people of merit by his permission, apportioning by their merit in religion. Of them the one of a need, of them the one of two needs, of them the one of needs. He busied himself with them in what set them and the ummah aright, asking them of what he asked and informing them of what behooved them, saying: 'Let the witness convey to the absent; convey to me the need of who cannot convey his need. Whoever conveys to a ruler the need of who cannot convey it to him, Allah shall make his feet firm on the Day of Resurrection.' None was mentioned with him but that, accepting from none other. They entered upon him seekers, parting not but of tasting, leaving him guides. He said: I asked him of his going out, how he acted therein? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held his tongue but for what aided and united them, not dividing nor repelling them. He honored every people's noble, putting him over them. He warned of people, guarding from them, without folding from any his cheer or character. He inspected his Companions, asking people of what is among people. He deemed good good, strengthening it; deemed foul foul, weakening it. Moderate of affair, not differing; heedless not, lest they be heedless or incline. For every state with him a preparation. Falling not short of truth nor exceeding it. Those nearest him of people the best of them; best with him the most general in counsel; greatest with him in station the best in consolation and support. He said: I asked him of his sitting. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat not nor stood but upon remembrance of Allah, claimed no places, forbidding claiming them. When he ended at a people, he sat where the sitting ended with him, commanding that. He gave every sitter his share; his sitter deemed none dearer to him than he. Whoever sat with him or stood with him for a need, he bore with till he was the departing. Whoever asked him a need, he was not turned back but with it or gentle word. His openness and character encompassed people; he became to them a father, they in truth with him equal. His sitting a sitting of forbearance, modesty, patience, and trust; voices not raised therein, sanctities not slandered therein, its slips not spread - surpassing in Godfearing, humble; venerating the elder, pitying the young, preferring the needy, guarding the stranger. I said: How was his conduct among his sitters? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was ever-cheerful, easy of character, soft of side; neither rude, coarse, clamorous, foul, backbiting, nor praising. Heedless of what he disliked; not despaired of, nor disappointed therein. He left himself of three: disputation, excess, and what concerns him not; left people of three: he blamed none, shamed none, sought no one's nakedness; spoke only in what he hoped its reward. When he spoke, his sitters bowed heads as though birds upon their heads; when he fell silent, they spoke, disputing not with him. Whoever spoke, they listened till he finished. Their discourse with him the discourse of their first. He laughed of what they laughed of, marveled of what they marveled of. He bore the stranger's harshness in speech and asking, till his Companions would attract them, saying: 'When you see the need-seeker seeking it, guide him.' He accepted praise only from a requiter, interrupted no one's speech till he exceeded, cutting it with prohibition or rising. I said: How was his silence? He said: The Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) silence was upon four: forbearance, caution, estimation, and thought. As for his estimation, in leveling look and hearing among people. As for his thought - or he said: his remembrance - in what remains and perishes. Forbearance was gathered him in patience; nothing angered or provoked him. Caution was gathered him in four: taking the good, that they follow him; leaving the foul, that they refrain from it; striving his view in what sets his ummah aright; standing in what was gathered them of this world's and the Hereafter's affair."
"23253","Explanation of the account of Hind ibn Abi Halah, from Abu 'Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam. 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu 'Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam say: His word 'magnificent, venerated' - magnificence in the face is its nobility and fullness with beauty and splendor. 'Medium' - between tall and short. 'Lanky' - excessive in tallness, likewise in everything. His word 'wavy of hair' - wavy: neither lank without break, nor intensely curled; he is curled between these. 'Tress' - hair plaited, like braided; whence 'Umar's word: 'Whoever mats, plaits, or braids, upon him is shaving.' His word 'long of eyebrows, thick' - length in eyebrows is arching with length at their ends, which is thickness therein. His word 'without joining' - joining is the meeting of the eyebrows till they connect; he says: he is not thus, but between them is a gap; a man thus is called ablaj, and al-Asma'i mentioned the Arabs deem this handsome. His word 'between them a vein anger fills' - when he angered, the vein between the eyebrows filled; its filling is its thickness, prominence, and fullness. His word 'aquiline of nose' - meaning the nose; aquilinity is fineness with elevation in its bridge; of it: an aquiline man, an aquiline woman. 'High-nosed' - the nose fine without aquilinity. His word 'thick of beard' - thickness: the beard neither fine nor long, but dense without greatness or length. His word 'wide of mouth' - I deem he means fullness in the lips. His word 'bright-toothed' - bright: in his teeth fineness and sharpness; of it: a bright man, a bright woman; whence Dhu al-Rummah's word. 'Gapped' - in his teeth separation. 'Hair-line' - hair between throat-pit and navel, hair running like a line. His word 'neck of a statue' - neck is the neck; statue is the image. His word 'large of joints' - people differed on joints: some said they are bones, meaning great of frames; some make joints the heads of bones. Joints in other than this: battalions in wars. 'Forearms' - the two bones in the forearms joined to the hands; he described him with length of arm. 'Long of bones' - bones: every marrowy bone like shanks, forearms, arms; their length describes him with length of bones. His word 'thick of hands and feet' - meaning some thickness therein. The sole's hollow: in its inside between its front and heel, which touches not earth of the feet in treading. His word 'hollow' - meaning the elevation of that place of his feet holds off earth, taken from belly's slenderness, which is slimness; of it: a hollow man, a hollow woman. His word 'smooth of feet' - meaning they are smooth, no breakage on their backs; hence he said: water glides off them - meaning water has no stay upon them. His word 'when he stepped, with force' - meaning swaying, taken from ships' swaying. His word 'swift of pace' - wide of steps. His word 'as though descending a slope' - I see he means he is facing what is before him, lowering his gaze, raising it not to heaven; thus is the descender. Then he explained, saying: 'Lowering his gaze, his look to earth more than to heaven.' His word 'when he turned, he turned wholly' - meaning he twists not his neck without his body, for in this is some lightness and rashness. His word 'gentle' - soft and easy; of it a man is called gentle; whence the hadith 'when he wished to urinate he inclined to soft ground.' His word 'when he angered he turned away' - turning is averting, and may be caution. His word 'parting from like hailstones' - parting: baring teeth laughing without guffaw; hailstones' grain is hail, likening thereto his teeth's whiteness. His word 'they enter seekers' - seekers: searchers, singular seeker; whence their word: the seeker lies not to his people. His word 'for every state with him a preparation' - meaning a provision prepared for it. His word 'he claims no places' - he makes none for himself a known place; he sits where possible in the place wherein his need is. Then he explained, saying: 'He sits where the sitting ends with him.' Whence his hadith - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: 'That he forbade a man to claim a place in the mosque as the camel claims.' His word on his sitting: 'sanctities not slandered therein' - he says: women not described therein; whence his hadith - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: 'That he forbade poetry when women are described therein.' Abu 'Ubayd said: Abu Isma'il al-Mu'addib narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Men in the mosque were reciting poetry to one another; Ibn al-Zubayr came, saying: 'In Allah's sanctuary and at Allah's House do you recite poetry?' A Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'No harm upon you, O Ibn al-Zubayr, if you corrupt not yourself; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade poetry only when women are described therein, or wealth is satirized therein.' His word 'its slips not spread' - slips: lapses, not spoken of; of it: I spread, I spread; the noun thereof is spreading. This ha' in 'its slips' returns to the sitting - do you not see the speech's beginning is that he asked of his sitting? It is also said there were no slips of his sitting needing any to relate - 'its slips': meaning the sitting's slips, none relating of another."
"23254","The account of Umm Ma'bad al-Khuza'iyyah on the description of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. 30 - 'Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi narrated to us, Mukram ibn Muhriz al-Khuza'i narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Hizam ibn Hisham, from his father, from his grandfather Hubaysh ibn Khalid: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he left Makkah emigrating to Madinah - he, Abu Bakr, Abu Bakr's freedman 'Amir ibn Fuhayrah, and their guide al-Laythi 'Abd Allah ibn Urayqit - passed the two tents of Umm Ma'bad al-Khuza'iyyah. She was a bold, sturdy woman sitting cross-legged at the tent's courtyard, then giving drink and food. They asked her for meat and dates to buy of her, finding nothing of that with her; the people were without provisions, in famine. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at a ewe in the tent's corner, saying: ""What is this ewe, O Umm Ma'bad?"" She said: A ewe weakness left behind from the flock. He said: ""Is there milk in her?"" She said: She is weaker than that. He said: ""Do you permit me to milk her?"" She said: Yes, may my father and mother be your ransom; if you see milking in her, milk her. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for her, wiped her udder with his hand, named Allah the Exalted, and supplicated for her in her ewe. She spread her legs for him, flowed, and chewed. He called for a vessel sufficing a party, milking therein copiously till foam topped it. Then he gave her drink till she was quenched, gave his Companions drink till they were quenched, and drank last of them - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Then they rested, then he milked therein a second time after the first till he filled the vessel. Then he left it with her, pledged her, and departed from her. She had not tarried long before her husband Abu Ma'bad came driving lean goats, swaying with emaciation, their marrow little in forenoon. When Abu Ma'bad saw the milk he marveled, saying: Whence is this milk to you, O Umm Ma'bad, while the ewe is away, dry, and no milch one in the house? She said: No, by Allah, but a blessed man passed by us, of state such-and-such. He said: Describe him to me, O Umm Ma'bad. She said: I saw a man manifest of comeliness, bright of face, fair of form; no paunch disfigured him, no smallness disparaged him; handsome, symmetrical; in his eyes blackness, in his lashes length, in his voice hoarseness, in his neck loftiness, in his beard density; long-eyebrowed, joined; silent, dignity upon him; speaking, splendor named and lofty him; most beautiful and splendid of people from afar, sweetest and fairest from near; sweet of speech, decisive, neither scant nor babbling, as though his speech beads of a string descending; middle-statured, no despair of tallness, no eye scorning him for shortness; a branch between two branches, the freshest of the three in aspect and best in stature; companions encompassing him, when he speaks they listen to his word, when he commands they hasten to his command; served, thronged; neither frowning nor refuted. Abu Ma'bad said: By Allah, he is the Companion of Quraysh of whose affair was mentioned to us at Makkah what was mentioned; I had meant to accompany him, and I shall surely do if I find thereto a way. In the morning a voice at Makkah - they hearing the voice, knowing not its owner - said verses. He said: When Hassan ibn Thabit al-Ansari heard that, he extemporized answering the caller, saying verses."
"23255","The account of Sawad ibn Qarib, may Allah be pleased with him. 31 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri narrated to us, Bishr ibn Hajar al-Shami narrated to us, 'Ali ibn Mansur al-Anbari narrated to us, from 'Uthman ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Waqqasi, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi, he said: While 'Umar ibn al-Khattab sat in the mosque, a man passed at the mosque's rear. A man said: O Commander of the Believers, do you know this passer? He said: No; who is he? He said: This is Sawad ibn Qarib, a man of Yemen, of nobility and standing among them; he is the one to whom his familiar came with the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appearance. 'Umar said: Bring him to me. He was called to him. He said: Are you Sawad ibn Qarib? He said: You - may Allah remove your doubt - did your familiar come to you with the Prophet's, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, appearance? He said: Yes. He said: Are you upon your soothsaying as you were? He angered with severe anger, saying: O Commander of the Believers, none has faced me with this since I accepted Islam. 'Umar said: Glory be to Allah; by Allah, our polytheism was greater than your soothsaying. Inform me of your familiar's coming to you with the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appearance. He said: Yes, O Commander of the Believers. While I was one night between sleeping and waking, my familiar came to me, struck me with his foot, saying: Rise, O Sawad ibn Qarib, understand and comprehend if you comprehend; a Messenger has been sent of Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib, calling to Allah and His worship. Then he versified verses. He said: I paid his word no heed, saying: Leave me to sleep, for I have become drowsy. On the second night he came to me, struck me with his foot, saying: Did I not tell you, O Sawad ibn Qarib, rise, understand and comprehend if you comprehend; a Messenger has been sent of Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib, calling to Allah and His worship. Then the jinni versified verses. He said: I paid his word no heed. On the third night he came to me, struck me with his foot, saying: Did I not tell you, O Sawad ibn Qarib, understand and comprehend if you comprehend; a Messenger has been sent of Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib, calling to Allah and His worship. Then the jinni versified verses. He said: Love of Islam fell in my soul, and I desired it. In the morning I tightened my mount, setting out toward Makkah. Midway I was told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had emigrated to Madinah. I came to Madinah, asking of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). It was said to me: He is in the mosque. I ended at the mosque, hobbled my she-camel, entering it, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and people around him. I said: Hear my word, O Messenger of Allah. Abu Bakr said: Draw near, draw near. He kept with me till I drew near him, standing before him. He said: Come, inform me of your familiar's coming to you. I said verses. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he and his Companions, rejoiced at my Islam with great joy till it was seen in their faces. 'Umar leapt to him, embracing him, saying: I had loved to hear this from you."
"23256","The account of 'Atikah bint 'Abd al-Muttalib's dream on the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) triumph at Badr. 32 - Mas'adah ibn Sa'd al-'Attar narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Imran narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum bint 'Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt, from 'Atikah bint 'Abd al-Muttalib, she said: I saw a rider appear upon Abu Qubays, crying: O family of perfidy, O family of sin, go forth for three. Then he took a rock of Abu Qubays, casting it at the Corner; the rock split, and no house of Quraysh's houses remained but a fragment entered it, save the houses of Banu Zuhrah. Al-'Abbas said: This is a dream; conceal it, mention it not. Al-'Abbas went out, meeting al-Walid ibn 'Utbah ibn Rabi'ah, mentioning it to him; al-Walid mentioned it to his father, and the account spread. Al-'Abbas said: In the morning I circumambulated the House, with Abu Jahl among a party of Quraysh speaking of 'Atikah's dream. When Abu Jahl saw me, he said: O Abu al-Fadl, when you finish your circumambulation, come to us. When I finished, I came till I sat with them. Abu Jahl said: O Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib, were you not content that your men prophesy till your women prophesy? 'Atikah has claimed in this dream of hers that he said: Go forth for three. We shall wait for you these three; if what she says is true, it shall be. If the three pass with nothing thereof, we shall write against you a writ that you are the most lying household among the Arabs. Al-'Abbas said: By Allah, there was nothing from me to him but that I denied that, denying she had seen anything. Al-'Abbas said: In the evening a woman of 'Abd al-Muttalib's daughters came to me, saying: Are you content that this transgressor falls upon your men then assailss your women while you hear, with no rebuke from you? By Allah, had Hamzah been, he would not have said what he said. I said: By Allah, it was, with no rebuke from me to him. By Allah, I shall surely confront him; if he repeats, I shall suffice you. Al-'Abbas said: In the morning of the dream of 'Atikah's third day I went out angered that a matter had escaped me, wishing to attain something thereof. He said: By Allah, as I walked toward him - a light man, sharp of face, sharp of sight, sharp of tongue - he left the mosque's door hastening. I said to myself: What is with him, may Allah curse him - is all this fear of me, that I revile him? He had heard what I had not heard: he heard the voice of Damdam ibn Zur'ah ibn 'Amr al-Ghifari crying in the valley's belly, having hamstrung his camel, turned his cloak, torn his shirt, saying: O company of Quraysh, Muhammad has set out with his Companions; I see not you attaining her. Aid, aid. Al-'Abbas said: What came of the affair busied me from him and him from me. They set out upon every difficult and easy mount. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, granted His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) triumph at Badr. 'Atikah bint 'Abd al-Muttalib said on her dream's truth and Quraysh's belying her, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smote them, verses."
"23257","Naming the mockers of whom He said: {Indeed, We suffice you against the mockers}. 33 - al-Qasim ibn Zakariyya al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Hakim al-Naysaburi narrated to us, Mubashshir ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from Ja'far ibn Iyas, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, on His word {Indeed, We suffice you against the mockers}: al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, al-Aswad ibn 'Abd Yaghuth, al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib Abu Zam'ah of Banu Asad ibn 'Abd al-'Uzza, al-Harith ibn Ghaytal al-Sahmi, and al-'As ibn Wa'il al-Sahmi. Jibril, peace be upon him, came to him; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complained them to him. He showed him al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah; Jibril gestured to his ankle-vein, saying: 'What have you done?' He said: I have sufficed him to you. Then he showed him al-Harith ibn Ghaytal, gesturing to his belly, saying: 'What have you done?' He said: I have sufficed him to you. Then he showed him al-'As ibn Wa'il, gesturing to his sole, saying: 'What have you done?' He said: I have sufficed him to you. As for al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, he passed a man of Khuza'ah feathering arrows; it struck his ankle-vein, severing it. As for al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib, he blinded. Of them, some say: he blinded thus; some say: he settled under a tree, saying: O my sons, will you not defend me? I have perished, pierced by thorn in my eye. They would say: We see nothing. He remained thus till his eyes blinded. As for al-Aswad ibn 'Abd Yaghuth, sores came out in his head, dying of them. As for al-Harith ibn Ghaytal, yellow water seized him in his belly till his excrement came from his mouth, dying. As for al-'As ibn Wa'il, while he was thus, a thorn entered his foot till it filled thereof, dying."
"23258","The account of Abu Sarihah al-Ghifari, whose name is Hudhayfah ibn Asid, on the coming-forth of the Beast. 34 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Talhah ibn 'Amr, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Abu Sarihah al-Ghifari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Beast has three comings-forth of the age. It comes forth once from the farthest desert; its mention spreads among desert folk, its mention enters not the town. Then it hides a long time after that. Then it comes forth another near Makkah; its mention spreads among desert folk and its mention spreads in Makkah. Then it hides a long time. Then, while people are in the greatest of mosques in sanctity with Allah, best and noblest to Allah of mosques - the Sacred Mosque - nothing startles them but the mosque's side swelling between the Black Corner and the Station to the door of Banu Makhzum, to the right of the one going out to the mosque. People scatter from it severally. A band of Muslims stands firm for it, knowing they shall not escape Allah. It comes forth to them, shaking dust off its head. It appears to them, settling their faces till leaving them as though brilliant stars. Then it turns in the earth, no seeker attaining it, no fleer escaping it. Even a man seeks refuge from it with prayer; it comes to him from behind, saying: Where is so-and-so - now you pray? He turns his face to it, and it marks him in his face, then goes. People converse in their houses, accompany in their journeys, share in wealth. The disbeliever is known from the believer, till the disbeliever says to the believer: O believer, settle my right; the believer says to the disbeliever: Settle my right."
"23259","The account of Anas ibn Malik - may Allah be pleased with him - on Friday's merit. 35 - Abu Yazid Yusuf ibn Yazid al-Qaratisi al-Misri narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim Abu Yusuf the judge narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Hayyan narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Jibril came to me with like a mirror. I said: What is this? He said: Friday. Allah sent me with it to you; with us it is the master of days, with us the Day of Increase. Your Lord has taken in Paradise a valley, wide, of white musk. When it is Friday, He descends upon His Throne; with Him descend the prophets, the truthful, and the martyrs. Then the Throne is ringed with pulpits of gold crowned with peridot, pearl, and ruby; thereon sit the prophets, the truthful, and the martyrs. The people of the chambers come till they sit upon dunes of white musk. Their Lord manifests to them; they look upon His face. He says: Am I not He who fulfilled to you My promise? They say: Yes indeed. He says: Am I not He who completed upon you My blessing? They say: Yes indeed. He says: This is My station and My promise; ask Me. They say: We ask You pleasure. He says: My pleasure has settled you My abode; He witnesses them upon His pleasure of them. Ask Me. They ask till their desire ends. He gives them what has not occurred to a mortal's heart, nor eye seen. Then He rises from His Throne; the people of the chambers rise from their chambers in a white tent of pearl wherein is no crack nor split - or in a tent of red ruby, or a tent of green peridot. Therein are its colors, thence its chambers; therein its rivers thereby, therein its fruits, therein its servants and spouses. They are toward nothing more longing nor more expectant than toward Friday, that their Lord - the Mighty and Majestic - descend to them, that they increase toward Him in beholding, and upon them dignity. Hence Friday is called the Day of Increase."
"23260","The account of the Trumpet. 36 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Nahwi al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad al-Nabil narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Rafi' narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to us while among a company, saying: When Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, finished creating the heavens and earth, He created the Trumpet, giving it to Israfil; he holds it upon his mouth, his gaze fixed upon the Throne, awaiting when he shall be commanded. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the Trumpet? He said: The horn. I said: How is it? He said: Mighty; by the One who sent me with the truth, the vastness of a circle therein is like the expanse of the heavens and earth. He shall blow therein three blasts: the first blast the blast of terror, the second the blast of swooning, the third the blast of standing for the Lord of the worlds. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall command Israfil with the first blast, saying: Blow the blast of terror. He shall blow the blast of terror, and the people of the heavens and earth shall be terrified, except whom Allah wills. He shall command him, and he shall prolong and extend it, not pausing - which is what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says {These await but a single cry, which has no rider}. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall move the mountains as clouds move, becoming mirage. Then the earth shall quake with its people a quaking, becoming like the perishing ship in the sea struck by waves, capsizing with its people like the lamp hung from the Throne swayed by winds. Which is what He says: {On the day the quaking quakes * followed by the follower * hearts that day trembling * their sights humbled}. People shall reel upon its face; nursing mothers shall forget, bearers shall lay down, children shall grey. Devils shall fly fleeing from terror till they reach the horizons; angels shall come to them, striking their faces, returning. People shall turn away fleeing, having from Allah no protector, calling one another - which is what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says {the Day of Calling}. While they are thus, the earth shall split in two splits from horizon to horizon; they shall see a mighty matter the like they have not seen. There shall seize them thereof distress and terror known to Allah. Then the heaven shall be folded, becoming like molten dregs. Then the heaven shall split, its stars scattering, its sun and moon eclipsed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dead know nothing of that. Abu Hurayrah said: O Messenger of Allah, whom did Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, except when He says {And all in the heavens and earth shall be terrified, except whom Allah wills}? He said: Those are the martyrs; terror reaches only the living. They are alive with their Lord, provided for; Allah shall shield them that day's terror, securing them from it. It is Allah's torment He sends upon the worst of His creation. Which is what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says {O people, fear your Lord; the quake of the Hour is a mighty thing * On the day you see it, every nursing mother shall forget what she nursed, every bearer shall lay down her burden, you shall see people drunk though they are not drunk, but Allah's torment is severe}. They shall be in that affliction as long as Allah wills, though it shall be long. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall command Israfil with the blast of swooning; the people of the heavens and earth shall swoon, except whom Allah wills. When they have perished, the Angel of Death shall come to the Compeller, the Mighty and Majestic, saying: O Lord, the people of the heavens and earth have died, except whom You willed. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says - knowing best who remains: Who remains? He says: O Lord, You remain, the Living who dies not; Your Throne-bearers remain; Jibril and Mika'il remain; and I remain. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: Let Jibril and Mika'il die. Allah shall give the Throne speech, saying: O Lord, shall Jibril and Mika'il die? Allah says: Be silent, for I have decreed death upon all beneath My Throne. They die. The Angel of Death comes to the Compeller, saying: O Lord, Jibril and Mika'il have died. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says - knowing best who remains: Who remains? He says: You remain, the Living who dies not; Your Throne-bearers remain; and I remain. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: Let My Throne-bearers die. They die. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commands the Throne, and the Trumpet is seized from Israfil. Then the Angel of Death, peace be upon him, comes to the Compeller, saying: O Lord, Your Throne-bearers have died. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says - knowing best who remains: Who remains? He says: O Lord, You remain, the Living who dies not; and I remain. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: You are of My creation; I created you for what you saw. Die. He dies. When none remains but Allah, the One, the Subduer, the Single, the Unique, the Eternal - who begets not nor is begotten - being last as He was first, He shall fold the heavens and earth as the scroll is folded for writing. Then He shall spread them, then grasp them thrice. Then He shall say: I am the Compeller, I am the Compeller, thrice. Then He shall call with His voice: To whom is dominion today? {To whom is dominion today?} thrice. None shall answer Him. Then He shall say to Himself: {To Allah, the One, the Subduer}. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says {On the day the earth shall be changed to other than earth, and the heavens}. He shall spread it, drag it, then extend it as the 'Ukaz leather is extended, {You shall see therein no crookedness nor unevenness}. Then Allah shall drive creation with a single driving; they shall be in this changed like's like wherein they were at first: who was in its belly was in its belly, who was upon its back was upon its back. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall send down upon them water from beneath the Throne. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall command the heaven to rain; it shall rain forty days, till water is above them twelve cubits. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall command bodies to grow; they shall grow as truffles grow, or as herbs grow. When their bodies are complete, being as they were, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: Let My Throne-bearers live. They live. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commands Israfil; he takes the Trumpet, placing it upon his mouth. Then He says: Let Jibril and Mika'il live. They live. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, calls the spirits; they are brought, believers' spirits glowing light, others' spirits darkness. He grasps them all, then casts them into the Trumpet. Then He commands Israfil to blow the blast of resurrection; spirits come forth as though bees, filling between heaven and earth. Allah the Exalted says: By My might and majesty, every spirit shall surely return to its body. He enters spirits into the earth to the bodies, entering through nostrils. Then they walk in bodies as venom walks in the stung. Then the earth shall split from them - I am the first from whom the earth splits. They come forth thereof hastening; to your Lord they rush {hastening to the Caller; the disbelievers say: This is a difficult day} - barefoot, naked, uncircumcised. Then they shall stand one standing, its measure seventy years, looking not at you, judging not between you. You shall weep till tears cease, then weep blood, sweating till it reaches of you bridling you, or reaching chins. You shall cry, saying: Who shall intercede for us to our Lord, judging between us? They shall say: Who is worthier thereof than your father Adam, peace be upon him? Allah created him with His hand, breathed into him of His spirit, and spoke to him face to face. They shall come to Adam, seeking that of him. He shall come, saying: I am not its man. They shall seek aid of the prophets, prophet by prophet; whenever they come to a prophet, he shall refuse them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Until they come to me. I shall set out till I come to the open ground, falling prostrate. Abu Hurayrah said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the open ground? He said: Before the Throne - till Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sends to me an angel, taking my upper arms, raising me. He shall say to me: O Muhammad. I shall say: Yes, at Your service, O Lord. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall say: What is your affair? - knowing best. I shall say: O Lord, You promised me intercession; intercede me among Your creation, judging between them. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall say: I have interceded you; I come to you, judging between you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall return, standing with people. While we stand, we shall hear a mighty sound from heaven, frightening us. The people of the lowest heaven shall descend, like those on earth of jinn and men. When they draw near the earth, the earth shall shine with their Lord's light; they shall take their ranks. We shall say to them: Is our Lord among you? They shall say: No; He comes. Then they shall descend in that measure of doubling, till the Compeller - Blessed and Exalted - descends in shades of cloud and angels. His Throne that day shall bear eight - today they are four. Their feet upon the nethermost earth's bounds, the heavens to their laps, the Throne upon their shoulders. They have a clamor of their tasbih, saying: Glory to the Possessor of power and might; glory to the Possessor of dominion and sovereignty; glory to the Living who dies not; glory to Him who makes creatures die and dies not; All-holy, Most-holy, most-holy, most-holy; glory to our Lord, the Most High, Lord of angels and spirit; glory to the Most High who makes creatures die and dies not. Allah shall place His Throne where He wills of His earth. Then He shall call with His voice, saying: O company of jinn and men, I have listened to you since I created you to this day of yours, hearing your word, seeing your deeds, your scrolls read upon you. Whoever finds good, let him praise Allah; whoever finds other than that, let him blame none but himself. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall command Hell; there shall come forth thereof a towering neck. Then He shall say: {Did I not covenant to you, O sons of Adam, that you worship not Satan - he is to you a manifest enemy * and that you worship Me - this is a straight path * he led astray of you much creation - did you not comprehend * this is Hell you were promised} - or thereby you belied - 'Asim doubted - {This day be distinguished, O criminals}. Allah shall distinguish people; nations shall kneel. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says {And you shall see every nation kneeling; every nation called to its book: Today you are recompensed what you did}. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall judge among His creation except the two weighty, jinn and men. Allah the Exalted shall judge between beasts and cattle, till He judges for the hornless against the horned. When Allah finishes that, no claim remains of one against another. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says to it: Be dust. Thereupon the disbeliever shall say: Would that I were dust. Then Allah shall judge between servants. The first judged therein shall be blood. Every slain in Allah's path shall come; Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall command all slain to bear his head, his jugulars gushing, saying: O Lord, for what did this one kill me? He shall say - knowing best - : For what did you kill them? He shall say: I killed them that might be to You. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall say to him: You spoke truth. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall make his face like the sun's light. Then angels shall pass with him to Paradise. Every slain upon other than that shall come, bearing his head, his jugulars gushing, saying: O Lord, for what did this one kill me? He shall say - knowing best - : Why did you kill them? He shall say: O Lord, I killed them that might be to me. He shall say: May you perish. Then no soul he killed but he shall be killed thereby, no wrong he wronged but he shall be seized thereby. Allah's, the Mighty and Majestic's, will was: if He wills He torments him, if He wills He has mercy on him. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall judge between what remains of His creation He wills, till no wrong of any against any remains but He seizes thereby for the wronged from the wronger. Even he shall charge the mixer of milk with water, then selling it, to separate the milk from water. When Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, finishes that, a caller shall call, hearing all creation: Let every people join their gods and what they worshipped besides Allah. None who worshipped besides Allah shall remain but his gods are figured before him. That day an angel of the angels shall be made in 'Uzayr's image, an angel of the angels in 'Isa's image. Then these the Jews shall follow, these the Christians. Then their gods shall lead them to the Fire - which is what He says {Had these been gods, they would not have come to it; all therein abiding}. When none remains but the believers, among them the hypocrites, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall come to them in what shape He wills of His shape, saying: O people, people have gone; join your gods and what you worshipped. They shall say: By Allah, we have no god but Allah, the Mighty and Majestic; we worshipped none besides Him. He shall turn from them - He being Allah who comes to them. He shall tarry as long as Allah wills to tarry. Then He shall come to them, saying: O people, people have gone; join your gods and what you worshipped. They shall say: By Allah, we have no god but Allah; we worshipped none besides Him. He shall uncover to them His leg; He shall manifest to them of His greatness what they know that He is their Lord. They shall fall to chins prostrate upon their faces. Every hypocrite shall fall upon his back. Allah shall make their spines like cattle's horns. Then Allah, Blessed and Exalted, shall permit them; they shall rise. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall strike the Path between the backs of Hell, like a razor's edge, or a sword's edge. Upon it hooks, grapples, thorns like Sa'dan thorns. Before it a bridge, slippery, sliding. They shall pass like the eye's blink, a glance, wind's passing, horses' steeds, mounts' steeds, men's steeds. Saved sound, saved scratched, and piled upon his face in Hell. When the people of Paradise reach Paradise, they shall say: Who shall intercede for us to our Lord, that we enter Paradise? They shall say: Who is worthier thereof than your father Adam, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, created him with His hand, breathed into him of His spirit, and spoke to him face to face. They shall come to Adam, seeking that of him. He shall mention a sin, saying: I am not its man; but upon you is Nuh, for he is Allah's first messenger. Nuh shall be brought; that shall be sought of him. He shall mention a sin, saying: I am not its man. He shall say: Upon you is Ibrahim, for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, took him as friend. Ibrahim shall be brought; that shall be sought of him. He shall mention a sin, saying: I am not its man. He shall say: Upon you is Musa, for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, brought him near as confidant, spoke to him, and sent down upon him the Torah. Musa, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, shall be brought; that shall be sought of him. He shall mention a sin, saying: I am not its man; but upon you is Allah's spirit and word, 'Isa son of Maryam, peace be upon him. 'Isa son of Maryam shall be brought; that shall be sought of him. He shall say: I am not your man; but upon you is Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They shall come to me - and with my Lord are three intercessions He promised me. I shall set out, coming to Paradise, taking the door's ring. I shall seek opening; it shall be opened for me. I shall be greeted and welcomed. When I enter Paradise and look upon my Lord, I shall fall prostrate. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has permitted me of His praise and glorification what He has permitted none of His creation. Then He, the Mighty and Majestic, shall say: Raise your head, O Muhammad, and intercede. Raise, O Muhammad; intercede, you shall be interceded; ask, you shall be given. When I raise my head, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall say - knowing best - : What is your affair? I shall say: O Lord, You promised me intercession; intercede me among Paradise's people, that they enter Paradise. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall say: I have interceded you; I have permitted them to enter Paradise. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: By the One who sent me with the truth, you in this world are not more knowing of your spouses and dwellings than Paradise's people of their spouses and dwellings. Each of them shall enter upon seventy-two spouses of what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, originates, and two human of Adam's children, with merit over those Allah originated by their worship in this world. He shall enter upon the first in a chamber of ruby, upon a couch of gold crowned with pearl. Upon her seventy pairs of sundus and istabraq. Then he shall place his hand between her shoulders, then look to his hand from her breast, behind her garments, skin, and flesh. He shall look to her leg's marrow as one of you looks to the thread in the ruby's tube. Her liver to him a mirror, his liver to her a mirror. While he is with her, wearying her not, she wearying him not, coming to her no time but finding her virgin. His member never flags, her front never complains. While he is thus, it shall be called: We know you weary not nor are wearied - but there is no semen nor death - but you have other spouses. He shall go out, coming to them one by one. Whenever he comes to one, she shall say to him: By Allah, I see nothing in Paradise fairer than you, nothing in Paradise dearer to me than you. When the people of the Fire fall in the Fire, there shall fall therein a creation of your Lord's creation their deeds destroyed. Of them whom the Fire takes his feet, exceeding not that. Of them whom it takes to his shanks' halves. Of them whom it takes to his knees. Of them whom it takes to his waist. Of them whom it takes his whole body save his face - Allah has forbidden its image upon it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall say: O Lord, intercede me among who fell in the Fire. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall say: Bring out whom you knew. Those shall come out till none of them remains. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall permit intercession; no prophet nor martyr shall remain but he shall be interceded. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall say: Bring out whom you found in his heart a dinar's weight of faith. Those shall come out till none of them remains. Then Allah shall intercede, saying: Bring out whom you found in his heart faith of two-thirds dinar, half dinar. Then He shall say: a third dinar. Then He shall say: a quarter dinar. Then He shall say: a carat. Then He shall say: a mustard grain. Those shall come out till none of them remains, till none remains in the Fire who did for Allah any good ever, none remains with intercession but he shall be interceded. Till even Iblis shall stretch, seeing Allah's, the Mighty and Majestic's, mercy, hoping to be interceded for. Then He shall say: I remain, the Most Merciful of the merciful. He shall enter His hand into Hell, bringing forth thereof what none counts but He, as though they are char. They shall be cast in a river called the River of Life. They shall grow as the grain grows in the torrent's burden. What faces the sun thereof greenish, what faces shade thereof yellowish. They shall grow as truffles grow, till they are like pearls. Written in their necks: the Hell ones, the Freed of the Most Merciful. Paradise's people shall know them by that writing. They did no good ever. They shall tarry in Paradise as long as Allah wills, that writing in their necks. Then they shall say: Efface from us this writing. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall efface from them."
"23261","The account of 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl and Arbad ibn Qays. 37 - Mas'adah ibn Sa'd al-'Attar al-Makki narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Imran narrated to us, 'Abd Allah and 'Abd al-Rahman, sons of Zayd ibn Aslam, narrated to me, from their father, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Ibn 'Abbas: that Arbad ibn Qays ibn Juzayy ibn Khalid ibn Ja'far ibn Kilab, and 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl ibn Malik, came to Madinah to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They ended at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he sat, sitting before him. 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl said: O Muhammad, what do you grant me if I accept Islam? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For you what is for the Muslims, upon you what is upon them. 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl said: Will you grant me the command after you if I accept Islam? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is not for you nor your people; but for you are the horses' reins. He said: I am now among Najd's horses' reins; grant me the desert, yours the towns. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No. When he rose from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), 'Amir said: By Allah, I shall surely fill it against you with horses and men. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall bar you. When 'Amir and Arbad left, 'Amir said: O Arbad, I shall busy Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from you with talk; strike him with the sword. If you kill Muhammad, people shall but accept blood-money, disliking war; we shall give them blood-money. Arbad said: I shall do. They came back to him. 'Amir said: O Muhammad, rise with me that I speak to you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose with him; they withdrew to the wall. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood with him, speaking to him. Arbad unsheathed the sword; when he placed his hand upon his sword, it dried upon the sword's hilt; he could not draw the sword. Arbad delayed the striking upon 'Amir. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned, seeing Arbad and what he did, turning from them. When 'Amir and Arbad left the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), till they were at al-Harrah, Harrat Waqim, they camped. Sa'd ibn Mu'adh and Usayd ibn Hudayr came out to them, saying: Depart, O enemies of Allah; may Allah curse you. 'Amir said: Who is this, O Sa'd? He said: This is Usayd ibn Hudayr the scribe. He said: We left till he was at al-Raqm; Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sent upon Arbad a thunderbolt, killing him. 'Amir went out till he was at al-Harim; then Allah sent upon him an ulcer, seizing him. Night reached him in the house of a woman of Banu Salul; he kept wiping his ulcer in his throat, saying: A gland like a camel's gland, in a Saluli woman's house, desiring to die in her house. Then he mounted his horse, spurring it till he died upon it, returning. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sent down concerning them {Allah knows what every female bears, and what wombs diminish} to His word {They have no guardian besides Him}. He said: The successive angels, by Allah's command, guard Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he mentioned Arbad and what killed him. He said: {He it is who shows you lightning in fear and hope} to His word {He is severe in might}."
"23262","The account of al-Fadl ibn al-'Abbas on retaliation. 38 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Ma'n ibn 'Isa al-Qazzaz narrated to us, al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Iyas al-Laythi narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid ibn Qusayt, from his father, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me; I went out to him, finding him feverish, his head bandaged. He said: ""Take my hand, O Fadl."" I took his hand till he ended at the pulpit, sitting upon it. Then he said: ""Call among people."" I called among people; people gathered. He praised Allah, extolled Him. Then he said: O people, rights from among you have neared me. Whose back I have flogged - here is my back, let him retaliate thereof. Nay, whose honor I have reviled - here is my honor, let him retaliate thereof. Whose wealth I have taken - here is my wealth, let him retaliate thereof. Nay, let no man say: I fear hatred from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Nay, hatred is not of my nature nor affair. Nay, dearest of you to me who takes a right if it be his, or absolves me, meeting Allah with pure soul. Nay, I see not that availing me till I stand among you repeatedly. Then he descended, praying Zuhr. Then he returned to the pulpit, returning to his word on hatred and other than it. Then he said: ""O people, whoever has anything with him, let him return it; let him not say: this world's disgrace. Nay, this world's disgrace is easier than the Hereafter's disgrace."" A man rose to him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, I have with you three dirhams. He said: ""We belie no speaker, nor make him swear among them; how came it to be yours with me?"" He said: Remember the day a needy one passed by you, and you commanded me to pay them to him. He said: ""Pay them to him, O Fadl."" Then another man rose to him, saying: With me are three dirhams I misappropriated in Allah's path. He said: ""Why did you misappropriate them?"" He said: I needed them. He said: ""Take them from him, O Fadl."" Then he said: ""O people, whoever fears something upon himself, let him rise that I supplicate for him."" A man rose to him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, I am a liar, a hypocrite, sleepy. He said: ""O Allah, grant him truthfulness and faith; remove sleep from him when he wishes."" Then another man rose to him, saying: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I am a liar, a hypocrite; there is nothing of things but I have done it. 'Umar said to him: Man, you have disgraced yourself. He said: ""Hush, O Ibn al-Khattab; this world's disgrace is easier than the Hereafter's disgrace."" Then he said: ""O Allah, grant him truthfulness and faith; turn his affair to good."" 'Umar spoke to them with a word. He said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab - may Allah be pleased with him - said: O Messenger of Allah, we are pleased with Allah as Lord, Islam as religion, Muhammad as Prophet. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""I am of 'Umar, 'Umar of me; truth after me is with 'Umar wherever he be."""
"23263","The account of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah on the Messenger of Allah's, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, dream. 39 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Ahmad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah al-Fazari narrated to us, al-Wazir ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out: I saw last night a wonder. I saw a man of my ummah encompassed by angels; his ablution came, rescuing him thereof. I saw a man of my ummah encompassed by devils; Allah's remembrance came, freeing him of them. I saw a man of my ummah panting with thirst; Ramadan's fasting came, giving him drink. I saw a man of my ummah, before him darkness, behind him darkness, to his right darkness, to his left darkness, above him darkness, beneath him darkness; his pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage came, bringing him out of darkness. I saw a man of my ummah, the Angel of Death coming to seize his spirit; his kindness to his parents came, turning him from him. I saw a man of my ummah speaking to the believers, they speaking not to him; his kinship-joining came, saying: This one joins his kinship. They spoke to him, he spoke to them, becoming with them. I saw a man of my ummah coming to people in circles; whenever he came to a circle, he was driven; his bathing from major impurity came, taking his hand, seating him with them. I saw a man of my ummah shielding his face with his hands from the Fire's blaze; his charity came, becoming shade upon his head, cover upon his face. I saw a man of my ummah, the torment's guards coming to him; his commanding right and forbidding wrong came, rescuing him thereof. I saw a man of my ummah falling in the Fire; his tears wept from Allah's fear came, bringing him out of the Fire. I saw a man of my ummah, his scroll falling to his left; his fear of Allah came, taking his scroll, placing it in his right. I saw a man of my ummah trembling as the palm-frond trembles; his good opinion of Allah came, stilling his trembling. I saw a man of my ummah crawling upon the Path once, kneeling once, clinging once; his blessing upon me came, taking his hand, raising him upon the Path till he passed. I saw a man of my ummah ending at Paradise's doors, the doors shut against him; his testimony that there is no god but Allah came, taking his hand, entering him into Paradise."
"23264","The account of Ayyub the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. 40 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-'Allaf narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Nafi' ibn Yazid informed us, from 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ayyub the Prophet - peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - his affliction tarried with him eighteen years. Near and far rejected him, save two men of his brethren, most devoted of his brethren to him, visiting him morning and evening. One of them said to his companion one day: Know, by Allah, Ayyub has sinned a sin none of the worlds has sinned. His companion said to him: And what is that? He said: For eighteen years Allah has not had mercy on him, uncovering what is with him. When the man saw Ayyub's state, he could not bear till he mentioned that to him. Ayyub said: I know not what you say, save that Allah knows I would pass two men disputing, mentioning Allah, returning to my house, expiating for them, disliking Allah to be mentioned but in truth. He said: He would go out for his need; when he relieved his need, his wife would hold his hand till he reached. One day he tarried upon her. Allah revealed to Ayyub in his place: Stamp with your foot - this a cool washing and drink. She deemed him slow, reaching him looking. He came to her, Allah having removed his affliction, fairest as he was. When she saw him, she said: O, may Allah bless you - have you seen this tried Prophet of Allah? He is upon a state; by Allah, upon that I have seen none resembling him more than you when he was sound. He said: I am he. He had two threshing-floors: a wheat threshing-floor and a barley threshing-floor. Allah sent two clouds; when one was upon the wheat, it poured therein gold till it overflowed; the other poured in the barley threshing-floor silver till it overflowed."
"23265","The account of the Cave. 41 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, 'Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us, Isma'il ibn 'Abd al-Karim al-San'ani narrated to us, 'Abd al-Samad ibn Ma'qil narrated to me, he said: I heard Wahb ibn Munabbih say: al-Nu'man ibn Bashir al-Ansari narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention al-Raqim, saying: Three men were in a cave; the mountain fell upon the cave's door, sealing it upon them. One of them said: Recall which of you did a good deed, that Allah by His mercy may have mercy on us. One of them said: I did a good deed once. I had hirelings working work for me; I hired each man of them for known wage. One day a man came to me midday; I hired him on his companions' condition. He worked the rest of his day as one of them worked his whole day. I saw in the rein that I not diminish him what I hired his companions for, for his striving in his work. One of them said: You gave this like what you gave me, working but half the day. I said: O servant of Allah, I wronged you nothing of your condition; it is but my wealth, judging therein as I will. He angered, went, leaving his wage. I placed his right in the house's side as long as Allah willed. Then cattle passed me after that; I bought therewith a young she-camel of cows. It reached what Allah willed. After a while an aged weak shaykh passed me, unknown to me, saying: I have with you a right. He reminded till I knew him. I said: It is you I seek; this is your right. I offered it to him all. He said: O servant of Allah, mock me not; if you give me not charity, give me my right. I said: By Allah, I mock you not; it is your right, nothing of mine therein. I pushed it to him all. O Allah, if I did that for Your face, relieve us. The mountain split till they saw light, beholding. The other said: I did a good deed once. I had surplus; people were struck with hardship. A woman came to me seeking kindness of me. I said: By Allah, it is not of me without yourself. She refused me, went, then returned. She reminded me by Allah; I refused her, saying: No, by Allah, it is not without yourself. She refused me, went, then returned, mentioning to her husband. He said to her: Give him yourself, enrich your dependents. She returned to me, adjuring me by Allah; I refused her, saying: By Allah, it is not without yourself. When she saw that, she yielded herself to me. When I uncovered her, she trembled beneath me. I said to her: What is your affair? She said: I fear Allah, Lord of the worlds. I said to her: You feared Him in hardship, I feared Him not in ease. I left her, giving her for the right upon what I uncovered her. O Allah, if You know I did that for Your face, relieve us. He said: The mountain split till they knew, clarifying to them. The other said: I did a good deed once. I had two aged great parents, and sheep of mine. I would feed my parents, give them drink, sate them, then return to my sheep. One day rain befell me, detaining me. I returned not till evening. I came to my family, taking my milker, milking, my sheep standing. I went to my parents, finding them asleep. It pained me to wake them, pained me to leave my sheep. I remained sitting, my milker upon my hand, till morning woke them; I gave them drink. O Allah, if I did that for Your face, relieve us. al-Nu'man said: As though I hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: The mountain said: Bang - Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, relieved them; they went out."
"23266","The account of Pharaoh's daughter's hairdresser. 42 - Abu Ma'n Thabit ibn Nu'aym al-Huji narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas al-'Asqalani narrated to us; and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib informed, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: On the night I was taken by night, I found a good scent. I said: What is this good scent, O Jibril? He said: This is the scent of Pharaoh's daughter's hairdresser and her children. I said: What is her affair? He said: While she combed Pharaoh's daughter, the comb fell from her hand. She said: In Allah's name. Pharaoh's daughter said: My father? She said: No; but my Lord, your Lord, and your father's Lord is Allah. She said: Have you a lord other than my father? She said: Yes. She said: Shall I inform him that? She said: Yes. She informed him; he called for her, saying: O woman, have you a lord other than I? She said: Yes; my Lord and your Lord is Allah. He ordered a hollow of copper, heated. Then her children were taken, cast therein one by one. She said: I have to you a need. He said: And what is it? She said: I love that you gather my bones and my child's bones in one cloth, burying them together. He said: That is yours upon us. Her children kept cast in the hollow till it ended at a suckling son of hers. She seemed to hold back for him. He said to her: O mother, plunge; this world's torment is easier than the Hereafter's torment. Ibn 'Abbas said: Four small ones spoke: 'Isa son of Maryam, Jurayj's companion, Yusuf's witness, and Pharaoh's daughter's hairdresser's son."
"23267","The account of Jurayj the monk. 43 - Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah narrated to me, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That a man called Jurayj was a monk. His mother came to him, saying: O Jurayj, show me your face that I look upon it. She found him praying. He said: My mother and my prayer - I deem I prefer my mother over my prayer. She left. Then she came to him a second time, calling: O Jurayj, show me your face that I look upon it. She found him praying. He said like his word to her. She said: You refuse to show me your face; may Allah not make you die till you look in the adulteresses of the town. He knew that would befall him. A slave-girl pasturing her people's sheep near his cell conceived. When she delivered, it was said to her: Who is your companion? She said: Jurayj the monk - he descended, wronging me. He was brought, taken to their king. When he passed, they opened the adulteresses' door till they were brought out laughing at him. He smiled. They said: Why do you laugh today, till you passed the adulteresses? When he was entered upon their king, Jurayj said: Where is the boy she bore? He was brought. Jurayj said to him: Who is your father? He said: My father is so-and-so, naming him. Allah the Exalted cleared Jurayj."
"23268","The account of the speaking infant. 44 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Hawdhah ibn Khalifah narrated to us, he said: 'Awf narrated to us, from Khilas ibn 'Amr, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: While a woman among those before you nursed a son of hers, a proud horseman passed her, upon him a fair badge. The woman said: O Allah, make me not die till I see my son like this horseman. The boy loosed the breast, saying: O Allah, make me not like this horseman. Then he returned to the breast, nursing. Then an Abyssinian or black slave-girl passed her, dragged. She said: I seek my son's refuge with Allah from dying this Abyssinian or black one's death. He said: O Allah, make me die her death. His mother said: O my son, I asked your Lord to make you like this horseman, and you said: O Allah, make me not like him. I asked your Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, not to make you die this Abyssinian or black one's death, and you asked your Lord her death. The boy said: You asked your Lord to make me like a man of the Fire's people. The Abyssinian or black one - her people would revile and strike her, saying: Allah suffices me."
"23269","The account of Musa and al-Khidr, upon them prayer and peace. 45 - 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: We were with him when the people said: Nawf al-Shami claims that the Musa who went seeking knowledge is not the Musa of Banu Isra'il. Ibn 'Abbas was reclining; he sat upright, saying: Is it thus, O Sa'id? I said: I heard him say that. Ibn 'Abbas - may Allah be pleased with him - said: Nawf has lied. Ubayy ibn Ka'b narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah's mercy upon us and upon Musa; had he not felt shy, seized by scruple, saying {If I ask you of anything after it, accompany me not}, he would have seen of his companion a wonder. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he mentioned a prophet of the prophets, would begin with himself, saying: Allah's mercy upon us and upon Salih; Allah's mercy upon us and upon the brother of 'Ad. Then he said: While Musa was one day addressing his people, he said to them: None on earth is more learned than I. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed to him that on earth is one more learned than you. Its sign: provision yourself a salted fish; when you lose it, he is where you lose it. He provisioned a salted fish, setting out he and his lad. When they reached the place they were commanded, they ended at the rock. Musa, peace be upon him, went seeking, his lad placing the fish upon the rock; it stirred, taking its way in the sea as a tunnel. His lad said: When Allah's Prophet comes, I shall tell him. Satan made him forget. They went on, befalling them what befalls travelers of toil and weariness - what befalls the traveler of toil and weariness would not befall him till he passed what he was commanded. Musa said to his lad: Bring us our morning meal; we have met of this journey of ours toil. His lad said: O Prophet of Allah, did you see when we retired to the rock? I forgot to tell you; none made me forget to mention it but Satan. It took its way in the sea wondrously. He said: That is what we sought. They returned upon their tracks, tracing the trace till they ended at the rock. He circled it; behold a man wrapped in his garment. He greeted him; he raised his head. He said: Who are you? He said: Musa. He said: Which Musa? He said: The Musa of Banu Isra'il. He said: What is with you? He said: I was told you have knowledge; I wished to accompany you. {He said: You shall never bear with me patience}. {He said: You shall find me, if Allah wills, patient, disobeying you no command * He said: If you follow me, ask me of nothing till I make thereof to you mention}. They went till they boarded; those therein left, he staying behind to scuttle it. Musa said to him {Have you scuttled it to drown its people? * You have come a grave thing * He said: Did I not tell you: you shall never bear with me patience}. {He said: Blame me not for what I forgot, burden me not of my affair hardship}. They went till they came upon boys playing on the sea's shore; among them a boy, none among the boys cleaner than he. He took him, killing him. Musa started thereat, saying {Have you slain a pure soul without a soul? * You have come a grave thing}. {He said: Did I not say: you shall never bear with me patience}. Scruple of his companion seized him; he felt shy, saying {If I ask you of anything after it, accompany me not; you have attained of me excuse}. They went till they came to the people of a mean village; Musa was struck with hunger, they hosted them not. {They found therein a wall about to fall, so he raised it}. Musa said to him, seeing therein of hunger {Had you wished, you would have taken thereon wage}. He said {This is parting between me and you}. Musa took his garment's edge, saying {As for the ship, it belonged to poor men working in the sea} {Behind them was a king seizing every ship by force}. When he passes upon it, seeing it scuttled, he leaves it; its people patch it with a wood piece, benefiting thereby. As for the boy, he was stamped the day he was stamped a disbeliever. Love of his parents had been cast upon him. Had he lived, he would have burdened them with transgression and disbelief. {We wished their Lord to exchange them better than him in purity, nearer in mercy}. His father fell upon his mother; she conceived, bearing better than him in purity, nearer in mercy. {As for the wall, it belonged to two orphan boys in the town; beneath it was their treasure. Their father was righteous; your Lord wished they reach their maturity, extracting their treasure - mercy from your Lord. I did it not of my command. That is the interpretation of what you could not bear patience upon}."
"23270","46 - 'Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-San'ani narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Thawr narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ya'la ibn Muslim and 'Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Musa, Allah's Messenger - peace be upon him - reminded people one day till eyes overflowed, hearts softened, then left. A man reached him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, is there on earth any more learned than you? He said: No. He was blamed, for he returned not knowledge to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. It was said: Yes indeed. He said: O Lord, where? He said: The junction of the two seas. He said: O Lord, make for me a sign I know that. He said: Where the fish leaves you. Then he mentioned Abu Ishaq's account, adding therein: He said: He said: O Musa, I have knowledge you ought not know; you have knowledge I ought not know. He said: A bird took with its beak of the sea. He said: By Allah, my knowledge and your knowledge beside Allah's knowledge are but as this bird took of the sea."
"23271","The account of al-Jassasah. 47 - Hafs ibn 'Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Ma'mar al-Muq'ad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Mu'allim narrated to me, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Buraydah narrated to us, he said: 'Amir ibn Sharahil al-Sha'bi, Sha'b Hamdan, narrated to me: that he asked Fatimah bint Qays, sister of al-Dahhak ibn Qays - of the first Muhajir women: Narrate to me a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), ascribing it to none but you. She said: If you wish, I shall surely do. He said to her: Yes, narrate to me. She said: I heard the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caller call: Prayer is gathering. I went out to the mosque, praying with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I was in the women's row next the men's backs. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he sat upon the pulpit, laughing. He said: ""Let every man keep his place of prayer."" Then he said: ""Do you know why I gathered you?"" They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: By Allah, I gathered you not for desire nor terror; but I gathered you because Tamim al-Dari was a Christian man. He came, pledged, accepted Islam, and narrated to me an account agreeing with what I used to narrate to you of the False Messiah. He narrated to me that he boarded a sea ship with thirty men of Lakhm and Judham. The waves sported with them a month in the sea. Then they were cast to an island in the sea at sunset. They sat in the ship's boat, entering the island. A beast met them, shaggy, abundant of hair; they knew not its front from its back for abundance of hair. They said: Woe to you, what are you? It said: I am al-Jassasah. They said: And what is al-Jassasah? It said: O people, go to this man in the monastery; he is eager for your news. When it named to us a man, we feared thereof it be a deviless. We set out swiftly till we entered the monastery. Therein was the greatest man we ever saw in creation, most firm in bonds - his hands joined to his neck, between his knees to his ankles, with iron. We said: Woe to you, what are you? He said: You have gained my news; inform me who you are? They said: We are people of the Arabs; we boarded a sea ship, meeting the sea when it swelled. The waves sported with us a month. Then it cast us to this island of yours. We sat in its boat, entering the island. A beast met us, shaggy, abundant of hair; we know not its front from its back for abundance of hair. We said: Woe to you, what are you? It said: I am al-Jassasah. We said: And what is al-Jassasah? It said: Betake to this man; he is eager for your news. We came to you swiftly, fearing thereof, not safe from its being a deviless. He said: Inform me of Baysan's palms. We said: Of which affair thereof do you ask? He said: I ask you of its palms - do they fruit? We said to him: Yes. He said: It is soon that they fruit not. He said: Inform me of Lake Tiberias. We said: Of which affair thereof do you ask? He said: Is there water therein? They said: It is abundant of water. He said: Inform me of Zughar's spring. They said: Of which affair thereof do you ask? He said: Is there water in the spring? Do its people sow with the spring's water? We said: Yes; it is abundant of water, its people sowing with its water. He said: Inform me of the unlettered Prophet, what has he done? They said: He left Makkah, settling Yathrib. He said: Did the Arabs fight him? We said: Yes. He said: How did he do with them? We informed him he had prevailed over the Arabs near him, they obeying him. He said: Was it that? We said: Yes. He said: That is better for them, to obey him. I inform you of me: I am the False Messiah. It is soon I be permitted to come forth. I shall come forth, journeying in the earth, leaving no town but I descend it in forty nights, save Makkah and Taybah. They are both forbidden to me. Whenever I wish to enter one of them, an angel meets me, sword in hand unsheathed, barring me thereof. Upon every pass thereof are angels guarding it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, striking his staff upon the pulpit: ""This is Taybah, this is Taybah, this is Taybah"" - meaning Madinah. ""Did I not narrate to you that?"" People said: Yes. He said: ""Tamim al-Dari's account pleased me, for it agreed with what I used to narrate to you of him, of Madinah and Makkah. Nay, he is in Syria's sea or Yemen's sea. No - rather from the east's direction, from the east's direction. And he gestured with his hand toward the east."" She said: I memorized this from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"23272","The account of Abu Umamah al-Bahili - may Allah be pleased with him - on the coming-forth of the Dajjal. 48 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Damrah ibn Rabi'ah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi 'Amr al-Saybani, from 'Amr ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hadrami, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us. Most of his address was mention of the Dajjal, warning us - narrating to us of him - till he finished his address. Of what he said to us that day: Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has sent no prophet but he warned his ummah of him. I am the last of the prophets, you the last of the nations. He shall come forth among you inevitably. If he comes forth while I am among you, I am every Muslim's disputant. If he comes forth among you after me, every man is his soul's disputant; Allah is my successor over every Muslim. He shall come forth from a gap between Iraq and Syria - ravaging right, ravaging left. O servants of Allah, stand firm. He shall begin, saying: I am a prophet, no prophet after me. Between his eyes is written disbeliever, every believer reading it. Whoever of you meets him, let him spit in his face, reciting the openings of the Cave's chapter. He shall be empowered over a soul of Adam's sons, killing it, then giving it life. He shall not exceed that, empowered over no soul besides it. Of his trial: with him are a paradise and a fire. His fire is paradise, his paradise fire. Whoever is tried with his fire, let him close his eyes, seeking Allah's aid; it shall be coolness and peace, as the fire was coolness and peace upon Ibrahim. His days are forty days: a day like a year, a day like a month, a day like a Friday, a day like the days, his last days like mirage. The Dajjal shall morning at Madinah's door, evening before reaching its other door. They said: How shall we pray, O Prophet of Allah, in those long days? He said: ""Estimate therein as you estimate in the long days. Nothing of the earth shall remain but he treads and prevails it, save Makkah and Madinah. He comes to none of their houses' houses but an angel meets him, sword unsheathed, till he settles at the red hillock, at the salt-flat's end, at the torrents' meeting. Then it shall quake - Madinah shall quake with its people three quakes; no hypocrite man nor hypocrite woman shall remain but he shall come out to him. Madinah that day shall expel its dross as the bellows expels iron's dross. That day called the Day of Salvation."" Umm Sharik said: Where are the Muslims - the Arabs - that day? He said: ""They that day are few; most of them at Bayt al-Maqdis. He shall come forth, besieging them. The people's imam that day a righteous man. It shall be said: Pray the dawn. When he says takbir, entering therein, 'Isa son of Maryam - Allah's blessings upon him and His peace - shall descend. When that man sees him, he shall know him, stepping back walking backwards. 'Isa, peace be upon him, shall advance, placing his hands between his shoulders, saying: Pray, for it was established but for you. 'Isa, peace be upon him, shall pray behind him. Then he shall say: Open the door. They shall open the doors. With the Dajjal that day seventy thousand Jews, each with crown and gilded sword. When he looks upon 'Isa, peace be upon him, he shall melt as lead melts, as salt melts in water. Then he shall come out fleeing. 'Isa, peace be upon him, shall say: I have in you a strike you shall not escape me with. He shall reach him, killing him. Nothing of what the Mighty and Majestic created wherein a Jew hides but Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall make it speak - no stone, tree, nor beast but it shall say: O Muslim servant of Allah, this is the Jew - kill him - save al-Gharqad; it is of their trees, speaking not. 'Isa shall be in my ummah a just judge, an equitable imam. He shall break the cross, kill the swine, abolish the poll-tax, leave charity. None shall be driven upon sheep nor camel. Hatred and mutual loathing shall be lifted. Every venomous beast's venom shall be removed, till the boy enters his hand in the serpent, harming him not; the girl harms the lion, harming her not. Camels shall be among - like their dog; the wolf among sheep as though their dog. He shall fill the earth with peace. He shall strip their disbelievers' kingdom; no king shall be but he accepts Islam. The earth shall be like silver's dining-tray. Its plants shall grow as on Adam's, Allah's blessings upon him, time. A party shall gather upon a grape-cluster, sufficing them. A party shall gather upon a pomegranate, sufficing them. The ox shall be for such-and-such of wealth; the horse for dirhams."""
"23273","In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. {Our Lord, give us from Yourself mercy, and prepare for us of our affair guidance}. The shaykh Abu al-Hasan Mas'oud ibn Abi Mansour ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Jammal al-Asbahani informed us, with my reading upon him there in the year five hundred ninety-five, my father Abu Mansour Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi informed us, Abu al-Husayn Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Fadhshah informed us, Abu al-Qasim Sulayman ibn Ahmad ibn Ayyub al-Tabarani informed us, he said: The account of Bilal ibn Rabah on the Messenger of Allah's, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, spending. 49 - Ahmad ibn Khulayd al-Halabi narrated to us, Abu Tawbah al-Rabi' ibn Nafi' narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Sallam, that he heard Abu Sallam say: 'Abd Allah al-Hawzani narrated to me, he said: I met Bilal, caller of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Bilal, narrate to me how was the Messenger of Allah's, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, spending? He said: He had nothing. I was the one who undertook that of him since Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sent him till he died. When a Muslim man came to him, seeing him bare, he would command me with him; I would go, borrow, buy a cloak, clothe and feed him. Till a man of the polytheists met me, saying: O Bilal, I have means; borrow of none but me. I did. One day I performed ablution, then stood to call for prayer. The polytheist came among a band of traders. When he saw me, he said: O Abyssinian. I said: At your service. He frowned at me, saying to me a mighty word. He said to me: Do you know how long between you and the month? I said: Near. He said: Between you and it are but four; I shall seize you for what is upon you. I gave you what I gave, not for your honor nor your companion's honor upon me; but I gave you to seize you, taking you my slave, returning you pasturing sheep as you pastured before that. There took me in myself what takes in people's souls. Then I went, calling for prayer. When I prayed the night prayer, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to his family. I sought his permission; he permitted me. I said: O Messenger of Allah, the polytheist of whom I borrowed said to me such-and-such. You have not wherewith to settle for me, nor have I. He shall disgrace me. Permit me to come to some of these tribes who have accepted Islam, till Allah brings His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what settles for me. I went out till I reached my house, placing my sword, bag, shield, and sandals at my head, facing with my face the horizon. Whenever I slept an hour, I awoke. When I saw night upon me, I slept, till the first dawn column split. I wished to go, when a man hastened calling: O Bilal, answer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I went, coming to him, with four kneeling mounts, their loads upon them. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), seeking his permission. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Rejoice; Allah has brought you your settlement. I praised Allah the Exalted. He said: ""Did you not pass the four kneeling mounts?"" I said: Yes indeed. He said: Yours are their necks and what is upon them. Upon them are clothing and food, gifted them to me the chief of Fadak. Take them, then settle your debt. I did, putting down their loads, then hobbling them. Then I rose to my calling of the dawn prayer. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, I went out to al-Baqi', placing my fingers in my ears, calling, saying: Whoever sought the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for debt, let him attend. I kept selling till two ounces or an ounce and half remained in my hand. Then I went to the mosque, most of the day gone. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat in the mosque alone. I greeted him. He said to me: ""What did what is with you do?"" I said: Allah has settled all that was upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); nothing remains. He said: ""Has anything remained?"" I said: Yes. He said: ""See that you relieve me thereof, for I enter not upon any of my family till you relieve me thereof."" None came to us till evening. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the night prayer, he called me, saying to me: ""What did what is with you do?"" I said: It is with me; none has come to us. He spent the night in the mosque till morning, remaining the second day till late in the day. Two riders came; I went with them, feeding and clothing them. When he prayed the night prayer, he called me, saying: ""What did what is with you do?"" I said: Allah has relieved you thereof, O Messenger of Allah. He said takbir, praising Allah out of pity lest death reach him with that upon him. Then I followed him till he came to his wives, greeting woman by woman till he reached his night's lodging. That is what you asked me of."
"23274","The account of Umm Sulaym's loaf, and what Allah manifested therein of the Messenger of Allah's, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, proof. 50 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, Abu Salih 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Talhah came to Umm Sulaym - she being Anas ibn Malik's mother - Abu Talhah his stepfather. He said: Have you, O Umm Sulaym, anything? I passed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he taught the People of the Bench the Women chapter, having bound upon his belly a stone from hunger. She said: I had some barley; I ground it. Then he sent me to the markets - the markets being walled gardens of theirs. I brought them some firewood, making thereof a loaf. Then he said: Have you condiment? She said: I had a skin wherein was clarified butter; I know not if aught remained therein? She brought it to him; he squeezed it, saying: Two's squeezing reaches more than one's squeezing. They squeezed it together, bringing forth like a date. She greased therewith the loaf. Then he called me, saying: O my son - Anas - do you know the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? I said: Yes. He said: I left the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the People of the Bench, teaching them. Call him, call none with him besides him. See that you disgrace me not. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he saw me, he said: ""Perhaps your father has sent you to us."" I said: Yes. He said to the people: ""Go."" They that day eighty men. He took my hand. When I neared the house, I pulled my hand from his hand. Abu Talhah began seeking me in the house, pelting me with stones, saying: You have disgraced me with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he went out to him, informing him the news. He said: ""It shall not harm you."" He commanded them; they sat. Then he entered; we brought him the loaf. He said: ""Is there condiment?"" Umm Sulaym said: O Messenger of Allah, we had a skin; I squeezed it, I and Abu Talhah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring it; three's squeezing reaches more than two's squeezing. It was brought to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) squeezed it with them, bringing forth thereof like a date. They wiped the loaf therewith. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped it with his hand, supplicating therein blessing. Then he said: ""Call me ten."" I called ten; they ate thereof till they drank their fill of satiety. They kept entering ten by ten till they were sated. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat; we sat with him, eating till some remained."
"23275","The account of Jabir's kid, and what Allah manifested therein of the Messenger of Allah's, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, proof. 51 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Ashkib al-Kufi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ayman, from his father, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dug the Trench; the Muslims were struck with severe hardship, till the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bound upon his belly a rock from hunger. I went to my family, saying: I have seen in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions' face hunger. I slaughtered a kid of ours, ordering my family to bake some barley with them. They cooked the kid. Then I called the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informing him of what I made. He said: Go, prepare what is with you till I come to you. I went, preparing what was with us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, the army all. I said: O Messenger of Allah, it is but a kid I made for you and some of your Companions. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Bring a bowl."" I brought him a bowl. He said: ""Serve stew therein."" Then he supplicated thereon blessing. Then he said: ""In Allah's name."" Then he said: ""Admit ten."" I did. When they tasted, sated, they left; I admitted another ten, till the army was sated, the food as it was."
"23276","The account of Abu 'Amrah al-Ansari, whose name is Usayd ibn Malik, on the increase in the Tabuk campaign, and what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, manifested thereby of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proof. 52 - Abu 'Abd al-Malik Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Qurashi al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-'Ala' ibn Zabr narrated to us, my father 'Abd Allah ibn al-'Ala' narrated to me, from al-Zuhri and al-Awza'i, they said: al-Muttalib ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Hantab narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi 'Amrah al-Ansari narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a campaign he campaigned; people were struck with hunger. People sought the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permission to slaughter some of their mounts. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) purposed to permit them therein. 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said: O Messenger of Allah, when we slaughter our mounts, then meet our enemy tomorrow hungry men. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""What deem you, O 'Umar?"" He said: Call people with their provisions' remnants; then supplicate for us therein blessing. Allah the Exalted shall sate us by your supplication, if Allah wills. He said: As though upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a cover, uncovered. He called for a garment, commanding therewith; it was spread. Then he called people with their provisions' remnants. They brought what was with them. Of people who brought the bowl of food or the handful; of them who brought like the egg. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded therewith; it was placed upon that garment. Then he supplicated therein blessing, speaking what he willed to speak. Then he called among the army; they came. Then he commanded them; they ate, tasted, filling their vessels and provision-bags. Then he called for a leather bucket; it was placed before him. Then he called for water, pouring it therein. Then he rinsed therein, speaking what Allah willed to speak. Then he entered his little finger therein. I swear by Allah: I saw the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fingers gush springs of water. Then he commanded people; they drank, watered, filling their skins and vessels. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed till his molars appeared. Then he said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah, alone, no partner with Him, and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. None meets Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, with them but he shall enter Paradise, upon whatever deed."
"23277","The account of Abu Qatadah's water-skin - his name al-Harith ibn Rib'i al-Ansari - and what Allah manifested of the Messenger of Allah's, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, proof. 53 - Yusuf the judge and Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, they said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Sumayr, from 'Abd Allah ibn Rabah al-Ansari, from Abu Qatadah, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey. While they were in a night, veiled from the road, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dozed, inclining from the saddle. I came to him, propping him with my hand. When he felt a man's hand's touch, he straightened, saying: ""Who is this?"" I said: Abu Qatadah. Then he went on, dozing till he inclined from the saddle. I came to him, propping him with my hand. When he felt my hand's touch, he straightened, saying: ""Who is this?"" I said: Abu Qatadah - in the second or third. He said: ""I know not but I have burdened you since the night."" I said: No, by my father and mother; but I see drowsiness and sleep have burdened you. Would you turn aside, alighting till your drowsiness or sleep leaves you? He said: ""I fear people will move on."" I said: No, by my father and mother. He said ... I turned aside from the road; I found a thicket of trees. I said: O Messenger of Allah, this is a thicket of trees I have found. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned aside, those next him turning aside with him from the road. They alighted, veiled by the thicket from the road. They awoke only with the sun risen upon us. We rose, fearful of our prayer. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Gently, gently."" Till the sun was high. Then he said: Whoever prayed not these two bowings before the dawn prayer, let him pray them. He said: Those who had not prayed them prayed them. Then he commanded; prayer was called. He prayed with us, greeting. When he greeted, he said: ""We, by Allah's praise, were not in aught of this world's affair diverting us from our prayer; but our spirits were in Allah's hand, sending them as He willed. Whoever this prayer reaches tomorrow morning, let him make up with it its like."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, thirst. He said: ""No thirst upon you. O Abu Qatadah, bring me my small cup upon the mount."" I brought him a cup between the two cups; he poured therein. Then he said: ""Give the people drink."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called, raising his voice: ""Nay, whoever his vessel reaches, let him drink what is therein."" We came in a circle; I gave man by man drink. Then I returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he poured in the cup; I gave who followed him drink. Then I returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he poured in the cup; I gave another circle drink. Till I gave seven draughts drink. I craned, looking if aught remained therein. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) poured it in the cup. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I find no great thirst; drink. He said: ""You are the people's cupbearer since today."" I drank. Then he poured; he drank. Then we mounted."
"23278","The account of Ya'la ibn Murrah al-Thaqafi on the Farewell Pilgrimage, and what Allah manifested of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proof therein. 54 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ya'la ibn Murrah, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I saw of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) three things none saw before me. I was with him on Makkah's road; he passed a woman with a son of hers, with madness the like I have not seen madness severe thereof. She said: O Messenger of Allah, this son of mine as you see. He said: ""If you wish, I shall supplicate for him."" He supplicated for him, then went on. He passed a camel stretching its neck, bellowing. He said: ""Bring me this one's master."" He was brought. He said: ""This says: I was born with you, you employed me, till when I aged you wished to slaughter me."" Then he went on, seeing two separate trees. He said: ""Go, command them to join."" They joined; he relieved his need. Then he said to me: ""Go, tell them to part."" I told them; they parted. When he turned, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed the boy playing with boys, his mother having prepared six rams, gifting him two rams, saying: Nothing of the madness has returned to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing but knows I am Allah's Messenger, save the disbelievers of jinn and men."
"23279","The account of Fatimah's marriage, may Allah be pleased with her, and what Allah manifested therein of the Messenger of Allah's, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, proof. 55 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-San'ani al-Dabari narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Yahya ibn al-'Ala' al-Bajali, from his uncle Shu'ayb ibn Khalid, from Hanzalah ibn Sabrah ibn al-Musayyib ibn Najyah, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: Fatimah would be mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); none mentioned her but she was refused him, till they despaired of her. Sa'd ibn Mu'adh met 'Ali, saying: By Allah, I see not the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) withholding her but for you. 'Ali said to him: Why deem you that? By Allah, I am not one of the two men: I am not a man of this world seeking what is with me - he knows I have neither yellow nor white - nor am I the disbeliever he softens with her for his religion, meaning he conciliates him with her. I am the first to accept Islam. Sa'd said: I adjure you, you shall surely relieve it from me; I have therein relief. He said: What shall I say? He said: Say: I have come suitor to Allah and His Messenger for Fatimah bint Muhammad. He said: 'Ali went, meeting the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he twisted a mat. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: ""As though you have a need, O 'Ali."" He said: Yes; I have come to you suitor to Allah and His Messenger for Fatimah daughter of Muhammad. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: ""Welcome"" - a faint word. Then he returned to Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, saying to him: I did what you commanded me; he added not but welcoming me with a faint word. Sa'd said to him: He has married you, by the One who sent him with the truth; there is no breaking now nor lying with him. I adjure you, you shall surely come to him tomorrow, saying to him: O Prophet of Allah, when shall you house me? 'Ali said: This is upon me harder than the first. Shall I not say: O Messenger of Allah, my need? He said: Say as I commanded you. 'Ali went, saying: O Messenger of Allah, when shall you house me? He said: ""Tonight, if Allah wills."" Then he called Bilal, saying: ""O Bilal, I have married my daughter to my cousin. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered; when the women saw him they went. I love that my ummah's practice be food at marriage. Go to the sheep, take a ewe and four or five measures, making me a bowl, that I may gather thereon the Emigrants and Helpers. When you finish thereof, inform me therewith."" He went, doing what he commanded him. Then he brought him a bowl, placing it before him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thrust in its top. Then he said: ""Admit people to me group by group, letting no group pass to another"" - meaning when a group finishes, it returns not a second. People began coming; whenever a company finished, another came, till people finished. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to what remained thereof, spitting therein, blessing, saying: ""O 'Ali, carry it to your mothers, telling them: Eat, feeding who visits you."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose, entering upon the women, saying: ""I have married my daughter to my cousin. You know her station with me. I commit her to him now; your daughter is yours."" The women rose, perfuming her of their perfume and jewelry, dressing her of their garments and jewelry. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered. When the women saw him they went, between them and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a curtain. Asma' bint 'Umays stayed behind. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: ""As you are, at your ease - who are you?"" She said: I am she who guards your daughter. The girl on her building night must have a woman near her; if a need befalls her, or she wishes aught, she confides that to her. He said: ""I ask my God to guard you from before you, behind you, your right, your left, from the accursed Satan."" Then he called for Fatimah; she came. When she saw 'Ali sitting beside the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she was shy, weeping. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pitied, lest her weeping be because 'Ali has no wealth. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""What makes you weep? I have not stinted you of myself; I have chosen for you the best of my family. By Him in whose hand is my soul, I have surely married you to one blest in this world; in the Hereafter he is of the righteous."" She softened thereof. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O Asma', bring me the basin, filling it water."" Asma' brought him the basin, filling it water. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rinsed therein, washing therein his feet and face. Then he called Fatimah, taking a handful of water, striking therewith her head, a handful between her breasts. Then he sprinkled his skin and her skin. Then he embraced her, saying: ""O Allah, she is of me, I of her. O Allah, as You removed from me impurity, purifying me, purify her."" Then he called for another basin. Then he called 'Ali, doing with him like what he did with her. Then he supplicated for him as he supplicated for her. Then he said: ""Rise to your house. Allah has joined you, blessed your going, set your mind aright."" Then he rose, closing upon them his door with his hand. Ibn 'Abbas said: Asma' bint 'Umays informed me that she watched the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he kept supplicating for them specially, associating none in his supplication for them, till he vanished in his chamber, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"23280","The Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) letter to 'Amr ibn Hazm on charity, when he sent him to Yemen. 56 - Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Dawud, al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, from his father, from his grandfather: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Yemen's people a letter wherein are obligations, practices, and blood-moneys. He sent it with 'Amr ibn Hazm; it was read to Yemen's people. This is its copy: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Muhammad, Allah's Messenger, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to Shurahbil ibn 'Abd Kulal, al-Harith ibn 'Abd Kulal, Nu'aym ibn 'Abd Kulal, qayl of Dhu Ru'ayn, Ma'afir, and Hamdan. As for what follows: Your messenger has returned; you have been given of the spoils Allah's fifth, and what Allah has written upon the believers of the tenth in landed property. What heaven waters, running or rain-fed, therein is the tenth when it reaches five awsuq. In every five pasturing camels a ewe, till they reach twenty-five. When they exceed twenty-four by one, therein is a she-camel in her second year. If a she-camel in her second year be not found, a male in his third year, till they reach thirty-five. When they exceed thirty-five by one, therein is a she-camel in her third year, till they reach forty-five. If they exceed forty-five by one, therein is a she-camel fit for covering, till they reach sixty. When they exceed sixty by one, therein is a she-camel in her fifth year, till they reach seventy-five. If they exceed seventy-five, therein are two she-camels in their third year, till they reach ninety. If they exceed by one, therein are two she-camels fit for covering, till they reach a hundred twenty. If they exceed a hundred twenty, in every forty a she-camel in her third year, in every fifty a she-camel fit for covering. In every thirty cattle a young ox in its second year or a young she in its second year. In every forty cattle a cow. In every forty pasturing sheep a ewe, till they reach a hundred twenty. If they exceed a hundred twenty by one, therein are two ewes, till they reach two hundred. If they exceed by one, therein are three ewes, till they reach three hundred. If they exceed, in every hundred sheep a ewe. In charity shall be taken neither aged, emaciated, defective, nor the flock's he-goat. Separated shall not be joined, joined shall not be separated, fearing charity. What is taken of the two partners, they shall settle between them equally. In every five ounces of silver five dirhams. What exceeds, in every forty dirhams a dirham. Therein is nothing below five ounces. In every forty dinars a dinar. Charity is not lawful to Muhammad nor his household. It is but zakat wherewith they purify themselves - for the believers' poor, in Allah's path. In a slave, farm, nor its workers is aught, when its charity is rendered of the tenth. There is in a Muslim slave nor his horse aught. In the letter was: The greatest of greater sins with Allah on the Resurrection Day are associating with Allah, killing the believing soul without right, fleeing in Allah's path on the battle day, disobeying parents, casting at the chaste, learning sorcery, devouring usury, devouring the orphan's wealth. 'Umrah is the lesser pilgrimage. None touches the Qur'an but pure. No divorce before possession, no manumission till you buy. Let none of you pray in a single garment with his side bare. Let none of you pray braiding his hair. In the letter was: Whoever kills a believer wantonly upon proof, it is retaliation, unless the slain's guardians consent. In the believing soul is the blood-money, a hundred camels. In the nose, when its base is severed, is the blood-money. In the tongue is the blood-money. In the lips is the blood-money. In the testicles is the blood-money. In the male organ is the blood-money. In the spine is the blood-money. In the eyes is the blood-money. In the one leg is half the blood-money. In the skull-exposing wound is a third of the blood-money. In the penetrating wound is a third of the blood-money. In the bone-removing wound are fifteen camels. In every finger of hand and foot's fingers are ten camels. In the tooth are five camels. In the bone-baring wound are five camels. A man is killed for a woman. Upon gold people are a thousand dinars."
"23281","The account of 'Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - on charities. The account of Abu Bakr - may Allah be pleased with him - on charities. 57 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, from al-Harith, from 'Ali - I deem from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - that he said: Bring a quarter of the tenth. Of every forty dirhams a dirham. Nothing is upon you till you reach or complete two hundred dirhams. When they are two hundred dirhams, therein are five dirhams. What exceeds, upon that reckoning. In sheep, in every forty sheep a ewe. If they be but thirty-nine, nothing is upon you therein till they reach a hundred twenty. When they exceed a hundred twenty by one, therein are two ewes to two hundred. When they exceed by a ewe, therein are three ewes to three hundred. Then in every hundred sheep a ewe. In cattle, in thirty a young ox, in forty a grown she. Nothing is upon workers. In camels, in five a ewe; in four nothing. In fifteen three ewes. In twenty four ewes. In twenty-five five of sheep. When they exceed by one, therein is a she-camel in her second year. If there be no she-camel in her second year, a male in his third year, to thirty-five. When they exceed by one, therein is a she-camel in her third year, to forty-five. When they exceed by one, therein is a she-camel fit for covering, to sixty. When they exceed by one, therein are two she-camels fit for covering, to a hundred twenty. If camels be more than that, in every fifty a she-camel. Separated shall not be parted, parted shall not be joined, fearing charity. Aged shall not be taken, defective, nor he-goat, unless the charity-taker wills. In plants, what rivers water or heaven waters is the tenth; what is watered, therein is half the tenth. Charity is in every year - I deem he said: once. In 'Asim's account: If there be in camels neither a she-camel in her second year nor a male in his third year, two ewes."
"23282","58 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab al-Jumahi narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated to us - my father narrated to me - Thumamah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Anas narrated to us, that Anas narrated to him, that Abu Bakr, when he was made caliph, wrote him this letter when he directed him to al-Bahrayn: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. This is the charity obligation the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made obligatory upon the Muslims, which his Messenger was commanded with. Whoever of the Muslims is asked it upon its face, let him give it. Whoever is asked above it, let him not give it. In every twenty-four of camels and below, sheep: in every five a ewe. When they reach twenty-five to thirty-five, therein is a female she-camel in her second year. If therein be no she-camel in her second year, a male in his third year, with nothing with it. When they reach thirty-six to forty-five, therein is a she-camel in her third year. When they reach forty-six to sixty, therein is a she-camel fit for covering. When they reach sixty-one to seventy-five, therein is a she-camel in her fifth year. When they reach seventy-six to ninety, therein are two she-camels in their third year. When they reach ninety-one to a hundred twenty, therein are two she-camels fit for covering. When they exceed a hundred twenty, in every forty a she-camel in her third year, in every fifty a she-camel. If camels' ages differ in charity obligations: whoever's camels reach the fifth-year-she's charity, finding not the fifth-year-she, having a she fit for covering - it shall be accepted of him, making with it two ewes if easy, or twenty dirhams. Whoever's reaches the she-fit-for-covering's charity, having not the she fit for covering, having a fifth-year-she - it shall be accepted of him, the charity-taker giving him twenty dirhams or two ewes. If his charity reaches a she fit for covering, having but a she in her third year - it shall be accepted of him, a she in her third year, giving with it two ewes or twenty dirhams. Whoever's charity reaches a she in her third year, having it not, having a she fit for covering - it shall be accepted of him, the she fit for covering, the charity-taker giving him twenty dirhams or two ewes. Whoever's charity reaches a she in her third year, having it not, having a she in her second year - it shall be accepted of him, a she in her second year, giving with it twenty dirhams or two ewes. Whoever's charity reaches a she in her second year, having not a she in her second year, having a she in her third year - it shall be accepted of him, a she in her third year, the charity-taker giving him twenty dirhams or two ewes. If there be no she in her third year upon its face, having a male in his third year - it shall be accepted of him, with nothing with it. Whoever has but four camels, therein is nothing, unless its lord wills. When they reach five camels, therein is a ewe. Sheep's charity is in its pasturing: if forty to a hundred twenty, therein is a ewe. If they exceed a hundred twenty to reach two hundred, therein are two ewes. If they exceed two hundred to three hundred, therein are three ewes. If they exceed three hundred, in every hundred a ewe. In charity shall be brought out neither aged, defective, nor he-goat, unless the charity-taker wills. Separated shall not be joined, joined shall not be parted. What is of two partners, they shall settle between them equally. If a man's pasturing be lacking forty by one, therein is no charity, unless its lord wills. In silver is a quarter of the tenth. If there be no wealth but a hundred ninety dirhams, therein is no charity, unless its lord wills."
"23283","The Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) address on the Conquest day. 59 - Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah al-Jarjarani narrated to us, Sulayman ibn al-Hakam ibn 'Awanah narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn al-Walid al-Hamdani, from Sinan ibn al-Harith ibn Musarrif, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Mujahid, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Khuza'ah was ally to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Banu Bakr, a clan of Banu Kinanah, were ally to Abu Sufyan. Between them was a truce in Hudaybiyah's days. Banu Bakr raided Khuza'ah in that period. They sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), seeking his aid. He set out aiding them in Ramadan, fasting till he reached Qudayd, then breaking fast, saying: ""Let people fast in travel, breaking fast. Whoever fasts, it suffices him; whoever breaks fast, make-up is due upon him."" Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, opened Makkah to them. When he entered, he leaned his back to the Ka'bah. Then he improvised a word. Then he said: ""Hold weapons, save Khuza'ah from Banu Bakr."" Till a man came, saying: O Messenger of Allah, a man has been killed at al-Muzdalifah. He said: ""This sanctuary is sacred by Allah's sanctity. It was not lawful to who was before me, nor lawful to who is after me. It was lawful to me but an hour of the day. It is not lawful for a Muslim to brandish therein a weapon. Its herbage shall not be cut, its trees shall not be lopped, its game shall not be startled."" A man said: O Messenger of Allah, save idhkhir; it is for our houses and graves. He said: Save idhkhir. The most insolent of people against Allah is who kills in Allah's sanctuary, kills other than his killer, or kills for pre-Islamic vengeance. A man rose, saying: O Messenger of Allah, I fell upon so-and-so's slave-girl; she bore me. Command my child be returned to me. He said: ""He is not your child; it is not allowed in Islam. The claimed against is worthier of oath, unless proof stands. The child is to the bed's master; to the adulterer the stone."" A man said: And what is the stone? He said: ""The stone. Whoever commits adultery with a woman he owns not, or a woman of another people, and she bears - he is not his child; he inherits not, is not inherited. The believers are a hand against others; their bloods are equal; their least covenants for them, their farthest returns for them. No Muslim shall be killed for a disbeliever, no covenanter in his covenant. People of two religions inherit not one another. A woman shall not be married upon her paternal aunt, nor upon her maternal aunt. She shall not travel above three with other than a mahram. Pray not after dawn till the sun rises; pray not after 'Asr till the sun sets."""
"23284","The Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) address in the Farewell Pilgrimage. 60 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna al-'Anbari narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Isma'il ibn 'Ulayyah narrated to us, Ayyub informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abi Bakrah, from Abu Bakrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed in his pilgrimage, saying: Time has turned as its shape the day Allah created the heavens and earth. The year is twelve months. Of them four sacred: three successive - Dhu al-Qa'dah, Dhu al-Hijjah, al-Muharram - and Rajab of Mudar between Jumada and Sha'ban. Then he said: ""What day is this?"" We said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He fell silent till we thought he would name it by other than its name. He said: ""Is it not the Sacrifice day?"" We said: Yes indeed. He said: ""What month is this?"" We said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He fell silent till we thought he would name it by other than its name. He said: ""Is it not Dhu al-Hijjah?"" We said: Yes indeed. He said: ""What land is this?"" We said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He fell silent till we thought he would name it by other than its name. He said: ""Is it not the Town?"" We said: Yes indeed. He said: ""Your blood, wealth, and honors are upon you sacred, as your this day's sanctity, in your this month, in your this land. You shall meet your Lord; He shall ask you of your deeds. Nay, return not after me astray, some striking others' necks. Nay, have I conveyed? Let the witness of you convey to the absent; the conveyed may be more comprehending than some who heard."""
"23285","The account of the Ansari and the Thaqafi who came to the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him of some deeds; he informed them of what they came with before they asked him. 61 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us; and Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, they said: 'Attaf ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Rafi' narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Khayf mosque. A man of the Ansar and a man of Thaqif came to him, greeting him, supplicating for him a good supplication. Then they said: We have come to you, O Messenger of Allah, asking you. He said: If you wish, I shall inform you of what you have come asking me of, doing so; if you wish, I shall be silent and you ask me, doing so. They said: Inform us, O Messenger of Allah; we shall increase in faith, or increase in certainty. The Ansari said to the Thaqafi: Ask. He said: Rather you, ask him; I know for you a right, so ask him. The Ansari said: Inform me, O Messenger of Allah. He said: You have come asking me of your going out of your house heading the Sacred House and what is yours therein; of your circumambulating the House and what is yours therein; of your two bowings after the circumambulation and what is yours therein; of your going between al-Safa and al-Marwah and what is yours therein; of your standing the evening of 'Arafah at 'Arafah and what is yours therein; of your stoning the pillars and what is yours therein; of your shaving your head and what is yours therein; of your circumambulating after that and what is yours therein; of your sacrificing and what is yours therein; and of the pouring forth. He said: Yes, by the One who sent you with the truth; with this very thing I have come asking you. He said: When you go out of your house heading the Sacred House, your she-camel places not a foot nor lifts it but Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, writes for you thereby a good deed, effaces from you a sin, raises for you thereby a degree. As for your circumambulating the House, you place not a foot nor lift it but Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, writes for you thereby a good deed, effaces from you thereby a sin. As for two bowings after the circumambulation, they are to you as freeing a neck of Banu Isma'il. As for your going between al-Safa and al-Marwah, as freeing seventy necks. As for your standing the evening of 'Arafah, Allah descends to the lowest heaven, boasting of you to the angels, saying: These are My servants; they have come to Me disheveled, dusty, from every deep pass, hoping My mercy and forgiveness. Were your sins as the sands of 'Alij in number, or as the sea's foam, I would forgive them. Pour forth, servants, forgiven you and who you interceded for. As for your stoning the pillars, for every pebble thereof you stoned, a great of the destroying, necessitating greater sins. As for your sacrificing, stored for you with your Lord. As for your shaving your head, for every hair you shaved, a good deed; He effaces from you thereby a sin. He said: O Messenger of Allah, if sins be fewer than that? He said: Then it shall be stored for you in your good deeds. As for your circumambulating the House after that, you circumambulate, sinless. An angel shall come till he places his hand between your shoulders, then says: Work for what you face; forgiven you what has passed. The Thaqafi said: Inform me, O Messenger of Allah. He said: You have come asking me of prayer. When you wash your face, sins scatter from your eyes' lashes. When you wash your hands, likewise sins scatter from your hands' nails. When you wipe your head, likewise sins scatter from your head. When you wash your feet, sins scatter from your feet's nails. When you stand to prayer, recite of the Qur'an what is easy. Then when you bow, settle your hands upon your knees, parting your fingers till you are tranquil bowing. When you prostrate, settle your face of all prostration till you are tranquil prostrating. Gnaw not pecking. Pray of the night's first and last. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what if I pray it all? He said: Then you are - then you are."
"23286","The account of Mazin ibn al-Ghadubah. 62 - Musa ibn Jumhour al-Tinnisi narrated to us, 'Ali ibn Harb al-Mawsili narrated to us, Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib al-Kalbi narrated to us, from his father, from 'Abd Allah al-'Umani, from Mazin ibn al-Ghadubah, he said: I used to tend an idol called Bahir at Sama'il, a village in 'Uman. We sacrificed one day with it an 'atirah - the slaughter. I heard a voice from the idol saying verses. He said: I feared thereof, saying: This is a wonder. Then I sacrificed after days an 'atirah; I heard a voice from the idol saying verses. I said: This is a wonder; it is good willed for us. While we were thus, a man of the Hijaz arrived. We said: What news behind you? He said: A man has appeared called Ahmad, saying to whoever comes to him: 'Answer Allah's Caller.' I said: This is the tidings of what I heard. I went to the idol, breaking it fragments. I mounted my mount, coming to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He explained to me Islam; I accepted Islam. I said verses. Bahir fragments - Bahir, meaning 'Amr ibn al-Salt and his brothers, Banu Khutamah. Mazin said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a man fond of merriment, wine-drinking, and the wanton - Ibn al-Kalbi said: wanton: the adulterous of women. Years pressed upon us, taking terrors, emaciating offspring and dependents. I have no child. Supplicate Allah to remove from me what I find, bring us modesty, grant me a child. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, exchange him for merriment reciting the Qur'an, for the unlawful the lawful, for adultery chastity of the private part, for wine quenching wherein is no sin. Bring him modesty, grant him a child. Mazin said: Allah removed from me what I found, brought us modesty. I learned half the Qur'an. 'Uman prospered. I performed pilgrimages. Allah granted me Hayyan ibn Mazin. I began saying verses. The wastes, fond, adultery chastity, jihad. When I came to my people, they blamed and reviled me, ordering a poet of theirs; he lampooned me. I said: If I answer him, lampooning is but for myself. I withdrew them to the sea's shore, saying verses. There came to me of them a great company, saying: O cousin, we faulted upon you a matter, disliking it for you. If you refuse, your affair and religion - return, manage our affairs. I was the manager of their affairs, returning with them. Then Allah guided them after to Islam."
